{"title":"Exploring the Unseen — PhD defense","prelude":"From motion to measurement","preludeChapter":false,"style":"seminar","accent":"clay","backdrop":"black","font":"serif","plate":"scrim","transition":"fade","bibliography":"decks/references.bib","speakerNotes":{"manifest":"decks/phd-defense.speaker-cues.json"},"progress":{"style":"swell","side":"right","rows":1},"slides":[{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":1050,"layout":[". . . .",". copy art .",". . . ."],"cols":"5vw 1.10fr 0.90fr 5vw","rows":"7vh 1fr 7vh","cellAlign":{"copy":"center","art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"PhD defence · 10 September 2026","at":"copy"},{"type":"title","text":"Exploring the Unseen","at":"copy"},{"type":"subtitle","text":"An MRI-Based Examination of Blood–Brain Barrier Integrity,\nBrain Microstructure, and Metabolic Shifts\nin Post-Concussion Syndrome","at":"copy"},{"type":"body","text":"Edis Devin Tireli  ·  University of Copenhagen  ·  Rigshospitalet","at":"copy"},{"type":"scene","scene":"brain3d","at":"art","args":{"render":"wire","label":false,"yaw":104,"tilt":12,"spin":0.02,"scale":1.15,"aspect":1.1,"maxHeight":0.78,"fps":18,"dpr":1}},{"id":"thesis-download-qr","type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/thesis-download/thesis-download-qr.svg","caption":"Follow live · Download thesis","alt":"Scan to follow the presentation live or download the thesis.","at":"copy","fit":"contain","overlay":{"left":"0","bottom":"calc(-34 * var(--u))","width":"min(220px, 13.75vw)","height":"auto","z":3}}],"seconds":10,"notes":"Title slide. Let the native wire-and-dot brain rotate quietly while the room settles. State the thesis title, your name and that the defence connects MRI signal formation to blood–brain barrier exchange and cerebral oxygen metabolism after concussion.","order":0},{"id":"the-work-in-one-view","order":0.5,"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["overview"],"rows":"1fr","blocks":[{"type":"defense-overview","at":"overview","bullets":[{"text":"Three years studying persistent symptoms after concussion","highlight":"persistent symptoms"},{"text":"Despite normal CT and routine MRI","highlight":"normal CT and routine MRI"},{"text":"Controlled perturbations: magnetic and oxygen","highlights":["Controlled perturbations","magnetic","oxygen"]}]}],"notes":"Three short prompts on the presentation backdrop. The first fades in on entry; Next reveals the second, then the third. The full narration continues unchanged. Next after the third prompt continues to Prelude.","seconds":30,"stepNotes":["Three years studying persistent symptoms after concussion","Despite normal CT and routine MRI","Controlled perturbations: magnetic and oxygen"]},{"id":"prelude","template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"00","heading":"Prelude","eyebrow":"from impact to the evidence landscape","seconds":6,"notes":"A deliberately unnumbered-in-the-talk prelude marker. The runner and evidence landscape establish the clinical problem and current evidence before Study I begins as the first numbered study chapter.","order":1},{"id":"from-impact-to-mr-signal","omit":true,"template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"01","heading":"From impact to MR signal","eyebrow":"motion · nuclei · encoding · measurement","seconds":7,"notes":"Chapter transition.","order":2},{"id":"acute-to-persistent-symptoms","order":2,"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"concussionRunner","at":"figure","args":{"revealSteps":3,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1}}],"seconds":35,"notes":"The supplied smoke-runner performance starts automatically as this slide arrives. Click once: motion stops at a deterministic frame and the cranium resolves in clay red, with no symptom labels yet. Click again: eight acute symptom labels trickle in close to the head and body. Headache, dizziness or imbalance, nausea or vomiting, confusion or feeling dazed, memory disturbance, vision problems, and light or noise sensitivity can occur acutely. Brief loss of consciousness is labelled as possible rather than defining: concussion can occur without it. Click a third time: the runner moves again in slow–fast–slow motion while an alternating sun–moon clock crosses a shallow arc behind the body from week 1 to week 112, ending with the person at rest. The final state is a literature-derived clinical phenotype rather than a reproduction of every RPQ item. Domains present in both phases retain orange: headache; dizziness/imbalance; visual/ocular-motor symptoms; light/noise sensitivity; and memory difficulty. Persistent domains not shown in the acute state enter in purple: neck pain/cervicogenic symptoms; mental fatigue; poor concentration; slowed thinking; sleep disturbance; irritability/mood change; depression/anxiety; and exercise intolerance. The acute-only labels nausea/vomiting, confusion/feeling dazed, and possible brief loss of consciousness dissolve. These symptoms and domains are nonspecific, may overlap, and are not a diagnostic checklist; the slide does not imply that every person develops every symptom or that the labels transform one-to-one. Advance into the evidence landscape before introducing the scanner. The red focus and dissipation are conceptual, not a quantitative reconstruction of strain or injury severity.\n\n[Sources]\n- Tireli AV. PhD thesis. 2026. Introduction §1.1.\n- Patricios JS et al. Consensus statement on concussion in sport: Amsterdam 2022. Br J Sports Med. 2023;57:695–711. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2023-106898.\n- Rytter HM et al. Nonpharmacological Treatment of Persistent Postconcussion Symptoms in Adults. JAMA Netw Open. 2021;4:e2132221. doi:10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2021.32221.\n- Living Concussion Guidelines. Diagnosis/Assessment of Prolonged Symptoms. Updated April 2023."},{"id":"concussion-evidence","omit":true,"order":2.1,"template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"01","heading":"What do we actually know after concussion?","seconds":8,"notes":"Chapter transition."},{"id":"concussion-evidence-landscape","order":2.2,"subchapter":"The literature","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I","Study II","Study III"],"alt":"Interactive literature network with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links","states":[{"focus":"all","overview":true,"title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"subtitle":false},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["animals"],"title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"audienceStory":{"title":"Animal studies / models","points":["Animal studies test **cellular and vascular mechanisms**.","Interpretation depends on **species, injury model and time since injury**."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"What mechanisms are possible?","lines":["Animal studies test **cellular and vascular mechanisms**.","Interpretation depends on **species, injury model and time since injury**."]},"subtitle":false,"groupLabel":"Animal studies / models"},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["humans"],"title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"audienceStory":{"title":"Human studies","points":["**Human literature** across ages, populations and time points."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"Human studies","lines":["**Human literature** across ages, populations and time points."]},"subtitle":false,"groupLabel":"Human studies","cohortColors":{"humans":"#f2bd76"},"countScope":"Publications in this map","showPublicationTypes":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["militaryBlastHuman"],"title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"audienceStory":{"title":"Military, veterans & blast","points":["Injury mechanisms and exposure may differ from civilian concussion."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"Military, veterans & blast","lines":["Injury mechanisms and exposure may differ from civilian concussion."]},"subtitle":false,"groupLabel":"Military, veterans & blast","withinCohorts":["humans"],"cohortColors":{"militaryBlastHuman":"#f2bd76"},"countScope":"Publications in this map","say":"Military and veteran studies, together with the human literature on blast exposure. Injury mechanisms and exposure may differ from civilian concussion."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["sports"],"title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"audienceStory":{"title":"Athletes / repeated head impacts","points":["Injury mechanisms and recovery steps may differ between them and civilians."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"Athletes / repeated head impacts","lines":["Injury mechanisms and recovery steps may differ between them and civilians."]},"subtitle":false,"groupLabel":"Athletes / repeated head impacts","withinCohorts":["humans"]},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["children"],"title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"audienceStory":{"title":"Children and adolescents","points":["Brain development and recovery may differ."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"Children and adolescents","lines":["Brain development and recovery may differ."]},"subtitle":false,"groupLabel":"Children and adolescents","withinCohorts":["humans"]},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["humans"],"title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"audienceStory":{"title":"Human studies","points":["**Human literature** across ages, populations and time points."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"Human studies","lines":["**Human literature** across ages, populations and time points."]},"subtitle":false,"groupLabel":"Human studies","cohortColors":{"humans":"#f2bd76"},"countScope":"Publications in this map","showPublicationTypes":true,"humanOverviewReturn":true,"say":"Return to the full human literature before narrowing to adult civilian PCS/PPCS."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["adultCivilianPpcs"],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"groupLabel":"Adult clinical PCS/PPCS","audienceStory":{"title":"Adult clinical PCS/PPCS","points":["Persistent symptoms or a **clinical PCS diagnosis**.","Predominantly **civilian clinical and community cohorts**."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"Adult clinical PCS/PPCS","lines":["Persistent symptoms or a **clinical PCS diagnosis**.","Predominantly **civilian clinical and community cohorts**."]},"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":["humans"],"countScope":"Publications in this map","say":"Adults with persistent symptoms or a clinical PCS diagnosis, primarily recruited in civilian clinical and community settings. Mixed recruitment is described in the publication details."}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"overviewTour":true,"paperSequence":false,"populationTour":true,"speakingPrompts":true,"plainHeadings":true,"showMapLabels":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","populationFlow":"nested-human-pcs-v1"}],"seconds":65,"notes":"Open on the entire literature field. Next highlight animal studies/models, then all humans. Within humans, show military/veteran and blast literature before athletes, then children and adolescents. Return to all humans, then narrow to the 61 adult clinical PCS/PPCS publications. Oxygen-metabolism/flow and DCE-MRI branches follow their study results. Counts are publications in this map.","stepNotes":[]},{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":1100,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"id":"concussion-circuit-mechanics","order":101,"subchapter":"Literature","blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I"],"timelineVisitedBefore":[],"paperSequence":true,"returnAfterLast":false,"states":[{"focus":"mechanics+metabolism","focusThemes":["mechanics","metabolism"],"nodes":["Giza2014","Enger2015"],"side":"center","timelineLabel":"review framework + induced CSD experiment","figureLayout":"review-wave-pair","videoPlayback":"pause","figures":[{"role":"mouse-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/c57bl6-mouse-commons.jpg","alt":"Illustrative C57BL/6-background mouse, not an Enger study animal","label":"C57BL/6N MOUSE · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"review","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/giza-fig1.jpg","alt":"Original Giza and Hovda qualitative review timeline of ionic and metabolic changes after concussion","label":"Giza & Hovda · neurometabolic cascade"},{"role":"neuronal","video":"decks/assets/defense/literature/enger-2015-neuronal-calcium.mp4","alt":"Neuronal GCaMP6f fluorescence moving as a wave across mouse cortex during experimentally induced cortical spreading depression","label":"Enger · calcium inside neurons"},{"role":"glutamate","video":"decks/assets/defense/literature/enger-2015-glutamate.mp4","alt":"iGluSnFR fluorescence showing an extracellular glutamate wave during experimentally induced cortical spreading depression","label":"Enger · glutamate outside cells"}],"tag":"REVIEW + LIVE MOUSE CORTEX · CALCIUM AND GLUTAMATE WAVES","text":"The review sketches the neurometabolic cascade. Enger et al. make two of its moving signals visible.","citations":[{"text":"Giza CC, Hovda DA. Neurosurgery. 2014;75:S24–S33.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1227/NEU.0000000000000505"},{"text":"Enger R et al. Cereb Cortex. 2015;25:4469–4476.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/cercor/bhv054"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"Concussion can trigger an intense, acute energy crisis","points":["Neurons maintain **ion gradients** across their membranes: potassium is concentrated inside, sodium outside. Injury can disrupt this electrical balance and trigger **spreading depolarisation**.","Glutamate release excites neighbouring neurons. Restoring the gradients increases **ATP demand**, while blood flow and energy supply can be constrained."],"bridge":"","position":"below","definitions":["Enger: separate KCl-induced mouse recordings of neuronal calcium and extracellular glutamate, at 10× playback."]},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-mechanics/0","sequenceKey":"giza-enger","subtitle":false},{"focus":"mechanics+metabolism","focusThemes":["mechanics","metabolism"],"nodes":["Giza2014","Enger2015"],"side":"center","timelineLabel":"review framework + induced CSD experiment","figureLayout":"review-wave-pair","videoPlayback":"play","figures":[{"role":"mouse-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/c57bl6-mouse-commons.jpg","alt":"Illustrative C57BL/6-background mouse, not an Enger study animal","label":"C57BL/6N MOUSE · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"review","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/giza-fig1.jpg","alt":"Original Giza and Hovda qualitative review timeline of ionic and metabolic changes after concussion","label":"Giza & Hovda · neurometabolic cascade"},{"role":"neuronal","video":"decks/assets/defense/literature/enger-2015-neuronal-calcium.mp4","alt":"Neuronal GCaMP6f fluorescence moving as a wave across mouse cortex during experimentally induced cortical spreading depression","label":"Enger · calcium inside neurons"},{"role":"glutamate","video":"decks/assets/defense/literature/enger-2015-glutamate.mp4","alt":"iGluSnFR fluorescence showing an extracellular glutamate wave during experimentally induced cortical spreading depression","label":"Enger · glutamate outside cells"}],"tag":"REVIEW + LIVE MOUSE CORTEX · CALCIUM AND GLUTAMATE WAVES","text":"The review sketches the neurometabolic cascade. Enger et al. make two of its moving signals visible.","citations":[{"text":"Giza CC, Hovda DA. Neurosurgery. 2014;75:S24–S33.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1227/NEU.0000000000000505"},{"text":"Enger R et al. Cereb Cortex. 2015;25:4469–4476.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/cercor/bhv054"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"Concussion can trigger an intense, acute energy crisis","points":["Neurons maintain **ion gradients** across their membranes: potassium is concentrated inside, sodium outside. Injury can disrupt this electrical balance and trigger **spreading depolarisation**.","Glutamate release excites neighbouring neurons. Restoring the gradients increases **ATP demand**, while blood flow and energy supply can be constrained."],"bridge":"","position":"below","definitions":["Enger: separate KCl-induced mouse recordings of neuronal calcium and extracellular glutamate, at 10× playback."]},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-mechanics/1","sequenceKey":"giza-enger","subtitle":false},{"focus":"mechanics","node":"Pacheco2019","side":"center","subtitle":false,"figureLayout":"pacheco-full","figures":[{"role":"mouse-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/c57bl6-mouse-commons.jpg","alt":"Illustrative C57BL/6 mouse, not a study animal","label":"C57BL/6 MOUSE · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"electrophysiology","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/pacheco-electrophysiology-crop.png","alt":"Pacheco Figure 4 panels G and H showing electrophysiology after closed-skull mouse impact","label":"Pacheco · suppressed electrical activity"},{"role":"flow","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/pacheco-flow-crop.png","alt":"Pacheco Figure 5 panels A through C showing cerebral blood-flow recovery after closed-skull mouse impact","label":"Pacheco · blood-flow recovery over ~90 min"}],"tag":"MOUSE mTBI · ELECTRICAL ACTIVITY + CEREBRAL BLOOD FLOW · FIRST 90 MIN","text":"A stronger impact produces a spreading depolarization, briefly silences high-frequency activity, and reduces blood flow.","citation":{"text":"Pacheco JM et al. eNeuro. 2019;6:ENEURO.0070-19.2019.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1523/ENEURO.0070-19.2019"},"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"Reduced blood flow outlasts the initial suppression of cortical activity","points":["A closed-skull impact in **mice** can trigger spreading depolarisation followed by suppression of ongoing **cortical electrical activity**.","Electrical activity begins recovering after minutes. **Blood flow remains reduced longer**, recovering over approximately **90 minutes**."],"bridge":"","position":"below"},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-mechanics/3","cueSourceAliases":["concussion-circuit-mechanics/2"]}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"plainHeadings":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","alt":"Interactive literature network of 19,542 papers with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links"}],"seconds":112,"notes":"Click 0\n- Now consider **mechanics and neuronal function and energy metabolism**.\nPoint to: The circuit, with no paper figures or bullet text on screen\nGiza Figure 1 is a qualitative review schematic of ions, glucose metabolism and cerebral blood flow. It does not directly measure ATP or oxygen consumption. ATP is the energy source used by pumps that restore ion gradients. Glutamate is a major excitatory neurotransmitter. Calcium normally participates in signalling, including transmitter release; excessive influx can add to cellular and mitochondrial stress. This is the acute response, not proof that the same disturbance persists for years in PPCS. Giza & Hovda: Energy crisis section and Figure 1.\n\nClick 1\n- **Giza & Hovda · Enger et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nGiza Figure 1 is a qualitative review schematic of ions, glucose metabolism and cerebral blood flow. It does not directly measure ATP or oxygen consumption. ATP is the energy source used by pumps that restore ion gradients. Glutamate is a major excitatory neurotransmitter. Calcium normally participates in signalling, including transmitter release; excessive influx can add to cellular and mitochondrial stress. This is the acute response, not proof that the same disturbance persists for years in PPCS. Giza & Hovda: Energy crisis section and Figure 1.\n\nClick 2\n- **Concussion can trigger an intense, acute energy crisis**\n- Neurons maintain **ion gradients** across their membranes: potassium is concentrated inside, sodium outside. Injury can disrupt this electrical balance and trigger **spreading depolarisation**.\n- Glutamate release excites neighbouring neurons. Restoring the gradients increases **ATP demand**, while blood flow and energy supply can be constrained.\nPoint to: Giza Figure 1: ion disturbance, then glucose demand and blood flow\nGiza Figure 1 is a qualitative review schematic of ions, glucose metabolism and cerebral blood flow. It does not directly measure ATP or oxygen consumption. ATP is the energy source used by pumps that restore ion gradients. Glutamate is a major excitatory neurotransmitter. Calcium normally participates in signalling, including transmitter release; excessive influx can add to cellular and mitochondrial stress. This is the acute response, not proof that the same disturbance persists for years in PPCS. Giza & Hovda: Energy crisis section and Figure 1.\n\nClick 3\n- **Concussion can trigger an intense, acute energy crisis**\n- Neurons maintain **ion gradients** across their membranes: potassium is concentrated inside, sodium outside. Injury can disrupt this electrical balance and trigger **spreading depolarisation**.\n- Glutamate release excites neighbouring neurons. Restoring the gradients increases **ATP demand**, while blood flow and energy supply can be constrained.\nPoint to: The moving green fronts in Enger Movies 1 and 4\nGiza Figure 1 is a qualitative review schematic of ions, glucose metabolism and cerebral blood flow. It does not directly measure ATP or oxygen consumption. ATP is the energy source used by pumps that restore ion gradients. Glutamate is a major excitatory neurotransmitter. Calcium normally participates in signalling, including transmitter release; excessive influx can add to cellular and mitochondrial stress. This is the acute response, not proof that the same disturbance persists for years in PPCS. Giza & Hovda: Energy crisis section and Figure 1. Enger used GCaMP6f for intracellular neuronal calcium and iGluSnFR for extracellular glutamate. Texas Red labels the vasculature in Movie 1. This is chemically induced spreading depolarisation, not an impact experiment. The movie alone does not establish cell death or the mechanism behind dark circles and darkening.\n\nClick 4\n- Return to **mechanics and neuronal function**.\nPoint to: The circuit, with no paper figures or bullet text on screen\nPacheco Figure 4G–H and Figure 5. High-frequency activity means the 0.5–45 Hz recording band, distinguished from the slow depolarisation potential. The mean suppression lasted 6.88 minutes. Recovery was defined by the first spike in the power trace, not complete normalisation of every aspect of cortical function. The different recovery times do not prove that reduced flow alone causes the electrical depression.\n\nClick 5\n- **Pacheco et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nPacheco Figure 4G–H and Figure 5. High-frequency activity means the 0.5–45 Hz recording band, distinguished from the slow depolarisation potential. The mean suppression lasted 6.88 minutes. Recovery was defined by the first spike in the power trace, not complete normalisation of every aspect of cortical function. The different recovery times do not prove that reduced flow alone causes the electrical depression.\n\nClick 6\n- **Reduced blood flow outlasts the initial suppression of cortical activity**\n- A closed-skull impact in **mice** can trigger spreading depolarisation followed by suppression of ongoing **cortical electrical activity**.\n- Electrical activity begins recovering after minutes. **Blood flow remains reduced longer**, recovering over approximately **90 minutes**.\nPoint to: Figure 5: prolonged reduction and recovery of blood flow\nPacheco Figure 4G–H and Figure 5. High-frequency activity means the 0.5–45 Hz recording band, distinguished from the slow depolarisation potential. The mean suppression lasted 6.88 minutes. Recovery was defined by the first spike in the power trace, not complete normalisation of every aspect of cortical function. The different recovery times do not prove that reduced flow alone causes the electrical depression.","backup":true},{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"id":"concussion-circuit-immune","order":102,"subchapter":"Literature","blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I"],"timelineVisitedBefore":["Giza2014","Enger2015","Pacheco2019"],"paperSequence":true,"returnAfterLast":false,"states":[{"focus":"mechanics","focusThemes":["mechanics"],"node":"Taotao2021","side":"center","subtitle":false,"figureLayout":"species-data","figures":[{"role":"species","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/einstein-human-cc0.jpg","alt":"Albert Einstein portrait used only as a neutral visual shorthand for the human finite-element model; not a study participant","label":"HUMAN","position":"50% 18%","fit":"cover"},{"role":"species","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/wu-pig-user-reference.png","alt":"Domestic pig representing the pig finite-element model","label":"PIG","position":"50% 55%","fit":"cover"},{"role":"species","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/rhesus-macaque-varun.jpg","alt":"Rhesus macaque representing one nonhuman-primate model","label":"RHESUS MACAQUE","position":"50% 50%","fit":"cover","zoom":2,"zoomOrigin":"50% 25%"},{"role":"species","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/wu-baboon-commons.jpg","alt":"Olive baboon representing the baboon finite-element model","label":"BABOON","position":"50% 20%","fit":"cover"},{"role":"data","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/wu-2021-fig2.jpg","alt":"Original Wu Figure 2 showing harmonised finite-element brain models for human, pig, rhesus macaque, and baboon","label":"SPECIES-SPECIFIC FINITE-ELEMENT MODELS"},{"role":"data","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/wu-2021-fig4-axonal-risk.png","alt":"Wu Figure 4, complete upper-right panel: combined-species injury-risk functions versus maximum axonal strain MAS95; blue mild TBI, red severe TBI, with original observations and confidence intervals","label":"AXONAL STRAIN → INJURY RISK · FIG. 4"}],"tag":"HUMAN + PIG + RHESUS MACAQUE + BABOON · FINITE-ELEMENT IMPACT MODELS","text":"Across four species, maximum axonal strain tracked injury severity most consistently.","citation":{"text":"Wu T et al. J Neurotrauma. 2021;38:1879–1888.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1089/neu.2020.7445"},"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"publicationYear":"2021","audienceStory":{"title":"Axonal strain predicts injury severity across species","points":["**Axons** carry signals away from the neuron’s cell body. Axonal strain measures how much these fibres **stretch**.","In the **combined human, pig, rhesus macaque and baboon dataset**, maximum axonal strain was the strongest tested injury-severity predictor."],"bridge":"","position":"below"},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-immune/2"}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"plainHeadings":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","alt":"Interactive literature network of 19,542 papers with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links"}],"seconds":92,"notes":"Click 0\n- Now consider **mechanics and neuronal function**.\nPoint to: The circuit, with no paper figures or bullet text on screen\nMAS95 is the 95th percentile of maximum axonal strain. In the displayed Figure 4 panel, blue is the mild-TBI risk curve and red is the severe-TBI risk curve. Dashed curves are confidence intervals. The ranking of predictors comes from the statistical analysis, not the appearance of the sigmoid. The best metric differed in the separate species analyses. Say strain or stretching, not shear stress. Diagnostic differences and model-validation limits constrain cross-species generalisation.\n\nClick 1\n- **Wu et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nMAS95 is the 95th percentile of maximum axonal strain. In the displayed Figure 4 panel, blue is the mild-TBI risk curve and red is the severe-TBI risk curve. Dashed curves are confidence intervals. The ranking of predictors comes from the statistical analysis, not the appearance of the sigmoid. The best metric differed in the separate species analyses. Say strain or stretching, not shear stress. Diagnostic differences and model-validation limits constrain cross-species generalisation.\n\nClick 2\n- **Axonal strain predicts injury severity across species**\n- **Axons** carry signals away from the neuron’s cell body. Axonal strain measures how much these fibres **stretch**.\n- In the **combined human, pig, rhesus macaque and baboon dataset**, maximum axonal strain was the strongest tested injury-severity predictor.\nPoint to: Figure 2 species models, then Figure 4 axonal-strain injury-risk curves\nMAS95 is the 95th percentile of maximum axonal strain. The pooled analysis supports maximum axonal strain across injury severities; the best within-species metric varied. The lower injury-risk curve combines mild and severe injury, while the higher threshold is severe injury. Say strain (stretch), not shear stress. Model-derived retrospective thresholds are not a universal human safety limit.","backup":true},{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"id":"concussion-circuit-metabolism","order":103,"subchapter":"Literature","blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I"],"timelineVisitedBefore":["Giza2014","Enger2015","Pacheco2019","Taotao2021"],"paperSequence":true,"returnAfterLast":false,"states":[{"focus":"immune+nvu","focusThemes":["immune","nvu"],"node":"Nguyen2025","sequenceKey":"nguyen-repair","side":"center","figureLayout":"source-single","videoPlayback":"pause","timelineLabel":"minutes after repeated impact · vessel wrapping","figures":[{"role":"mouse-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/c57bl6-mouse-commons.jpg","alt":"Illustrative C57BL/6-background mouse, not a study animal","label":"C57BL/6-BACKGROUND MOUSE · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"movie","video":"decks/assets/defense/literature/nguyen-2025-microglia-wrap.mp4","alt":"Nguyen Movie S6: green microglia extend processes around red dextran-labelled vessels after repeated concussion; original scale bar and timestamp","label":"Nguyen S6 · microglia wrap injured vessels"}],"citations":[{"text":"Nguyen RH et al. PNAS. 2025;122:e2414316122 · Figure 3 and Movie S6.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2414316122"}],"audienceStory":{"title":"Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage","position":"below","points":["**Microglia**, resident immune cells, extend their processes around injured vessels after repeated concussion.","**Green microglia encase the red-labelled vessel.** Blocking this wrapping prevented the reduction in tracer leakage."],"definitions":["Movie S6: repeated-concussion mouse experiment. The wrapping develops over approximately one hour."]},"cueNew":{"theme":"vessels","summary":"Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage","bullets":["**Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage.**","**Microglia**, resident immune cells, extend their processes around injured vessels after repeated concussion.","**Green microglia encase the red-labelled vessel.** Blocking this wrapping prevented the reduction in tracer leakage."],"point":"Green microglial processes around the red-labelled vessel","sources":["Nguyen2025"],"detail":"Movie S6 and Figure 3. Green is GFP-labelled microglia; red is intravascular rhodamine dextran. The time stamp is hh:mm:ss. Imaging shows wrapping; the paper’s inhibition and permeability experiments support its functional contribution. This repeated-impact mouse result does not establish long-term human barrier function."},"title":false,"subtitle":false},{"focus":"immune+nvu","focusThemes":["immune","nvu"],"node":"Nguyen2025","sequenceKey":"nguyen-repair","side":"center","figureLayout":"source-single","videoPlayback":"play","timelineLabel":"minutes after repeated impact · vessel wrapping","figures":[{"role":"mouse-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/c57bl6-mouse-commons.jpg","alt":"Illustrative C57BL/6-background mouse, not a study animal","label":"C57BL/6-BACKGROUND MOUSE · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"movie","video":"decks/assets/defense/literature/nguyen-2025-microglia-wrap.mp4","alt":"Nguyen Movie S6: green microglia extend processes around red dextran-labelled vessels after repeated concussion; original scale bar and timestamp","label":"Nguyen S6 · microglia wrap injured vessels"}],"citations":[{"text":"Nguyen RH et al. PNAS. 2025;122:e2414316122 · Figure 3 and Movie S6.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2414316122"}],"audienceStory":{"title":"Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage","position":"below","points":["**Microglia**, resident immune cells, extend their processes around injured vessels after repeated concussion.","**Green microglia encase the red-labelled vessel.** Blocking this wrapping prevented the reduction in tracer leakage."],"definitions":["Movie S6: repeated-concussion mouse experiment. The wrapping develops over approximately one hour."]},"cueNew":{"theme":"vessels","summary":"Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage","bullets":["**Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage.**","**Microglia**, resident immune cells, extend their processes around injured vessels after repeated concussion.","**Green microglia encase the red-labelled vessel.** Blocking this wrapping prevented the reduction in tracer leakage."],"point":"Watch green processes progressively encase the red vessel","sources":["Nguyen2025"],"detail":"Movie S6 and Figure 3. Green is GFP-labelled microglia; red is intravascular rhodamine dextran. The time stamp is hh:mm:ss. Imaging shows wrapping; the paper’s inhibition and permeability experiments support its functional contribution. This repeated-impact mouse result does not establish long-term human barrier function."},"title":false,"subtitle":false},{"focus":"immune+nvu","focusThemes":["immune","nvu"],"sequenceKey":"nguyen-repair","side":"center","subtitle":false,"timelineLabel":"minutes after repeated impact · astrocyte removal","figureLayout":"source-single","videoPlayback":"play","figures":[{"role":"mouse-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/c57bl6-mouse-commons.jpg","alt":"Illustrative C57BL/6-background mouse, not a study animal","label":"C57BL/6-BACKGROUND MOUSE · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"movie","video":"decks/assets/defense/literature/nguyen-2025-astrocyte-removal.mp4","alt":"Nguyen Movie S8: microglia in green phagocytose red astrocyte fragments detached from blue-labelled vessels after repeated concussion; original scale bar and timestamps retained; title card omitted","label":"Nguyen S8 · microglia engulf detached astrocyte fragments"}],"tag":"MOUSE · REPEATED CLOSED-HEAD IMPACTS · MINUTES TO TWO WEEKS","text":"Microglia help seal injured vessels. A separate mouse study finds focal capillary abnormalities at two weeks.","citations":[{"text":"Nguyen RH et al. PNAS. 2025;122:e2414316122 · Movie S8.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2414316122"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"Microglia also remove detached astrocytes","position":"below","points":["**Microglia engulf detached astrocyte fragments**, including processes that normally support vessels.","After repeated injury, **reduced tracer leakage can coexist with greater loss of vascular-supporting cells**."],"definitions":["The consequences of this remodelling for long-term vascular regulation remain unmeasured."]},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-metabolism/3","node":"Nguyen2025","cueSourceAliases":["concussion-circuit-metabolism/2"]},{"focus":"nvu","focusThemes":["nvu"],"sequenceKey":"tagge-structure","side":"center","subtitle":false,"timelineLabel":"two weeks after repeated impacts · capillary structure","figureLayout":"source-single","videoPlayback":"pause","figures":[{"role":"mouse-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/c57bl6-mouse-commons.jpg","alt":"Illustrative C57BL/6-background mouse, not a study animal","label":"C57BL/6-BACKGROUND MOUSE · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"structure","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/tagge-em-clean.png","alt":"Mouse electron microscopy showing focal CA1 neurovascular abnormalities two weeks after repeated impact","label":"Tagge · capillary abnormalities at two weeks"}],"tag":"MOUSE · REPEATED CLOSED-HEAD IMPACTS · MINUTES TO TWO WEEKS","text":"Microglia help seal injured vessels. A separate mouse study finds focal capillary abnormalities at two weeks.","citations":[{"text":"Tagge CA et al. Brain. 2018;141:422–458 · Figure 3X/Z.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/brain/awx350"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"Microvascular injury remains visible two weeks after impact","points":["After repeated impacts in **mice**, electron microscopy shows **swollen astrocytic end-feet** around the capillary.","The supporting layer around the vessel, the **basal lamina**, is abnormally thick and tortuous."],"bridge":"","position":"below"},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-metabolism/4","node":"Tagge2018"},{"focus":"mechanics+biomarkers","focusThemes":["mechanics","biomarkers"],"nodes":["Ulyanova2023","Reyes2023"],"sequenceKey":"first-week-signals","side":"center","subtitle":false,"timelineLabel":"day 7–8 · two biological clocks","figureLayout":"week-pair","figures":[{"role":"pig-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/yucatan-minipig-sinc.jpg","alt":"Illustrative Yucatan minipig, not a study animal","label":"YUCATAN MINIPIG · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"human-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/einstein-human-context-4x3.jpg","alt":"Albert Einstein portrait used only as a neutral visual shorthand for the human cohort; not a study participant","label":"HUMAN ADULTS · ILLUSTRATIVE","position":"50% 28%"},{"role":"electrical","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature-network/ulyanova-fig3b-inhibitory.png","alt":"Ulyanova Figure 3b, complete panel: firing rate, spike width and amplitude of putative inhibitory neurons in hippocampal CA1, control versus seven days after rotational injury","label":"Ulyanova · inhibitory-neuron firing and spikes, day 7"},{"role":"blood","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature-network/reyes-fig1ac-gfap-nfl.png","alt":"Reyes Figure 1A and C, complete panels arranged side by side: plasma GFAP and NfL in controls and CT-negative mild TBI at less than six hours and approximately one week; original logarithmic axes, observations and p values retained","label":"Reyes · GFAP and NfL · controls, <6 h, ~1 week"}],"tag":"YUCATAN MINIPIG + HUMAN mTBI · THE FIRST WEEK","text":"Reduced inhibitory-neuron firing at day seven in minipigs. Human GFAP and NfL measurements distinguish CT-negative mTBI from controls at different times.","citations":[{"text":"Ulyanova AV et al. Communications Biology. 2023;6:1136.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-023-05491-w"},{"text":"Reyes J et al. Neurology. 2023;101:e1992–e2004.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1212/WNL.0000000000207881"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"","points":[],"columns":[{"title":"Concussion reduces inhibitory-neuron firing in a memory circuit","points":["**Minipigs, day 7:** putative inhibitory neurons in the hippocampus fired less frequently after rotational injury.","The **hippocampus supports memory**. Reduced inhibitory firing is consistent with impaired control of network activity."]},{"title":"Blood biomarkers can help identify concussion when CT appears normal","points":["**GFAP**, an astrocyte structural protein, was elevated within **6 hours** compared with uninjured controls.","**NfL**, an axonal structural protein, was elevated at **~1 week**. Different markers provide information at different times."]}],"bridge":"","position":"below"},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-metabolism/7"}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"plainHeadings":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","alt":"Interactive literature network of 19,542 papers with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links"}],"seconds":82,"notes":"Click 0\n- **Mechanical impact can compromise the blood–brain barrier.**\n- Rapid head acceleration can deform **endothelial cells**, the cells lining small blood vessels, and potentially disrupt the barrier.\n- The vessel and its surrounding cells maintain the **chemical separation between blood and brain tissue**.\nPoint to: The neurovascular and immune-response circuits\nThe mechanical link to vessels motivates examining barrier exchange and vascular function. Endothelial cells form the vascular lining. Supporting cells around the vessel contribute to barrier maintenance and vascular regulation. The next two papers are separate mouse experiments, with different injury protocols and observation times.\n\nClick 1\n- **Nguyen et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nMovie S6 and Figure 3. Green is GFP-labelled microglia; red is intravascular rhodamine dextran. The time stamp is hh:mm:ss. Imaging shows wrapping; the paper’s inhibition and permeability experiments support its functional contribution. This repeated-impact mouse result does not establish long-term human barrier function.\n\nClick 2\n- **Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage**\n- **Microglia**, resident immune cells, extend their processes around injured vessels after repeated concussion.\n- **Green microglia encase the red-labelled vessel.** Blocking this wrapping prevented the reduction in tracer leakage.\nPoint to: Green microglial processes around the red-labelled vessel\nMovie S6 and Figure 3. Green is GFP-labelled microglia; red is intravascular rhodamine dextran. The time stamp is hh:mm:ss. Imaging shows wrapping; the paper’s inhibition and permeability experiments support its functional contribution. This repeated-impact mouse result does not establish long-term human barrier function.\n\nClick 3\n- **Microglia wrap injured vessels and help limit leakage**\n- **Microglia**, resident immune cells, extend their processes around injured vessels after repeated concussion.\n- **Green microglia encase the red-labelled vessel.** Blocking this wrapping prevented the reduction in tracer leakage.\nPoint to: Watch green processes progressively encase the red vessel\nMovie S6 and Figure 3. Green is GFP-labelled microglia; red is intravascular rhodamine dextran. The time stamp is hh:mm:ss. Imaging shows wrapping; the paper’s inhibition and permeability experiments support its functional contribution. This repeated-impact mouse result does not establish long-term human barrier function.\n\nClick 4\n- **Microglia also remove detached astrocytes**\n- **Microglia engulf detached astrocyte fragments**, including processes that normally support vessels.\n- After repeated injury, **reduced tracer leakage can coexist with greater loss of vascular-supporting cells**.\nPoint to: Watch green microglia engulf red material detached from the blue vessel\nMovie S8 shows engulfment of detached astrocytic material, distinct from the vessel-wrapping response in Movie S6. The paper links vascular sealing to protective microglia while showing greater parenchymal injury after repeat concussion. Do not equate lower tracer leakage with complete recovery of the neurovascular unit.\n\nClick 5\n- Return to **blood vessels and their supporting cells**.\nPoint to: The circuit, with no paper figures or bullet text on screen\nThis is the repeated-impact mouse experiment at two weeks. X shows the capillary and surrounding structures; Z is a closer view. c = capillary, e = endothelial cell, p = pericyte, ae = astrocytic end-foot. A pericyte is a vessel-associated support cell. These are focal ultrastructural abnormalities, not proof of a particular lesion in chronic civilian PPCS.\n\nClick 6\n- **Tagge et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nThis is the repeated-impact mouse experiment at two weeks. X shows the capillary and surrounding structures; Z is a closer view. c = capillary, e = endothelial cell, p = pericyte, ae = astrocytic end-foot. A pericyte is a vessel-associated support cell. These are focal ultrastructural abnormalities, not proof of a particular lesion in chronic civilian PPCS.\n\nClick 7\n- **Microvascular injury remains visible two weeks after impact**\n- After repeated impacts in **mice**, electron microscopy shows **swollen astrocytic end-feet** around the capillary.\n- The supporting layer around the vessel, the **basal lamina**, is abnormally thick and tortuous.\nPoint to: Tagge Figure 3X and Z: swollen end-feet and abnormal capillary structure\nThe displayed ultrastructure is Figure 3X and Z (not Figure 4): swollen astrocytic end-feet and abnormal capillary structure at two weeks. The single-repeat mouse protocol used two impacts 15 minutes apart. Selected adolescent post-mortem cases are a separate part of this paper. These observations do not establish the mechanism of chronic civilian PPCS.\n\nClick 8\n- Return to **mechanics and neuronal function and blood biomarkers**.\nPoint to: The circuit, with no paper figures or bullet text on screen\nUlyanova: CA1 is a subdivision of the hippocampus. These cells were classified from extracellular recordings as putative interneurons. Firing averaged 7.46 versus 3.09 Hz, with lower spike width and amplitude. The displayed panel does not show the separate pyramidal-cell gamma result. Reyes: adults with CT-negative mTBI, not NFL players. Approximately one-week follow-up, median day 8. The paper also found acute UCH-L1 and IL-6 elevations; those panels are not displayed. UCH-L1 participates in neuronal protein recycling; IL-6 is an immune-signalling molecule with extracerebral sources. Diagnostic performance is analysed separately. Biomarkers can assist clinical assessment; these group differences do not establish a universal diagnostic threshold or isolate BBB transport from release and clearance.\n\nClick 9\n- **Ulyanova et al. · Reyes et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nUlyanova: CA1 is a subdivision of the hippocampus. These cells were classified from extracellular recordings as putative interneurons. Firing averaged 7.46 versus 3.09 Hz, with lower spike width and amplitude. The displayed panel does not show the separate pyramidal-cell gamma result. Reyes: adults with CT-negative mTBI, not NFL players. Approximately one-week follow-up, median day 8. The paper also found acute UCH-L1 and IL-6 elevations; those panels are not displayed. UCH-L1 participates in neuronal protein recycling; IL-6 is an immune-signalling molecule with extracerebral sources. Diagnostic performance is analysed separately. Biomarkers can assist clinical assessment; these group differences do not establish a universal diagnostic threshold or isolate BBB transport from release and clearance.\n\nClick 10\n- **Concussion reduces inhibitory-neuron firing in a memory circuit** — Minipigs, day 7: putative inhibitory neurons in the hippocampus fired less frequently after rotational injury. The hippocampus supports memory. Reduced inhibitory firing is consistent with impaired control of network activity.\n- **Blood biomarkers can help identify concussion when CT appears normal** — GFAP, an astrocyte structural protein, was elevated within 6 hours compared with uninjured controls. NfL, an axonal structural protein, was elevated at ~1 week. Different markers provide information at different times.\nPoint to: Left: Ulyanova Figure 3b firing rate. Right: Reyes Figure 1A/C, GFAP and NfL against controls\nUlyanova: CA1 is a subdivision of the hippocampus. These cells were classified from extracellular recordings as putative interneurons. Firing averaged 7.46 versus 3.09 Hz, with lower spike width and amplitude. The displayed panel does not show the separate pyramidal-cell gamma result. Reyes: adults with CT-negative mTBI, not NFL players. Approximately one-week follow-up, median day 8. The paper also found acute UCH-L1 and IL-6 elevations; those panels are not displayed. UCH-L1 participates in neuronal protein recycling; IL-6 is an immune-signalling molecule with extracerebral sources. Diagnostic performance is analysed separately. Biomarkers can assist clinical assessment; these group differences do not establish a universal diagnostic threshold or isolate BBB transport from release and clearance.","backup":true},{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"id":"concussion-circuit-biomarkers","order":104,"subchapter":"Literature","blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I"],"timelineVisitedBefore":["Giza2014","Enger2015","Pacheco2019","Taotao2021","Nguyen2025","Tagge2018","Ulyanova2023","Reyes2023"],"paperSequence":true,"returnAfterLast":false,"states":[{"focus":"nvu+human_imaging","focusThemes":["nvu","human_imaging"],"nodes":["Yoen2021"],"sequenceKey":"human-barrier","side":"center","subtitle":false,"timelineLabel":"median 2 months · human DCE parameters","figureLayout":"human-dce","videoPlayback":"play","figures":[{"role":"human-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/einstein-human-context-4x3.jpg","alt":"Albert Einstein portrait used only as a neutral visual shorthand for an adult human cohort; not a study participant","label":"HUMAN ADULTS · ILLUSTRATIVE","position":"50% 50%"},{"role":"transfer","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/yoen-cortex-ktrans.png","alt":"Yoen cortical dynamic contrast-enhanced MRI transfer-constant result","label":"Yoen · cortical contrast transfer, Kᵗʳᵃⁿˢ"},{"role":"plasma","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/yoen-vp.png","alt":"Yoen plasma-volume results in brainstem and cerebellar white matter","label":"Yoen · cerebellar white matter + brainstem plasma volume"}],"tag":"HUMAN PCS · DYNAMIC CONTRAST MRI","text":"Patlak analysis suggests subtle microvascular changes in a symptomatic clinical cohort.","citations":[{"text":"Yoen H et al. Korean Journal of Radiology. 2021;22:118–130.","url":"https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"publicationYear":"2019","audienceStory":{"title":"Contrast MRI suggests regional microvascular changes in PCS","points":["**Patlak estimates:** higher cortical contrast transfer; lower plasma volume in cerebellar white matter and brainstem.","**42 symptomatic adults versus 29 symptomatic clinical controls;** median two months after injury."],"bridge":"","position":"below"},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-biomarkers/2"},{"focus":"metabolism+human_imaging","focusThemes":["metabolism","human_imaging"],"nodes":["Joyce2022","Teichner2021"],"sequenceKey":"chronic-metabolism","side":"center","subtitle":false,"timelineLabel":"months to years · MRS and FDG PET","figureLayout":"chronic-pair","figures":[{"role":"human-context","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/einstein-human-context-4x3.jpg","alt":"Albert Einstein portrait used only as a neutral visual shorthand for adult human cohorts; not a study participant","label":"HUMAN ADULTS · ILLUSTRATIVE","position":"50% 28%"},{"role":"mrs","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/joyce-fig2-glu-glx.jpg","alt":"Cropped Joyce Figure 2 panels showing the Glu and Glx distributions in adults with persistent post-concussion symptoms and controls","label":"Glu = glutamate · Glx = glutamate + glutamine · mmol/L"},{"role":"pet","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/teichner-fdg-figure1-unchanged.png","alt":"Teichner Figure 1 showing three individual chronic PCS FDG PET examples","label":"Teichner · regional uptake relative to whole-brain uptake"}],"tag":"HUMAN · CHRONIC SYMPTOMS · MRS + FDG PET","text":"MRS finds lower glutamate-related signals. FDG PET shows heterogeneous glucose uptake.","citations":[{"text":"Joyce JM et al. NeuroImage: Clinical. 2022;36:103152.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nicl.2022.103152"},{"text":"Teichner EM et al. Nuclear Medicine Communications. 2021;42:772–781.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/MNM.0000000000001397"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"","points":[],"columns":[{"title":"Regional glutamate is reduced in chronic PPCS","points":["**Around two years after injury**, glutamate and glutamate-plus-glutamine were lower in the anterior cingulate cortex.","The authors propose an **ongoing energy shortage**. Spectroscopy measures concentrations, not the rate of oxygen use."]},{"title":"Chronic PCS shows regional glucose hypermetabolism and hypometabolism","points":["**About 2.4 years after injury**, regional glucose-tracer uptake was both higher and lower relative to whole-brain uptake.","These are **different patients**. The results do not determine absolute whole-brain glucose metabolism."]}],"bridge":"","position":"below"},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-biomarkers/5"}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"plainHeadings":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","alt":"Interactive literature network of 19,542 papers with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links"}],"seconds":78,"notes":"Click 0\n- Now consider **blood vessels and their supporting cells and human imaging**.\nPoint to: The circuit, with no paper figures or bullet text on screen\nYoen: 42 patients with PCS and 29 clinical controls, many with headache. Median injury-to-scan time about two months. This was not solely a chronic PPCS cohort or a healthy-volunteer comparison. Cortical Ktrans differed in the pooled cohort (p=.042), not independently in either timing subgroup. The same difference was absent under extended Tofts. Lower vp is not itself a permeability measurement; it is a plasma-volume estimate, unlike the thesis blood-volume convention. RPQ/neuropsychological correlations were not significant. O’Brown Figure 2 video 2: green 10 kDa dextran outside magenta vessels in a three-day-old larva during barrier development. The adult fish photograph is species context. The movie does not determine the mechanism underlying Yoen’s MRI findings.\n\nClick 1\n- **Yoen et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nYoen: 42 patients with PCS and 29 clinical controls, many with headache. Median injury-to-scan time about two months. This was not solely a chronic PPCS cohort or a healthy-volunteer comparison. Cortical Ktrans differed in the pooled cohort (p=.042), not independently in either timing subgroup. The same difference was absent under extended Tofts. Lower vp is not itself a permeability measurement; it is a plasma-volume estimate, unlike the thesis blood-volume convention. RPQ/neuropsychological correlations were not significant. O’Brown Figure 2 video 2: green 10 kDa dextran outside magenta vessels in a three-day-old larva during barrier development. The adult fish photograph is species context. The movie does not determine the mechanism underlying Yoen’s MRI findings.\n\nClick 2\n- **Contrast MRI suggests regional microvascular changes in PCS**\n- **Patlak estimates:** higher cortical contrast transfer; lower plasma volume in cerebellar white matter and brainstem.\n- **42 symptomatic adults versus 29 symptomatic clinical controls;** median two months after injury.\nPoint to: Yoen Figure 4A/B/C: cortical Ktrans and regional plasma-volume estimates\nHigher cortical Ktrans was model-dependent and absent with extended Tofts. No significant continuous association with RPQ or cognitive scores; the categorical auditory-attention comparison was small. The authors acknowledge implausible absolute vp estimates. Keep transfer and volume separate; neither specifies the cellular mechanism.\n\nClick 3\n- **The persistence of the acute metabolic crisis remains uncertain.**\n- **Whether this acute disturbance persists into the chronic phase of PPCS is less well established.**\nPoint to: Return to the metabolism circuit, now in persistent symptoms\nThe acute mechanism motivates the question. Later clinical observations must establish what remains altered; they do not automatically demonstrate persistence of the same acute cascade.\n\nClick 4\n- **Joyce et al. · Teichner et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nJoyce Figure 2A: tissue metabolite concentrations, not the extracellular glutamate transient shown by Enger. The finding was region-specific and may reflect energy shortage; antioxidant metabolism is another interpretation the authors discuss. It does not directly measure ATP or oxygen consumption. Teichner Figure 1: individual examples, not a cohort-average map or a sequence in one patient. FDG is a glucose analogue. The analysis normalises uptake to whole-brain activity, so it does not establish an absolute whole-brain glucose-consumption rate. Compensation and inflammation are proposed explanations, not independently identified processes.\n\nClick 5\n- **Regional glutamate is reduced in chronic PPCS** — Around two years after injury, glutamate and glutamate-plus-glutamine were lower in the anterior cingulate cortex. The authors propose an ongoing energy shortage. Spectroscopy measures concentrations, not the rate of oxygen use.\n- **Chronic PCS shows regional glucose hypermetabolism and hypometabolism** — About 2.4 years after injury, regional glucose-tracer uptake was both higher and lower relative to whole-brain uptake. These are different patients. The results do not determine absolute whole-brain glucose metabolism.\nPoint to: Joyce Glu/Glx distributions, then Teichner’s three individual PET examples\nJoyce: 29 PPCS and 29 matched controls; lower anterior-cingulate Glu/Glx, no sensorimotor group difference. Energy shortage is the authors’ interpretation, not measured ATP depletion. Teichner: 64 patients and 37 controls; relative regional FDG changes, not an absolute global glucose-use measurement. These chronic adult cohorts predate our work.","backup":true},{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"id":"concussion-circuit-challenge","order":105,"subchapter":"Literature","blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I"],"timelineVisitedBefore":["Giza2014","Enger2015","Pacheco2019","Taotao2021","Nguyen2025","Tagge2018","Ulyanova2023","Reyes2023","Yoen2021","Joyce2022","Teichner2021"],"paperSequence":true,"returnAfterLast":true,"states":[{"focus":"nvu+immune","focusThemes":["nvu","immune"],"nodes":["Veksler2020","Rubin2023"],"sequenceKey":"football-remote","side":"center","subtitle":false,"timelineLabel":"repeated exposure to years after play","figureLayout":"football-pair","figures":[{"role":"amateur","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/amateur-football-game.jpg","alt":"Illustrative amateur American football game, not study participants","label":"AMATEUR PLAYERS · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"professional","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/nfl-players-game.jpg","alt":"Illustrative National Football League players, not study participants","label":"FORMER NFL PLAYERS · ILLUSTRATIVE"},{"role":"dce","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/veksler-control-player-distribution.png","alt":"Representative late-enhancement DCE maps and distribution in amateur American football players and controls","label":"Veksler · normalised permeability index (NPI)"},{"role":"pet","src":"decks/assets/defense/literature/nfl-tspo-pet.png","alt":"Group-mean TSPO PET maps in former NFL players and former elite swimmers","label":"Rubin · higher TSPO binding after retirement"}],"tag":"HUMAN · AMATEUR + FORMER PROFESSIONAL AMERICAN FOOTBALL","text":"Veksler reports enduring slow blood-to-brain transport. Rubin reports higher TSPO binding after NFL retirement. These are different measurements and cohorts.","citations":[{"text":"Veksler R et al. Brain. 2020;143:1826–1842.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/brain/awaa140"},{"text":"Rubin LH et al. JAMA Network Open. 2023;6:e2340580.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2023.40580"},{"text":"TSPO interpretation: Nutma et al. Nat Commun. 2023;14:5247.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-40937-z"}],"title":false,"nodeLabelYear":true,"audienceStory":{"title":"","points":[],"columns":[{"title":"Slow contrast transport persists in a subset of football players","points":["**NPI, the normalised permeability index,** measures late enhancement relative to the venous reference slope.","Repeat scans showed **persistence over months**, with different individual trajectories; symptom severity did not track NPI."]},{"title":"Former NFL players show higher binding to the mitochondrial protein TSPO","points":["**1–12 years after NFL retirement**, TSPO binding was higher than in former college swimmers.","**TSPO is a mitochondrial protein in several cell types.** Binding alone cannot identify harmful microglial activation."]}],"bridge":"","position":"below"},"cueSource":"concussion-circuit-challenge/2"}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"plainHeadings":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","alt":"Interactive literature network of 19,542 papers with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links"}],"seconds":48,"notes":"Click 0\n- Now consider **blood vessels and their supporting cells and glial responses**.\nPoint to: The circuit, with no paper figures or bullet text on screen\nVeksler Figure 1C/D/G shows cross-sectional maps and the group distribution. Persistence comes from separate repeat-scan panels E/F/I, extending to about ten months. NPI is a normalised late tissue signal slope referenced to the superior sagittal sinus, not Patlak Ki or Ktrans. The human scans alone do not identify a cellular transport route. Rubin: 27 former NFL players and 27 former college swimmers. Compare groups within each genotype row, because colour-scale maxima differ between rows. Binding did not account for the cognitive differences. Cross-sectional imaging 1–12 years after retirement does not show continuous elevation throughout that interval. Nutma’s work cautions against equating human TSPO binding with microglial activation: the signal has multiple cellular sources, and the examined human conditions did not show the activation-associated per-cell increases found in some rodents. That does not identify the cause of Rubin’s signal.\n\nClick 1\n- **Veksler et al. · Rubin et al.**\nPoint to: The highlighted paper nodes\nVeksler Figure 1C/D/G shows cross-sectional maps and the group distribution. Persistence comes from separate repeat-scan panels E/F/I, extending to about ten months. NPI is a normalised late tissue signal slope referenced to the superior sagittal sinus, not Patlak Ki or Ktrans. The human scans alone do not identify a cellular transport route. Rubin: 27 former NFL players and 27 former college swimmers. Compare groups within each genotype row, because colour-scale maxima differ between rows. Binding did not account for the cognitive differences. Cross-sectional imaging 1–12 years after retirement does not show continuous elevation throughout that interval. Nutma’s work cautions against equating human TSPO binding with microglial activation: the signal has multiple cellular sources, and the examined human conditions did not show the activation-associated per-cell increases found in some rodents. That does not identify the cause of Rubin’s signal.\n\nClick 2\n- **Slow contrast transport persists in a subset of football players** — NPI, the normalised permeability index, measures late enhancement relative to the venous reference slope. Repeat scans showed persistence over months, with different individual trajectories; symptom severity did not track NPI.\n- **Former NFL players show higher binding to the mitochondrial protein TSPO** — 1–12 years after NFL retirement, TSPO binding was higher than in former college swimmers. TSPO is a mitochondrial protein in several cell types. Binding alone cannot identify harmful microglial activation.\nPoint to: Left: Veksler control/player maps and NPI distribution. Right: Rubin players versus controls within each genotype row\nVeksler NPI is a normalised late slope (approximately 6–17 minutes), not modified-Tofts Ktrans or Patlak Ki. Tofts Ktrans and blood biomarkers did not differ significantly. Animal microscopy, histology and modelling support the authors’ slow-transport interpretation without identifying the human transport route directly. Rubin: higher TSPO binding and poorer cognition coexisted, but binding did not correlate with cognition within the former NFL group. Nutma: human TSPO signal is not a simple measure of per-cell microglial activation; cell density and non-microglial sources matter.\n\nClick 3\n- Return to **blood vessels and their supporting cells and glial responses**.\nPoint to: The vascular and glial circuits, with the paper figures dismissed\nVeksler Figure 1C/D/G shows cross-sectional maps and the group distribution. Persistence comes from separate repeat-scan panels E/F/I, extending to about ten months. NPI is a normalised late tissue signal slope referenced to the superior sagittal sinus, not Patlak Ki or Ktrans. The human scans alone do not identify a cellular transport route. Rubin: 27 former NFL players and 27 former college swimmers. Compare groups within each genotype row, because colour-scale maxima differ between rows. Binding did not account for the cognitive differences. Cross-sectional imaging 1–12 years after retirement does not show continuous elevation throughout that interval. Nutma’s work cautions against equating human TSPO binding with microglial activation: the signal has multiple cellular sources, and the examined human conditions did not show the activation-associated per-cell increases found in some rodents. That does not identify the cause of Rubin’s signal.","backup":true},{"id":"concussion-thesis-gap","order":106,"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":520,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I"],"states":[{"focus":"all","title":false,"subtitle":false},{"focus":"cohorts","title":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohorts":["ppcsTracer","ppcsMetabolism"],"cohortLegend":false,"subtitle":false},{"focus":"cohorts","title":false,"citations":["Study II · Tireli et al. Blood-brain barrier disruption in patients with persistent post-concussion symptoms. 2026.","Study III · Tireli et al. Reduced cerebral oxygen metabolism in patients with persistent post-concussion symptoms. 2026."],"cohorts":["ppcsTracer","ppcsMetabolism"],"cohortLegend":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"Oxygen consumption and hypoxia","color":"#f0805a"}],"subtitle":false},{"focus":"cohorts","title":false,"satellites":[{"node":"Study I","connectTo":"Study II","x":210,"y":716,"label":"Study I: p-Brain","role":"Analysis framework for Study II","color":"#78a8ff"}],"citations":["Study I · Tireli et al. p-Brain: a modular open-source framework for quantitative DCE-MRI. 2026.","Study II · Tireli et al. Blood-brain barrier disruption in patients with persistent post-concussion symptoms. 2026.","Study III · Tireli et al. Reduced cerebral oxygen metabolism in patients with persistent post-concussion symptoms. 2026."],"cohorts":["ppcsTracer","ppcsMetabolism"],"cohortLegend":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"Oxygen consumption and hypoxia","color":"#f0805a"}],"subtitle":false}],"audienceStory":[{"title":"Acute injury motivates questions about chronic physiology","points":["Experiments show how concussion can disturb **electrical signalling, energy balance and vessels**.","Human studies identify later physiological differences. Which changes remain in **chronic civilian PPCS**?"],"bridge":"","position":"above-map"},{"title":"Our studies in adults with persistent symptoms","points":["Clinical DCE studies already examine **tracer transfer and vascular volume** in symptomatic adults.","Our studies examine regional tracer measures and **global oxygen consumption during hypoxia** in persistent symptoms."],"position":"below"},{"title":"Tracer transport and the response to reduced oxygen supply","points":["**Study II:** voxel-wise Kᵢ and vᵦ, aggregated into **seven tissue compartments and 85 parcels**.","**Study III:** test how oxygen consumption responds when oxygen availability falls. The partly overlapping cohorts attended **separate MRI visits**."],"bridge":"","position":"above-map"},{"title":"Study I provides the method for Study II","points":["**p-Brain** makes the contrast-MRI analysis systematic.","Its applications extend beyond concussion."],"position":"below"}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"plainHeadings":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","alt":"Interactive literature network of 19,542 papers with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links"}],"subchapter":"Literature","seconds":25,"notes":"Click 0\n- **Acute injury motivates questions about chronic physiology**\n- Experiments show how concussion can disturb **electrical signalling, energy balance and vessels**.\n- Human studies identify later physiological differences. Which changes remain in **chronic civilian PPCS**?\nPoint to: The complete evidence landscape\nThe contribution is the chronic civilian cohort, acquisition and analysis workflow, combined influx/blood-volume interpretation and complementary test of oxygen-metabolic reserve. Yoen already used Patlak modelling, segmentation and RPQ, so those are not individually new. Testing symptom associations does not establish a significant association. Earlier studies measured oxygen metabolism after mild TBI using other approaches; no first-ever CMRO2 claim is made. These measurements constrain explanations but do not uniquely identify mitochondrial failure, inflammation, a cellular BBB lesion or capillary transit heterogeneity as the cause of symptoms. Historical-control differences and modelling assumptions remain important.\nSources: Thesis, https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016\n\nClick 1\n- Clinical DCE studies already examine **tracer transfer and vascular volume** in symptomatic adults.\n- Our studies examine regional tracer measures and **global oxygen consumption during hypoxia** in persistent symptoms.\nPoint to: Clinical tracer and metabolic comparison groups\nThe highlighted clinical comparisons are Yoo and Yoen for DCE-MRI, and Joyce and Teichner for metabolism in chronic symptoms. Yoen includes earlier and persistent symptoms. Generic methods and healthy hypoxia studies remain background. The next click introduces the two study markers.\nSources: https://doi.org/10.1007/s00330-018-5656-z, https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nicl.2022.103152, https://doi.org/10.1097/MNM.0000000000001397\n\nClick 2\n- **Study II:** regional tracer transfer and blood volume.\n- **Study III:** global oxygen consumption and blood flow, at rest and during controlled hypoxia.\nPoint to: Small Study II and Study III markers within the thematic map\nStudy II extends clinical DCE work such as Yoo and Yoen. Study III combines global oxygen consumption and flow with a controlled hypoxic challenge in adult PPCS. The published HOT-POCS protocol plans oxygen measurements in civilian PPCS using different MRI methods around hyperbaric treatment. No claim of the first PPCS oxygen measurement. Our baseline cohorts partly overlap, with separate MRI visits. Cross-sectional differences do not establish symptom causation.\nSources: Thesis, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00330-018-5656-z, https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10320499/\n\nClick 3\n- **p-Brain** makes the contrast-MRI analysis systematic.\n- Its applications extend beyond concussion.\nPoint to: Study I linked to Study II\nStudy I supplies the analysis used in Study II and has applications beyond concussion. It is the methodological foundation of this PPCS investigation, rather than a separate clinical PPCS study.\nSources: Thesis","backup":true},{"id":"complete-scanner-brain","omit":true,"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"iris","transitionMs":1450,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"voxelNVU","at":"figure","args":{"revealSteps":0,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"brainScale":1.27,"center":[0.49,0.48],"caption":false}}],"seconds":25,"notes":"After the evidence landscape has established what is known and unresolved, return to the complete brain inside the scanner lattice. There is no slide title, voxel highlight or added reveal. The canvas uses the full page height so the original brain scale is preserved without clipping its superior or inferior extent. Advance directly to the measured gadolinium bolus passage.","order":12},{"omit":true,"subchapter":"From fields to proton spin","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"cut","layout":["field"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"field":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"qftSpinorExcitation","at":"field","args":{"revealSteps":4,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":18,"dpr":1}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{QCD+QED}}={}&-\\frac14G^a_{\\mu\\nu}G^{a\\mu\\nu}-\\frac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}+\\sum_{q=u,d,s,\\ldots}\\bar q\\left(i\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu-m_q\\right)q,\\\\ D_\\mu={}&\\partial_\\mu-i g_sT^aG^a_\\mu-i eQ_qA_\\mu,\\qquad G^a_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu G^a_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu G^a_\\mu+g_sf^{abc}G^b_\\mu G^c_\\nu\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}=-\\frac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}+\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu-m_p)N-\\frac{e\\kappa_p}{4m_p}\\bar N\\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}NF_{\\mu\\nu}+\\cdots"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{\\phi}=\\frac12\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi-\\frac12m^2\\phi^2"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{\\phi}=\\frac12\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi-\\frac12m^2\\phi^2"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}=-\\frac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}+\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu-m_p)N-\\frac{e\\kappa_p}{4m_p}\\bar N\\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}NF_{\\mu\\nu}+\\cdots"}],"overlay":{"left":"5%","top":"1.4%","width":"90%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\\Delta\\varepsilon_{\\Psi,\\Lambda}(\\mathbf x,t)&=\\langle\\Psi|[T^{00}(\\mathbf x,t)]_{R,\\Lambda}|\\Psi\\rangle-\\langle0|[T^{00}(\\mathbf x,t)]_{R,\\Lambda}|0\\rangle,\\\\ \\Sigma_c(t)&=\\{\\mathbf x:\\Delta\\varepsilon_{\\Psi,\\Lambda}(\\mathbf x,t)=c\\}\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"Q_{\\mathrm{MRI}}r_p/\\hbar\\ll1,\\qquad \\Delta\\varepsilon_{\\Lambda}=K_{\\Lambda}*\\Delta\\varepsilon,\\qquad \\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{QCD+QED}}\\xrightarrow[Q]{\\mathrm{matching}}\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}"},{"tex":"\\Psi[\\phi,t]=\\langle\\phi|\\Psi(t)\\rangle,\\qquad \\hat\\phi(\\mathbf x)\\Psi=\\phi(\\mathbf x)\\Psi,\\qquad \\int\\mathcal D\\phi\\,|\\Psi[\\phi,t]|^2=1"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}q_{\\lambda}[\\phi]&=\\int d^3x\\,u_{\\lambda}(\\mathbf x)\\phi(\\mathbf x),\\\\ \\Psi_0(q_{\\lambda})&\\propto e^{-\\omega_{\\lambda}q_{\\lambda}^2/2},\\qquad \\Psi_{1_{\\lambda}}(q_{\\lambda})=\\sqrt{2\\omega_{\\lambda}}\\,q_{\\lambda}\\Psi_0(q_{\\lambda}),\\qquad \\Psi_{1_{\\lambda}}(0)=0\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}|f,s\\rangle&=b_s^\\dagger[f]|0\\rangle,\\qquad \\int d\\Pi_p\\,|f(\\mathbf p)|^2=1,\\\\ \\rho_f(\\mathbf x,t)&=\\langle f,s|:\\bar N(x)\\gamma^0N(x):|f,s\\rangle,\\qquad \\int d^3x\\,\\rho_f(\\mathbf x,t)=1\\end{aligned}"}],"overlay":{"left":"7%","bottom":"4.5%","width":"86%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}}],"source":"[@pdgQCD2025; @pdgProton2025; @tongqft; @harlowQFT2024; @floreaniniJackiw1988; @mitQftPauli2010; @hill2012nrqed]","seconds":115,"notes":"Keep one equation at the top and one at the bottom; let the moving manifold carry the rest. Begin below the proton scale. The expression at the top is the relevant QCD+QED sector, not the complete Standard Model. The rendered object is one level surface of a renormalised, vacuum-subtracted, coarse-grained energy density in a localised state |Psi>. It is not the quantum field itself, not a proton membrane, not spacetime and not a lattice-QCD calculation. Say: 'At this scale the state is described in quark, gluon and electromagnetic fields. I am drawing one derived isosurface of a local observable so that we can watch the description change.' The folds and breathing are illustrative scale texture.\n\nClick once. Say: 'MRI transfers momenta so small that Q_MRI r_p over hbar is negligible. The scanner cannot resolve the quark-gluon structure. We therefore match QCD+QED onto a proton effective field theory.' The smoothing is loss of spatial resolution and integration of short-distance modes; it is not a rough proton physically relaxing into a smooth one. Matching preserves low-energy amplitudes and packages composite structure into mass, form factors and Wilson coefficients. N(x) is a local EFT interpolating field, not a claim that a physical proton has zero size.\n\nClick again. Say: 'But what does a quantum state of a field mean? I will use the simplest drawable example: a real bosonic field. One complete function phi of x is one field configuration. The state is not one privileged shape; it is the wavefunctional Psi[phi], a complex amplitude over every possible whole configuration.' Each translucent manifold is one entire configuration. Their opacity encodes probability weight. They are simultaneous configuration-space alternatives, not several particles and not a material field hopping between shapes. The detour is explicit because a Dirac-field wavefunctional is Grassmann-valued and cannot honestly be rendered as an ordinary positive cloud of real surfaces.\n\nClick again. Say: 'Choose one real normal mode u_lambda. Its amplitude q_lambda is one direction through this infinite-dimensional configuration space. The vacuum weights the undeformed configurations most strongly. Applying a_lambda dagger multiplies the Gaussian by q_lambda: the central configuration becomes a node, while positive and negative deformations carry opposite wavefunctional sign.' Cyan and amber encode that sign. Do not say the two families are two particles. For the number eigenstate |1_lambda>, the probability distribution is stationary; the common camera orbit is only a way to see the manifolds. A deterministically breathing mean field would instead describe a coherent state or a number-state superposition.\n\nClick again. Say: 'Now return to the fermionic proton. A smeared creation operator makes one normalised proton wavepacket. What I can draw as an ordinary surface is not the Grassmann field N, but a bosonic observable built from it: the normal-ordered number density.' The cloud closes into the isodensity surface rho_f. The finite warm cap shows where the one-proton density is concentrated; it is not a hard proton boundary. Its integral is one in the normalised one-proton, low-energy, no-antiproton sector. The next slide keeps this packet and asks how its internal spinor state transforms under spatial rotations.\n\n[Sources]\n- Particle Data Group. Review of Quantum Chromodynamics and proton listing, 2025 update: the relevant QCD sector and finite proton structure.\n- Harlow D. MIT 8.323 Relativistic Quantum Field Theory I, §3.1: states as wavefunctionals over complete bosonic field configurations and free-field Fock states.\n- Floreanini R, Jackiw R. Physical Review D. 1988;37:2206–2224: fermionic functional representations on Grassmann configuration space.\n- Tong D. Quantum Field Theory, §§2, 4–5: free-field quantisation, Dirac fields, Fock space and smeared creation operators.\n- Hill RJ et al. Physical Review D. 2013;87:053017; Liu H. MIT QFT lectures: low-energy proton EFT and Pauli coupling.","order":13},{"omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":["field"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"field":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"qftSpinorTransform","at":"field","args":{"revealSteps":3,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":20,"dpr":1}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}=\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu-m_p)N-\\frac{e\\kappa_p}{4m_p}\\bar N\\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}NF_{\\mu\\nu}+\\cdots"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}=\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu-m_p)N-\\frac{e\\kappa_p}{4m_p}\\bar N\\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}NF_{\\mu\\nu}+\\cdots"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}=\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu-m_p)N-\\frac{e\\kappa_p}{4m_p}\\bar N\\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}NF_{\\mu\\nu}+\\cdots"}],"overlay":{"left":"5%","top":"1.4%","width":"90%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}N'(x)&=S(R)N(R^{-1}x),\\qquad \\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{eff}}[N',F']=\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{eff}}[N,F],\\qquad R\\in SO(3),\\\\ \\mathbf J_{\\mathrm{Noether}}&=\\mathbf L+\\mathbf S\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}J_i&=\\frac14\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\sigma^{jk}=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}\\sigma_i&0\\\\0&\\sigma_i\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}=\\frac{i}{2}[\\gamma^\\mu,\\gamma^\\nu],\\\\ [J_i,J_j]&=i\\varepsilon_{ijk}J_k,\\qquad S(R)=e^{-i\\theta^iJ_i}\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}U(\\hat{\\mathbf n},\\theta)&=\\exp[-i\\theta\\,\\hat{\\mathbf n}\\!\\cdot\\!\\boldsymbol\\sigma/2],\\qquad \\mathfrak{su}(2)\\cong\\mathfrak{so}(3),\\\\ \\pi:SU(2)&\\overset{2:1}{\\longrightarrow}SO(3),\\qquad \\pi(U)=\\pi(-U)=R,\\qquad SU(2)/\\{\\pm I\\}\\cong SO(3)\\end{aligned}"}],"overlay":{"left":"4%","bottom":"5.5%","width":"92%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}}],"source":"[@tongqft; @wigner1939; @cambridgeSU2; @cambridgeDoubleCover]","seconds":95,"notes":"Keep the effective Lagrangian at the top. State the symmetry directly. In the absence of an external scanner field, physical spatial rotations form SO(3), inside the Lorentz/Poincaré symmetry of the theory. The animated Cartesian frame is the physical rotation; the soft packet is merely a state being transformed. Invariance of the action gives the conserved Noether generator J=L+S. Spin is therefore genuine angular momentum—the intrinsic part of the generator—not a claim that the proton's surface is mechanically rotating.\n\nClick once. Show the three infinitesimal generators together. For a Dirac field their intrinsic part is obtained from the spatial sigma^{jk} matrices using the Levi-Civita tensor, and they satisfy the angular-momentum commutator. The massive-particle stabiliser is SO(3); quantum states may carry projective unitary representations of it, equivalently ordinary unitary representations of its universal cover SU(2). The proton occupies the spin-one-half representation.\n\nClick again. Remove every sphere metaphor. The two complex phase markers are distinct SU(2) elements U and minus U. Both arrows converge on the same physical rotating frame R because the covering map is two-to-one: pi(U)=pi(-U). Locally, the Lie algebras su(2) and so(3) are isomorphic; globally, SU(2) modulo its centre {plus or minus I} is SO(3). This is why a spinor can change sign under a 2-pi rotation while every ordinary spatial vector has returned. The sign is observable only through interference or relative phase, not as a flipped proton surface.\n\n[Sources]\n- Tong D. Quantum Field Theory, §4: spinor representation of spatial rotations.\n- Wigner EP. Annals of Mathematics. 1939;40:149–204: massive one-particle representations.\n- Hugh Osborn. Group Theory lecture notes, University of Cambridge: SU(2) generators.\n- Cambridge PQM Supplementary Notes 4: the double cover SU(2) → SO(3).","order":14},{"omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":["field"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"field":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"qftZeemanProton","at":"field","args":{"revealSteps":2,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":20,"dpr":1}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\left.\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}\\right|_{A_\\mu=0}=\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu\\partial_\\mu-m_p)N+\\cdots"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}=\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu\\partial_\\mu-m_p)N+\\color{#67c7d5}{\\left[-e\\bar N\\gamma^\\mu N A_\\mu-\\frac{e\\kappa_p}{4m_p}\\bar N\\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}N F_{\\mu\\nu}\\right]}"},{"tex":"\\mathcal L_{p,\\mathrm{eff}}=\\bar N(i\\gamma^\\mu\\partial_\\mu-m_p)N+\\color{#67c7d5}{\\left[-e\\bar N\\gamma^\\mu N A_\\mu-\\frac{e\\kappa_p}{4m_p}\\bar N\\sigma^{\\mu\\nu}N F_{\\mu\\nu}\\right]}"}],"overlay":{"left":"3%","top":"1.2%","width":"94%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"B_0=0:\\qquad [H_0,J_i]=0,\\qquad E_{+}=E_{-}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{NRQED}}&\\supset N^\\dagger\\frac{c_Fe}{2m_p}\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf B_0N,\\qquad c_F=F_1(0)+F_2(0)=1+\\kappa_p=\\frac{g_p}{2},\\\\ H_Z&=-\\frac{c_Fe}{m_p}\\mathbf S\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf B_0=-\\gamma_p\\mathbf S\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf B_0,\\qquad SO(3)\\to SO(2)_{\\hat z},\\\\ E_{+z}&=-\\frac{\\hbar\\gamma_pB_0}{2},\\qquad E_{-z}=+\\frac{\\hbar\\gamma_pB_0}{2},\\qquad \\Delta E=\\hbar\\omega_0\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\frac{N_{\\mathrm{ground}}-N_{\\mathrm{excited}}}{N}=\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{\\hbar\\gamma_pB_0}{2k_BT}\\right)\\simeq9.9\\times10^{-6}\\quad(3\\,\\mathrm T,\\ 310\\,\\mathrm K)"}],"overlay":{"left":"3%","bottom":"0.8%","width":"94%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}}],"source":"[@mitQftPauli2010; @hill2012nrqed; @nistProton2026; @bloch1946]","seconds":105,"notes":"Keep the effective Lagrangian at the top. Begin with B0 absent: the ensemble is drawn as many proton spin states with random directions and the two S_z eigenstates are degenerate. The cloud is not a collection of tiny rotating spheres; each arrow is an expectation-value cue for one state.\n\nClick once. Turn on the cyan scanner field continuously. The energy labels E_{+z} and E_{-z} must travel with their rails while the splitting grows—there is no later reveal merely to name them. The displayed derivation makes the matching explicit. In NRQED, the magnetic operator has coefficient c_F=F_1(0)+F_2(0)=1+kappa_p=g_p/2. The unit part is the magnetic moment already implied by minimal Dirac coupling; kappa_p is the anomalous contribution encoding composite proton structure. Since S=hbar sigma/2, the Hamiltonian is H_Z=-(c_F e/m_p) S dot B=-gamma_p S dot B. For the positive proton gyromagnetic ratio, |+>_z is lower and |->_z is upper. A fixed B0 chooses z and reduces physical spatial-rotation symmetry from SO(3) to axial SO(2). This is energy splitting, not spatial separation and not a mechanically rotating proton.\n\nClick again. Thermalisation gives only a tiny population excess in the lower-energy state. The visibly emphasized extra dot is deliberately not to scale; the equation supplies the true approximately 9.9-parts-per-million imbalance at 3 T and 310 K. This tiny population excess gives an equilibrium longitudinal ensemble magnetisation M0, but there is still no coherent transverse magnetisation and therefore no detectable MR signal. The next slide performs the quantum-to-ensemble coarse-graining and applies the RF interaction.\n\n[Sources]\n- Liu H. MIT 8.323 Lecture 26 and MIT 8.324 Lecture 15: nonrelativistic Pauli limit and anomalous magnetic moment.\n- Hill RJ et al. Physical Review D. 2013;87:053017: NRQED magnetic operator and c_F=F_1(0)+F_2(0).\n- NIST CODATA: proton gyromagnetic ratio.\n- Bloch F. Nuclear Induction. Physical Review. 1946;70:460–474.","order":15},{"omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":["field"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"field":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"qftEnsembleRfBridge","at":"field","args":{"revealSteps":2,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":20,"dpr":1}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\rho_s=\\frac12(\\mathbf 1+\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\boldsymbol\\sigma),\\qquad \\mathbf M(\\mathbf r,t)=n(\\mathbf r)\\gamma_p\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho_s(\\mathbf r,t)\\mathbf S]=\\frac{n\\hbar\\gamma_p}{2}\\mathbf p"},{"tex":"\\frac{H_{\\mathrm{rot}}}{\\hbar}=-\\Delta\\omega I_z-\\omega_1(I_x\\cos\\phi+I_y\\sin\\phi),\\qquad I_i=\\frac{S_i}{\\hbar}"},{"tex":"\\rho_s^{+}=U\\rho_s^{-}U^\\dagger,\\qquad U=\\mathcal T\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\int H_{\\mathrm{rot}}(t)\\,dt\\right]"}],"overlay":{"left":"4%","top":"1.5%","width":"92%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\mathbf M_{\\mathrm{eq}}=M_0\\hat{\\mathbf z},\\qquad M_+\\equiv M_x+iM_y=0"},{"tex":"\\omega_0=\\gamma_pB_0,\\qquad \\Delta\\omega=\\omega_0-\\omega_{\\mathrm{RF}},\\qquad \\omega_1=\\gamma_p|B_1^+|"},{"tex":"\\alpha=\\gamma_p\\int_0^\\tau |B_1^+(t)|\\,dt,\\qquad M_z\\xrightarrow{\\ R_\\perp(\\alpha)\\ }M_+=M_x+iM_y"}],"overlay":{"left":"4%","bottom":"4.2%","width":"92%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}}],"source":"[@bloch1946; @rabi1954; @nistProton2026]","seconds":70,"notes":"The previous slide gave a roughly ten-parts-per-million population excess. Here I coarse-grain an enormous pool of spin-one-half states into the quantity used by MRI: the magnetisation density. Rho_s is a normalized reduced one-spin density matrix for this pool; n turns its magnetic-moment expectation into moment per unit volume. For a correlated many-spin state the exact statement is a many-body trace of the sum of moments, but this reduced-pool description is the standard bridge. The paired fine arrows cancel. Their small thermal excess gives M0 along B0. Because the transverse phases are incoherent, M-plus is zero. This is still not a detectable signal.\n\nClick once. Apply a transverse RF field. In the frame rotating at the RF frequency, the co-rotating component B1-plus is nearly static. Delta omega is the detuning and omega1 is the nutation frequency. On resonance, the RF Hamiltonian couples the Zeeman states and creates off-diagonal coherence in rho_s. The cyan orbit is slowed enormously for visibility; neither it nor the orange vector is a proton body mechanically turning. If the laboratory RF is linearly polarized, B1-plus denotes its co-rotating component, so no additional factor of two is inserted here.\n\nClick again. The pulse evolves the density matrix unitarily while relaxation during this short idealized pulse is neglected. For a resonant fixed-phase hard pulse, its area sets the flip-angle magnitude alpha. A ninety-degree pulse converts the small longitudinal ensemble order into coherent transverse magnetisation M-plus. The large orange vector is the ensemble expectation. This coherent transverse quantity—not an individual proton and not the QFT field—is what can induce receive voltage.\n\n[Sources]\n- Bloch F. Nuclear Induction. Physical Review. 1946;70:460–474: bulk nuclear magnetisation, RF excitation and induction.\n- Rabi II, Ramsey NF, Schwinger J. Reviews of Modern Physics. 1954;26:167–171: rotating-frame treatment of magnetic resonance.\n- NIST CODATA: signed proton gyromagnetic ratio; gamma_p is positive and measured in radians per second per tesla here.","order":16},{"omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":["field"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"field":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"qftBlochRelaxation","at":"field","args":{"revealSteps":2,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":20,"dpr":1}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"\\frac{d\\mathbf M}{dt}=\\gamma_p\\mathbf M\\!\\times\\!\\mathbf B-\\frac{M_x\\hat{\\mathbf x}+M_y\\hat{\\mathbf y}}{T_2}+\\frac{(M_0-M_z)\\hat{\\mathbf z}}{T_1}"},{"tex":"\\frac{d\\mathbf M}{dt}=\\gamma_p\\mathbf M\\!\\times\\!\\mathbf B-\\frac{M_x\\hat{\\mathbf x}+M_y\\hat{\\mathbf y}}{T_2}+\\frac{(M_0-M_z)\\hat{\\mathbf z}}{T_1}"},{"tex":"\\frac{d\\mathbf M}{dt}=\\gamma_p\\mathbf M\\!\\times\\!\\mathbf B-\\frac{M_x\\hat{\\mathbf x}+M_y\\hat{\\mathbf y}}{T_2}+\\frac{(M_0-M_z)\\hat{\\mathbf z}}{T_1}"},{"tex":"\\frac{d\\mathbf M}{dt}=\\gamma_p\\mathbf M\\!\\times\\!\\mathbf B-\\frac{M_x\\hat{\\mathbf x}+M_y\\hat{\\mathbf y}}{T_2}+\\frac{(M_0-M_z)\\hat{\\mathbf z}}{T_1}"}],"overlay":{"left":"4%","top":"1.8%","width":"92%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"M_+(0)=M_x(0)+iM_y(0)"},{"tex":"M_+(t)=M_+(0)e^{-t/T_2}e^{-i\\omega_0t}"},{"tex":"S_{\\mathrm{FID}}(t)\\simeq S(0)e^{-t/T_2^*},\\qquad \\frac1{T_2^*}\\simeq\\frac1{T_2}+\\frac1{T_2'}"},{"tex":"M_z(t)=M_0+[M_z(0)-M_0]e^{-t/T_1}"}],"overlay":{"left":"5%","bottom":"4.5%","width":"90%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}}],"source":"[@bloch1946]","seconds":85,"notes":"Immediately after RF, the transverse ensemble is phase coherent. The Bloch equation is now a phenomenological open-system equation for the macroscopic magnetisation; it is not the unitary equation of one isolated spin. All three processes shown in it occur simultaneously. The clicks separate them only so that I can name them. The slow precession and repeated excitation flash are presentation-time rescalings.\n\nClick once. T2 describes irreversible loss of transverse coherence from microscopic interactions. In the simplest Bloch model, the transverse ensemble magnetisation decays exponentially while precessing at omega0. The cyan curve is that envelope. The vectors do not shrink because proton bodies lose size; the ensemble expectation loses phase coherence.\n\nClick again. The measured free-induction decay is usually faster because static B0 inhomogeneity and susceptibility create a distribution of resonance frequencies. The phasors fan out while the intrinsic T2 envelope remains. I call the resulting observed time T2-star. The reciprocal-rate addition shown here is only the common independent-exponential or Lorentzian approximation; it is not universal for arbitrary off-resonance distributions, and static dephasing can be partly refocused.\n\nClick again. At the same time, longitudinal magnetisation returns toward M0 with time constant T1. The orange recovery curve and rising longitudinal component arrive without removing the transverse curves. TR, used on the next slide, means the time from one excitation to the next. How much longitudinal magnetisation has recovered by that next excitation determines how much new transverse magnetisation the sequence can create.\n\n[Sources]\n- Bloch F. Nuclear Induction. Physical Review. 1946;70:460–474: phenomenological equations for precession, longitudinal recovery and transverse relaxation.","order":17},{"omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":["field"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"field":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"qftGradientEncoding","at":"field","args":{"revealSteps":3,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":20,"dpr":1}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"G(t)=0:\\qquad \\omega(\\mathbf r)\\simeq\\omega_0=\\gamma_pB_0"},{"tex":"B_z(\\mathbf r,t)=B_0+\\Delta B_0(\\mathbf r)+\\mathbf G(t)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf r"},{"tex":"B_z(\\mathbf r,t)=B_0+\\Delta B_0(\\mathbf r)+\\mathbf G(t)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf r"},{"tex":"B_z(\\mathbf r,t)=B_0+\\Delta B_0(\\mathbf r)+\\mathbf G(t)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf r"}],"overlay":{"left":"5%","top":"1.8%","width":"90%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"field","states":[{"tex":"s_c(t)\\propto\\int_\\Omega C_c(\\mathbf r)m_\\perp(\\mathbf r,t)\\,d^3r"},{"tex":"\\omega(\\mathbf r,t)=\\gamma_p B_z(\\mathbf r,t)"},{"tex":"\\mathbf k(t)=\\frac{\\gamma_p}{2\\pi}\\int_{t_0}^{t}\\mathbf G(\\tau)\\,d\\tau,\\qquad m_\\perp(\\mathbf r,t)=m_\\perp(\\mathbf r,0)e^{-i2\\pi\\mathbf k(t)\\cdot\\mathbf r}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}s_c(t)&=\\kappa\\!\\int_\\Omega C_c(\\mathbf r)m_\\perp(\\mathbf r,0)\\,\\exp\\!\\big[-R_2^*(\\mathbf r)t-i\\Delta\\omega(\\mathbf r)t\\big]\\,\\\\ &\\hspace{4.2em}\\times\\exp\\!\\big[-i2\\pi\\mathbf k(t)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf r\\big]\\,d^3r\\end{aligned}"}],"overlay":{"left":"3%","bottom":"4.0%","width":"94%","z":3,"pointerEvents":false}}],"source":"[@lauterbur1973; @twieg1983; @hoult1976; @gruber2018]","seconds":80,"notes":"Without an applied gradient, all positions in an ideal homogeneous sample precess at essentially the same Larmor frequency. A receive channel therefore records one complex sum over the coherent transverse magnetisation, weighted by its complex coil sensitivity C_c. I define C_c to absorb the reciprocity/conjugation convention. The receiver does not know where in the sample a contribution arose.\n\nClick once. A linear gradient makes the z-component of the magnetic field depend on position. Therefore the local angular frequency depends on position. The cyan field arrows differ in height across the physical sample; they are not another set of spin vectors. Delta B0 represents static off-resonance. Gradients do not create signal. They write position into phase and frequency.\n\nClick again. Integrating the gradient history gives the k-space coordinate. I use k in cycles per metre, so k equals gamma over two pi times the gradient integral and the Fourier phase is exp minus i two pi k dot r. The phasors fan continuously across the sample while the violet point moves through k-space. With a different radians-per-metre convention the two pi would move into k; I do not mix the conventions. One gradient sample is not an image—a trajectory through k-space supplies Fourier samples from which spatial structure is reconstructed.\n\nClick again. The changing coherent transverse magnetisation produces an induced receive voltage. After demodulation, each channel measures this baseband integral: coil sensitivity times the pre-encoding transverse object, intrinsic and static dephasing, and the spatial Fourier phase. The exponential contains the encoding exactly once; M-perp at time zero here means the object before gradient phase is applied. The visible oscillation is slowed and the proportionality hides coil loading and receiver gains. This is what the scanner actually measures: voltage as a function of time, not proton position and not longitudinal Mz.\n\n[Sources]\n- Lauterbur PC. Nature. 1973;242:190–191: image formation by spatially varying magnetic fields.\n- Twieg DB. Medical Physics. 1983;10:610–621: k-trajectory formulation of the NMR imaging process.\n- Hoult DI, Richards RE. Journal of Magnetic Resonance. 1976;24:71–85: receive sensitivity and NMR signal.\n- Gruber B et al. Journal of Magnetic Resonance Imaging. 2018;48:590–604: RF transmit and receive coil conventions.","order":18},{"id":"from-contrast-to-quantitative-maps","omit":true,"template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"02","heading":"From contrast to quantitative maps","eyebrow":"input function · tissue response · voxelwise inference","seconds":7,"notes":"Chapter transition.","order":19},{"id":"study-i","template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"01","heading":"Study I","eyebrow":"p-Brain · from bolus to quantitative tissue maps","seconds":9,"notes":"Chapter transition. Introduce Study I first, then advance directly into the continuously rotating p-Brain microstructure and voxel sequence.","order":20},{"id":"study-i-bolus-to-voxel","subchapter":"p-Brain","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":["head","figure"],"rows":"auto 1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study I · Follow one bolus—from artery to tissue voxel.","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"voxelPerfusion","at":"figure","args":{"revealSteps":20,"aspect":2.3,"maxHeight":0.8,"voxel":[0.48,-0.18,0.36],"src":"decks/data/patient-parcels.json","h01":"decks/data/h01-cortex-slab.json","vesselAtlas":"decks/data/brainproject-vessels.json","pericyteImage":"decks/assets/defense/pericyte-sem.png","dceVideo":"decks/assets/defense/real-dce-dual.mp4","dcePoster":"decks/assets/defense/real-dce-dual-poster.png","speakingPrompts":{"until":7,"hiddenSteps":[6],"bullets":[{"text":"MRI reconstructs the brain from signals produced by hydrogen nuclei.","highlights":["signals"],"from":0},{"text":"Radiofrequency pulses excite the nuclei; magnetic-field gradients encode position.","highlights":["Radiofrequency pulses","magnetic-field gradients"],"from":1},{"text":"Gadolinium changes how nearby water molecules respond to MRI, letting us follow its passage through the brain.","highlights":["Gadolinium","follow its passage"],"from":2}]},"introBulletSteps":2}}],"source":"[@tireli2026thesis; @shapsoncoe2024; @h01release2024; @brainproject2026]","seconds":160,"notes":"The first three states reveal the three MRI bullets independently: state 0 shows signals; click 1 adds excitation and spatial encoding; click 2 adds the gadolinium explanation. Throughout these states the same bare scanner brain rotates continuously. No artery or tracer appears until its own later click. Open on the unmarked, continuously rotating scanner brain. Click 3 reveals the connected arterial atlas without anatomical labels. Click 4 starts one small violet bolus at the arterial inlet. Click 5 introduces lightly weighted blue venous anatomy and continues the same packet into venous drainage. Click 6 places the real patient's axial DCE movie and its synchronized measured concentration trace side by side over the still-rotating brain; click 7 removes them. Click 8 selects the orange cortical voxel. Click 9 shows the whole H01 cortical slab with only the six presentation classes: excitatory neurons, inhibitory neurons, vasculature, astrocytes, microglia and oligodendrocytes. Other neuronal source labels remain merged into the neuronal rendering and are not separately named. Click 10 introduces the rotating neuronal microdomain. Click 11 adds the capillary. Click 12 temporarily lays the supplied scanning-electron micrograph over the still-visible capillary state with a soft shadow and no added words. Click 13 removes the micrograph and adds microglia; click 14 adds the oligodendrocyte; click 15 adds the astrocyte. The H01 vessel-associated object is absent from the slab, turntable videos, labels and complete state. All turntable streams remain live and share one rotation phase throughout the microscope sequence; reveal clicks perform no media seek or restart. No gadolinium particles appear during the cortical build. Click 16 introduces the synchronized vessel-only bolus passage and its wall-centred radial diffusion field on black. Click 17 restores the exact registered H01 cells behind the same continuing precomputed transport. Click 18 returns to the macro brain. Click 19 introduces the measured orange tissue-voxel C_t(t). Click 20 places the yellow sampling box on the superior, locally z-parallel segment of the atlas-labelled right internal carotid artery and adds C_a(t); the right-ICA path itself is highlighted so the selection is anatomically explicit without a floating brain-space label. Dots are measured p-Brain samples and lines are mild smoothing guides. This direct rICA curve teaches input timing; Study II's final models used the higher-SNR SSS concentration curve shifted to the rICA arrival reference as the effective input.\n\n[Sources]\n- Shapson-Coe et al. Science 2024;384:eadk4858. DOI: 10.1126/science.adk4858. H01 human cortex reconstruction.\n- H01 official released data, CC BY 4.0: cell bodies, neuron/glial meshes, vessel masks/surfaces and cortical-layer resources.\n- Armulik et al. Nature 2010;468:557–561: pericytes and BBB integrity.\n- Hartmann et al. Nature Neuroscience 2021;24:633–645: capillary pericytes and blood-flow resistance.\n- Mishra et al. Nature Neuroscience 2016;19:1619–1627 and Chever et al. Journal of Neuroscience 2010;30:15769–15777: astrocyte vascular signalling and potassium homeostasis.\n- Nimmerjahn et al. Science 2005;308:1314–1318 and Davalos et al. Nature Neuroscience 2005;8:752–758: microglial surveillance and injury response.\n- Lee et al. Nature 2012;487:443–448: oligodendroglial metabolic support of axons.\n- Brain Project / Z-Anatomy atlas, CC BY-SA 4.0, for source-derived intracranial arterial and venous geometry.","order":21,"sourceNotes":[{"url":"https://www.nibib.nih.gov/science-education/science-topics/magnetic-resonance-imaging-mri","note":"Introductory wording: RF excitation, position encoding by magnetic-field gradients, and gadolinium changing the response of nearby water protons. Relaxation-time terminology is introduced later."}]},{"id":"input-to-tissue-patlak","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["figure","equation"],"rows":"1fr auto","cellAlign":{"figure":"center","equation":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"kineticTransformCompositionStory","at":"figure","args":{"revealSteps":13,"aspect":2.62,"maxHeight":0.73,"src":"decks/data/patient-parcels.json","phaseSteps":[0,1,2,4,5,6,7,8,8,9,10,11,12,13]}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"equation","states":["C_t(t)=C_a(t)",{"tex":""},"C_{\\mathrm{transit}}(t)=C_a(t)*h_{\\mathrm{transit}}(t)",{"tex":""},{"tex":"\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{C_t(t)}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-unit}{C_a(t)}"},{"tex":"\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{C_t(t)}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-unit}{C_a(t)}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}"},{"tex":"\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-unit}{\\frac{C_a(t)}{C_a(t)}}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\n\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}&\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}\\\\[0.55em]\n\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference mm-linear-hidden}{Y}&\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference mm-linear-hidden}{=\\htmlClass{mm-reference-c}{c}+\\htmlClass{mm-reference-m}{m}X\n}\\end{aligned}","cancelMatchingInput":{"tex":"\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-unit}{\\frac{\\htmlClass{mm-cancel}{C_a(t)}}{\\htmlClass{mm-cancel}{C_a(t)}}}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}"}},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\n\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}&\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}\\\\[0.55em]\n\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{Y}&\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{=\\htmlClass{mm-reference-c}{c}+\\htmlClass{mm-reference-m}{m}X\n}\\end{aligned}","fadeIn":".mm-linear-reference"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\n\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}&\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}\\\\[0.55em]\n\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{Y}&\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{=\\htmlClass{mm-reference-c}{c}+\\htmlClass{mm-reference-m}{m}X\n}\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\n\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}&\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}\\\\[0.55em]\n\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{Y}&\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{=\\htmlClass{mm-reference-c}{c}+\\htmlClass{mm-reference-m}{m}X\n}\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\n\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}&\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}\\\\[0.55em]\n\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{Y}&\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{=\\htmlClass{mm-reference-c}{c}+\\htmlClass{mm-reference-m}{m}X\n}\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\n\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}&\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{\\varepsilon_1}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}\\\\[0.55em]\n\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{Y}&\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{=\\htmlClass{mm-reference-c}{c}+\\htmlClass{mm-reference-m}{m}X\n}\\end{aligned}"},{"tex":"\\begin{aligned}\n\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-lhs}{\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}}&\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-equals}{=}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e1}{v_{\\mathrm B}}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-plus}{+}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-e2}{K_i}\\htmlClass{mm-term mm-key-x}{\\frac{\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}\\\\[0.55em]\n\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{Y}&\\htmlClass{mm-linear-reference}{=\\htmlClass{mm-reference-c}{v_{\\mathrm B}}+\\htmlClass{mm-reference-m}{K_i}X\n}\\end{aligned}"}],"stateTransition":"none"}],"source":"[@patlak1983; @tireli2026thesis]","seconds":155,"notes":"Begin with the counterfactual C_t(t)=C_a(t). Click 1 replaces the single conduit with a thick arterial trunk branching into three smaller paths; the tissue curve makes its complete reduction to the dispersed, vascular-fraction-scaled shape as the branches appear. Click 2 names the illustrative convolution. Click 3 goes directly to tracer accumulation, completing the intuitive orange tissue curve with a slowly accumulating remainder. Smaller calibre alone does not broaden the curve.\n\nThe orange tissue curve stays fixed from click 3 onward. Click 4 overlays its lower, broadened yellow rapidly reversible component; the equation appears immediately and the word instantaneous follows one second later. Click 5 adds the visibly rising purple accumulated component. Yellow plus purple compose the orange C_t point by point. In this teaching schematic, C_a in the algebra denotes the effective tissue-facing input after the transit operation.\n\nClick 6 divides every term by C_a(t), wherever C_a(t) is nonzero. Hold the divided equation to explain it. Click 7 draws yellow cancellation strokes through the matching numerator and denominator C_a(t) factors, removes them, and leaves the complete normalized concentration-ratio equation. Click 8 fades in Y=c+mX beneath it, keeping the original expression fixed and the equals signs aligned. Click 9 introduces the Patlak plot and distinguishes late samples. Click 10 fits the late regime. Click 11 constructs the slope epsilon_2. Click 12 extrapolates the fit with a yellow dashed line to expose the intercept epsilon_1. Click 13 names the fitted parameters K_i and v_B. Both equation rows remain visible throughout the plot build.\n\nThis remains a teaching schematic, not a selected participant's measured tissue curve or a production p-Brain fit. The actual analysis used the higher-SNR SSS concentration curve shifted to the rICA arrival reference as its effective input. p-Brain did not fit the illustrative transit kernel used in the opening sequence. The left effective-input schematic retains its peak height through display normalization only; the tissue decomposition and numerical fit are unchanged.","order":22},{"omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"center","layout":["head","story","equation"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","cellAlign":{"story":"center","equation":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What does tissue do to the bolus?","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"patlakStory","at":"story","args":{"revealSteps":6,"aspect":2.75,"maxHeight":0.5,"seconds":4.8}},{"type":"math-sequence","at":"equation","states":["\\underbrace{C_t(t)\\equiv C_a(t)}_{\\text{identity: no tissue effect}}","C_t(t)=\\underbrace{v_{\\mathrm B}^{\\mathrm{app}}C_a(t)}_{\\text{rapidly reversible pool}}","C_t(t)=\\underbrace{v_{\\mathrm B}^{\\mathrm{app}}C_a(t)}_{\\text{reversible}}+\\underbrace{K_i\\int_0^tC_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}_{\\text{effectively irreversible accumulation}}","C_t(t)=C_a(t)\\left[\\underbrace{v_{\\mathrm B}^{\\mathrm{app}}}_{\\text{reversible pool}}+\\underbrace{K_i\\frac{\\int_0^tC_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}_{\\text{accumulated}}\\right]","\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_a(t)}=v_{\\mathrm B}^{\\mathrm{app}}+K_i\\frac{\\int_0^tC_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}","\\underbrace{\\frac{C_t}{C_a}}_{y}=\\underbrace{v_{\\mathrm B}^{\\mathrm{app}}}_{\\text{intercept}}+\\underbrace{K_i}_{\\text{slope}}\\underbrace{\\frac{\\int_0^tC_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau}{C_a(t)}}_{x}","\\boxed{y=v_{\\mathrm B}^{\\mathrm{app}}+K_i x}\\qquad\\text{fit only the linear regime}"]}],"source":"[@patlak1983; @tireli2026thesis]","seconds":135,"notes":"Click 1: if tissue did nothing, output would copy input. Click 2: the rapidly reversible compartments contribute an apparent distribution-volume intercept. Click 3: add an effectively irreversible accumulated compartment; what distinguishes it is the persistent late tail, not merely a taller peak. Click 4: factor out C_a(t). Click 5: divide both sides by C_a(t). Click 6: name y, intercept, slope and x. Final click: only now reveal the Patlak plot and fit the regime that has become linear. Patlak linearity begins only after the rapidly reversible compartments are in quasi-equilibrium with plasma while tracer in the accumulated compartment remains effectively irreversible over the fitted window. It is incorrect to argue that a low late plasma concentration itself suppresses back-diffusion. The intercept is an apparent rapidly reversible distribution volume; p-Brain reports it operationally as v_B. K_i is a net unidirectional influx constant and approaches permeability–surface-area product only in the low-extraction, permeability-limited regime. The current implementation fits the upper two thirds of the x-range; the assumptions and failure modes are shown in backup.","order":23},{"subchapter":"Voxelwise inference","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". . ."],"cols":"3vw 1fr 3vw","rows":"4vh auto 1fr 4vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"One voxel gives one fit. What if we repeat it everywhere?","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"voxelInferenceSweep","at":"art","args":{"revealSteps":1,"aspect":2.24,"maxHeight":0.75,"fps":10,"dpr":1,"src":"decks/data/patient-parcels.json"}}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":35,"notes":"Begin with the same continuous high-detail anatomy and one fitted voxel. The yellow arterial input remains fixed; the orange tissue response and fitted values belong to the displayed sampling location under the cursor. Click once: superior-to-inferior slices descend through the brain while each slice is traversed in an alternating EPI-style raster. The tissue curve changes in synchrony with that cursor, and each orange mark records one completed display sample. The 1,574 marks are a stride-sampled visualisation of the patient surface, not every native image voxel and not a claim that the brain is literally made of blocks. Their curves are generated from the measured base concentration curve plus parcel-level fitted parameters, timing offsets and small display jitter; they illustrate applying the same input-to-tissue model across the brain rather than reproducing every production voxel fit.","order":24},{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":[". . .",". head .","art art art",". . ."],"cols":"7vw 1fr 7vw","rows":"7vh auto 1fr 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"One acquisition. Two Patlak maps.","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"voxelMaps","at":"art","args":{"revealSteps":2,"aspect":2.25,"maxHeight":0.8,"fps":10,"dpr":1,"deconvolution":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-output-maps-voxel.png"}}],"seconds":45,"notes":"Begin with one large bivariate map: coral carries K_i and cyan carries v_B. Click once: the same anatomy separates into the Patlak slope K_i map and intercept v_B map. Click again: the three deconvolution outputs from the same acquisition—CBF, CBV and MTT—fade over the Patlak maps as the upper three rows of the original p-Brain output figure. These are actual p-Brain outputs, not synthetic example values.","order":25},{"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":[". . . .",". mark github .",". . . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 1fr 4vw","rows":"4vh 1fr 4vh","cellAlign":{"mark":"center","github":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-header.gif","alt":"Animated p-Brain identity","at":"mark"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-github-page.png","alt":"p-Brain documentation and software page","at":"github"}],"seconds":15,"notes":"p-Brain identity and GitHub documentation side by side. Continue directly to the modular framework. Detailed file-loading and method-setup workflow is available in Paper 1 backup.","id":"slide-16","order":26},{"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":760,"layout":[". . . . .",". head head head .",". voxelLabel . parcelLabel .",". voxel . parcel .",". . . . ."],"cols":"5vw 1fr 3vw 1fr 5vw","rows":"2vh auto auto 1fr 10vh","cellAlign":{"voxel":"center","parcel":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"p-Brain outputs","at":"head"},{"type":"body","text":"Voxelwise maps","at":"voxelLabel"},{"type":"body","text":"Segmented anatomical summaries","at":"parcelLabel"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-output-maps-voxel.png","alt":"Voxelwise CBF, CBV, MTT, Ki and vB maps from one acquisition","at":"voxel"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-output-maps-parcel.png","alt":"The same outputs summarised within SynthSeg anatomical parcels","at":"parcel"}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":40,"notes":"Compare the original p-Brain figures side by side. The left shows voxelwise outputs; the right summarises the same output quantities on the SynthSeg parcellation. Keep all five rows visible: CBF, CBV and MTT from deconvolution, followed by Ki and vB from Patlak. The figures illustrate spatially resolved maps and region-level summaries from the same acquisition. Next, show the optional diffusion track: tractography and structural connectivity.","id":"voxelwise-p-brain-outputs","order":27},{"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":760,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". . ."],"cols":"8vw 1fr 8vw","rows":"3vh auto 1fr 3vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"The same outputs, summarized by anatomy","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-output-maps-parcel.png","alt":"Original p-Brain figure showing the same CBF, CBV, MTT, Ki and vB maps summarized on SynthSeg parcels","at":"art","maxHeight":0.82}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":24,"notes":"Keep the five rows and acquisition fixed while changing only the spatial summary: the voxelwise outputs are projected onto the SynthSeg parcellation for region-level analysis.","omit":true,"order":28},{"id":"repcon-cohort","omit":true,"template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"03","heading":"The REPCon cohort","eyebrow":"trial design · cohort formation · baseline phenotype","seconds":8,"notes":"Chapter transition.","order":29},{"id":"repcon-trial-design","subchapter":"REPCon cohort · trial design","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":520,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"repconBaselineFrame","at":"figure","args":{"revealSteps":12,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":24,"dpr":1,"eligibilitySteps":true,"hideShuffleCaptions":true}}],"source":"[@larsson2026repcon]","seconds":55,"notes":"Click 1 shows inclusion criteria, fading in one row at a time: Age 18–70 years; At least one mTBI; Medical documentation according to WHO classification ICD-10; Post-concussion symptoms for more than 4 weeks after trauma; Proficient in Danish. Click 2 keeps inclusion visible and reveals exclusion criteria in the same way: Moderate or severe TBI; Unavailable for 12 consecutive weeks; Contraindication for MRI, including MRI contrast agents; Medical conditions that can obscure post-concussion symptoms; Cardiovascular diseases prohibiting planned physical exercise; Professional or elite athletes. Both eligibility views wait for a presenter click. Click 3 hides the table and begins the original testing animation; all later original states are shifted by two. The criteria reproduce the user-supplied REPCon table with only light wording cleanup.\n\nBegin with all 70 adults with PPCS as one visible cohort; there is no timeline yet. Click 3 introduces a single line labelled Testing and a person-sized checkpoint. Participants leave the intake pool one at a time, pass the clipboard check and scanner without overlapping, and fill the tested pool dynamically. The first five are intentionally slow enough to follow; the remaining 65 accelerate. If the sequence is allowed to finish, it automatically settles into the complete tested cohort; clicking early skips directly there. Click again to begin randomisation: four interleaved spirals form without a drawn spiral underneath and undergo many rapid internal exchanges. That animation automatically continues into the playing-card/block shuffle and then into the two assigned groups. The next presenter click starts the 12-week interval at week 1. Movement is slow at weeks 1 and 12 and faster in the middle; alternating sun and moon marks show passing days. The bicycle has no rider, its wheels rotate in the direction of travel, and the usual-activity vignette contains walking and correctly oriented sitting. The training arm completed two supervised individualized cycling sessions per week at 70–80% of the BCBT symptom-threshold heart rate; the usual-care arm continued ordinary life at the same activity level. At week 12, the sequence automatically advances into repeat testing and settles when all 70 have crossed. One more click rewinds the complete visual history—testing, weeks, group assignment, shuffle and randomisation—back to the original 70-person baseline. Click once more without leaving the slide: the ten people who did not attend baseline fade to grey in their existing positions, leaving the 60 attendees visible without an orientation jump. The walking, cycling and clock motion are narrative cues, not recorded activity traces. Studies II and III pool the randomized arms and analyse only pre-intervention baseline measurements as cross-sectional PPCS-versus-external-control comparisons. The healthy controls were imaging comparators, not the randomized trial control arm.\n\n[Source]\n- Larsson BW et al. JMIR Research Protocols. 2026;15:e93795. NCT05785000.","order":30},{"id":"repcon-attendance","omit":true,"subchapter":"Baseline attendance","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"cohortAttendance","at":"figure","args":{"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":18,"dpr":1}}],"seconds":20,"notes":"This is the first new state after the trial-design rewind, not a second 70-enrolled slide. The same 70 positions remain in place: 60 attended baseline and ten are grey because they did not attend. Non-attendance is not a data/QC exclusion, so these ten receive no lightning symbol.\n\n[Source]\n- Tireli AV. PhD thesis. 2026. REPCon participant flow.","order":31},{"id":"baseline-ppcs-profile","order":113,"subchapter":"Cohort","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":480,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"cohortProfile","at":"figure","args":{"src":"decks/data/repcon-demographics.json","revealSteps":7,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":18,"dpr":1,"shadow":true}}],"source":"[@tireli2026thesis]","seconds":45,"notes":"Begin with age as a single enlarged distribution. Click 1 moves age to the upper left. Click 2 introduces recorded sex enlarged; click 3 moves it to the upper right. Click 4 introduces time since the latest mTBI enlarged; click 5 moves it to the lower left. Click 6 introduces the number of documented mTBIs enlarged; click 7 settles it into the lower right, completing the 2×2 view. All four views use the same 60 PPCS participants who attended baseline MRI; denominators are not mixed across panels. Age was 40.25±11.29 years, median 39, range 23–66. Recorded sex was 48 female and 12 male. Time from the latest documented mTBI to baseline had mean 37.3 months (2240 months / 60 attendees), median 27.5 months, IQR 14–45.5 and range 3–216; the displayed density uses a labelled logarithmic x-axis because of the long right tail. Number of documented mTBIs had median 1, range 1–5: 45 reported one, six reported two, seven reported three, one reported four and one reported five. Say sex rather than gender because the source variable is binary F/M.\n\n[Sources]\n- REPCon baseline clinical export joined to the canonical Study II attendance/QC cohort; de-identified values only in the deck export.\n- Tireli AV. PhD thesis. 2026. Methods: REPCon cohort.","backup":true},{"id":"rivermead-questionnaire","order":114,"subchapter":"Cohort","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":480,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"rpqStory","at":"figure","args":{"src":"decks/data/repcon-demographics.json","revealSteps":3,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":18,"dpr":1,"shadow":true}}],"source":"[@king1995rpq; @eyres2005rpq]","seconds":70,"notes":"The opening state contains only the exact prompt and 0–4 response scale. Click 1: the three RPQ-3 items trickle down in yellow. Click 2: the 13 RPQ-13 items trickle down beside them. Click 3: the questions fade and the earlier people-based score distribution returns. Every glyph is one of the 60 baseline MRI attendees; four-point bins create readable stacks. Mean 27.7, median 27.5, IQR 19–37, range 4–52. Data caveat for questions only: this study-recorded variable was reconstructed as the recorded items 1–3 aggregate plus items 4–13 aggregate plus items 14, 15 and 16. The available CSV and workbook retain response 1 in the aggregates, and individual responses for items 1–13 were not present in the located exports, so a correctly recoded standard total cannot be reconstructed. Do not call the displayed variable a conventionally rescored RPQ total if asked. The current Study II manuscript export is additionally truncated at item 13; this slide does not use that variable.\n\n[Sources]\n- King NS et al. Journal of Neurology. 1995;242:587–592.\n- Eyres S et al. Clinical Rehabilitation. 2005;19:878–887.\n- REPCon fys_data_1-70.csv and EX1 clinical export; same 60 MRI-attendee IDs as the preceding slide.","backup":true},{"id":"study-ii-iii-cohorts","order":111,"subchapter":"Cohort","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"cohortPeople","at":"figure","args":{"revealSteps":5,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":18,"dpr":1,"qcImage":"decks/assets/defense/cohort/dce-qc-tissue-curve-single.jpg","qcImageSecond":"decks/assets/defense/cohort/dce-qc-tissue-curves.png"}}],"seconds":85,"notes":"Begin with the complete pre-QC acquisition/session sets—Study II 60 and Study III 52—and the complete control sets—Study II 21 and Study III 47. Click 1 retains every position but greys data/QC exclusions and adds a small red lightning symbol on the participant's chest: 14 PPCS from Study II, leaving 46; seven PPCS from Study III, leaving 45; and six Study II controls, leaving 15. The six controls comprised four failed input/tissue/Patlak review and two extreme calculated-value exclusions, so do not call all six signal failures. The final PPCS cohorts comprise 36 in both studies, 10 only in Study II, nine only in Study III and 15 in neither. Click 2 overlays the supplied single tissue-concentration curve. Click 3 removes it and returns to the unchanged cohort. Click 4 shows the supplied four-part tissue-concentration-curve QC image. Click 5 removes it and returns to the cohort. The following click continues to the Study II analysis pipeline. There are no arrows, flow nodes or disappearing people.\n\n[Sources]\n- Tireli AV. PhD thesis. 2026. Studies II and III participant flow and quality-control methods.\n- Two user-supplied p-Brain DCE tissue-concentration QC screenshots, 1 September 2026.","backup":true},{"id":"imaging-comparison-cohorts","order":112,"subchapter":"Cohort","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":480,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"studyDemographics","at":"figure","args":{"src":"decks/data/repcon-demographics.json","revealSteps":2,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":18,"dpr":1,"shadow":true}}],"source":"[@tireli2026; @tireli2026oxygen]","seconds":55,"notes":"Backup comparison of the two analytic cohorts. Open on the page title only. Click 1 shows Study II only. Click 2 adds Study III without changing the first panel. The age curves are kernel-density estimates of the actual individual ages, with each age retained as a rug mark. Recorded sex is categorical and is therefore shown as two non-stereotyped coloured proportions in a split bar, not smoothed and not encoded with person icons. Study II: 46 PPCS, age 40.2±11.7, 39F/7M; 15 healthy controls, age 54.3±9.6, 8F/7M. Study III: 45 PPCS, 35F/10M; age was available for 43 and was 41.4±10.8. Its 47 controls were age 34.2±13.2, 23F/24M. The opposite age imbalances are intentional to show rather than conceal: Study II controls were older, whereas Study III controls were younger. Both group models adjusted for age and sex, but adjustment does not make the cohorts matched. Use each study's own age values because age is recorded at its respective MRI visit.\n\n[Sources]\n- Study II canonical dataset.xlsx, tissue sheet, included_in_analysis=true.\n- Study III reviewer_table_data.xlsx and reproduce_tables.py paired normoxia/hypoxia selection.","backup":true},{"id":"philips-bridge","omit":true,"order":30.6,"subchapter":"Study II","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":300,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"philipsFlash","at":"figure","args":{"logo":"decks/assets/defense/cohort/philips-logo.png","aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":18,"dpr":1}}],"seconds":7,"notes":"The supplied Philips mark appears alone, small and transparent against the page, then fades away. It is a visual bridge from the MRI cohorts into Study II; advance after the mark has vanished.\n\n[Source]\n- User-supplied Philips logo image, 2 September 2026."},{"id":"study-ii","template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"02","heading":"Study II","eyebrow":"46 PPCS · 15 healthy controls","seconds":10,"notes":"Chapter transition. Study II analytic sample: 46 PPCS and 15 healthy controls.","order":29},{"id":"study-ii-pbrain-cohort","subchapter":"Study II · from cohort to maps","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"studyIIPbrainPipeline","at":"figure","args":{"src":"decks/data/patient-parcels.json","mark":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-header.gif","revealSteps":1,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"fps":12,"dpr":1}}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain; @tireli2026]","seconds":30,"notes":"Begin with the complete Study II analytic cohort—46 PPCS and 15 controls—feeding the same continuously animated p-Brain implementation. Click once: the output resolves into well-separated, upright K_i and v_B Patlak maps. K_i is the fitted slope and represents net unidirectional contrast exchange over the fitted window. v_B is the fitted intercept and is reported operationally as tracer-accessible vascular space. The maps retain the patient-anatomy visual language introduced in Study I; do not call them 'two maps per participant' or claim that every participant had an identical model outcome.","order":38},{"subchapter":"Paper 1","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["head head","charts brain"],"cols":"0.88fr 1.12fr","rows":"auto 1fr","cellAlign":{"charts":"center","brain":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Seven tissues. Two spatial signatures.","at":"head"},{"type":"chart","kind":"box","src":"decks/data/bbb-tissue.json","where":{"metric":"Ki"},"ylabel":"K_i (adjusted) · mL/100 g/min","aspect":2.15,"reveal":"panels","focus":true,"lines":"white","at":"charts"},{"type":"chart","kind":"box","src":"decks/data/bbb-tissue.json","where":{"metric":"vp"},"ylabel":"v_B (adjusted) · mL/100 g","aspect":2.15,"reveal":"panels","focus":true,"lines":"white","at":"charts"},{"type":"scene","scene":"brain3d","at":"brain","args":{"render":"wire","yaw":112,"tilt":12,"spin":0.02,"scale":0.72,"center":[0.31,0.49],"dot":1.18,"label":false,"contextOpacity":0.3,"revealSteps":6,"highlightsByStep":["cortex","subcortex","brainstem","cerebellum","wm","cerebellum_wm",["cortex","wm"]],"sliceByStep":[{"mode":"cortex","center":[0.83,0.49],"scale":1,"orthogonal":true},{"mode":"subcortex","center":[0.83,0.49],"scale":1,"orthogonal":true},{"mode":"brainstem","center":[0.83,0.49],"scale":1,"orthogonal":true},{"mode":"cerebellum","center":[0.83,0.49],"scale":1,"orthogonal":true},{"mode":"wm","center":[0.83,0.49],"scale":1,"orthogonal":true},{"mode":"cerebellum_wm","center":[0.83,0.49],"scale":1,"orthogonal":true},{"mode":"interface","color":"#D97757","center":[0.83,0.49],"scale":1,"orthogonal":true}],"maxHeight":0.72}}],"source":"[@tireli2026thesis]","seconds":110,"notes":"Advance through all seven pre-specified tissue classes. Both age- and sex-adjusted charts move with the same anatomy on every click: K_i is higher in cortical GM and the GM/WM interface after FDR (q=.020 for each), with cerebral white matter just above the threshold (q=.052); v_B is lower in all seven classes and all seven survive FDR. For each tissue, the shared anatomy shows the same subject's high-resolution T1-weighted axial, coronal and sagittal sections at that mask's largest cross-section. The plates are pre-oriented before export and receive no second browser-side rotation. The subcortical sagittal overlay uses bilateral thalamus, caudate, putamen and pallidum only. The anatomy is a spatial underlay; the charts carry the cohort inference.\nBackup grouping: filed under Paper 1 at the presenter’s request. The displayed quantitative results are from Study II.","order":109,"id":"seven-tissues-two-spatial-signatures","backup":true},{"subchapter":"Paper 1","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["head","brain"],"cols":"1fr","rows":"auto 1fr","cellAlign":{"brain":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What happens when we test every parcel?","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"parcelSweep","at":"brain","args":{"revealSteps":4,"aspect":2.02,"maxHeight":0.82,"src":"decks/data/patient-parcels.json","effects":"decks/data/study2-parcel-effects.json"}}],"source":"[@tireli2026thesis]","seconds":80,"notes":"Begin on one patient-specific 85-parcel DKT surface beside the participant's correctly oriented T1-weighted anatomy. Click 1: reproduce the original v_B audit parcel by parcel. The T1 slice moves to the active parcel; completed lower parcels remain dark blue and the two higher parcels coral while untouched anatomy keeps its original colour and opacity. The counter ends at 83 lower and 2 higher. Click 2: apply the within-metric FDR decision without changing anatomy—58 significant lower parcels stay blue and the remaining 27 become grey. Click 3: repeat the identical T1-linked directional sequence for K_i, ending at 56 higher and 29 lower. Click 4: apply FDR; all 85 become grey because none survive. Grey means statistically unresolved, not zero effect. The active parcel's q-value is shown during each sweep. The plates are pre-oriented before export and receive no second browser-side rotation. Say tracer-accessible blood volume, not density.\nBackup grouping: filed under Paper 1 at the presenter’s request. The displayed quantitative results are from Study II.","order":110,"id":"what-happens-when-we-test-every-parcel","backup":true},{"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". . ."],"cols":"7vw 1fr 7vw","rows":"5vh auto 1fr 9vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"The submitted tissue result.","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"assets/defense/study2-tissue-results.png","framed":true,"alt":"Study II tissue-level Kᵢ and v_b boxplots","at":"art"}],"source":"[@tireli2026]","seconds":40,"notes":"Use the submitted Study II figure as the audit trail after the native animation. The top row is K_i: cortex and the GM/WM interface survive FDR. The bottom row is v_b: all seven tissue classes are lower in PPCS. Values shown are age- and sex-adjusted partial residuals.","order":41,"id":"the-submitted-tissue-result"},{"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". . ."],"cols":"7vw 1fr 7vw","rows":"5vh auto 1fr 9vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"The parcel result preserves the two spatial signatures.","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"assets/defense/study2-parcel-results.png","framed":true,"alt":"Study II parcel-level PPCS minus healthy-control Kᵢ and v_b maps","at":"art"}],"source":"[@tireli2026]","seconds":40,"notes":"This is the submitted parcel projection. K_i differences are mixed and none survive parcelwise FDR. v_b is lower through nearly the whole parcellation: 83 of 85 lower and 58 survive FDR. The surface display includes interpolated display-only values for excluded cortical labels; inference uses only analysed parcels.","order":42,"id":"the-parcel-result-preserves-the-two-spatial-signatures"},{"id":"study-iii","template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"03","heading":"Study III","eyebrow":"45 PPCS · 47 healthy controls","seconds":10,"notes":"Study III overall analytic cohort: 45 PPCS and 47 healthy controls. Usable global CBF/CMRO₂: 41 PPCS at normoxia and 39 for reactivity, with 47 controls. Lactate: 38/17 at normoxia and 35/16 for reactivity. Counts vary by measurement and quality control; detailed participant flow is in Cohort backup.","order":43},{"id":"study-iii-capillary-oxygen","subchapter":"Measurement model","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["fig"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"fig":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"oxygenMicrodomain","at":"fig","args":{"revealSteps":2,"aspect":1.78,"maxHeight":0.91}}],"source":"[@shapsoncoe2024; @buxton1997oxygen; @gagnon2016oxygen]","seconds":75,"notes":"Use the vessel as the bridge from local exchange to the whole-brain Fick balance. State 0: show only the released H01 vessel geometry on black. Click 1: start one camera-locked intravascular oxygen passage while the precomputed three-dimensional tissue field appears outside the wall; radial arrival is delayed by d²/(4D), so oxygen farther from the vessel emerges later. Click 2: retain the moving passage and reveal two oriented three-dimensional sampling volumes. Each box was fitted in specimen coordinates and fully encloses a short local section of the same released vessel surface; they are not screen-space cubes and their displayed dimensions are not acquisition voxel dimensions. The lower and upper boxes are labelled O2 in and O2 out. The displayed direction is illustrative because the released H01 vessel has no measured flow direction; do not call either end arterial or venous. The surrounding oxygen field is a literature-parameterized forward simulation on released geometry, not measured H01 pO2 and not subject-specific CMRO2. H01 also does not measure cell-specific metabolic demand.\n\n[Sources]\n- Buxton RB, Frank LR. J Cereb Blood Flow Metab. 1997;17:64–72. doi:10.1097/00004647-199701000-00009.\n- Gagnon L et al. Front Comput Neurosci. 2016;10:82. doi:10.3389/fncom.2016.00082.","order":44},{"id":"study-iii-fick-brain","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["fig"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"fig":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"oxygenBalance","at":"fig","args":{"revealSteps":5,"aspect":1.78,"maxHeight":0.91}}],"source":"[@tireli2026oxygen; @brainproject2026]","seconds":110,"notes":"Click 0: Begin with the brain alone. No arterial or venous vessels, acquisition labels, finger or equation terms are highlighted.\n\nClick 1: The rotating finger and clip-on pulse oximeter appear. Label the approximation SpO2 ≈ SaO2 and reveal only SaO2 in the equation. The anatomy has no highlighted vessels yet. The thumb, sensor and light path are schematic geometry, not a participant scan or a reconstruction of the exact scanner-compatible device.\n\nClick 2: Add the venous drainage tree in blue and emphasize the posterior superior sagittal sinus measurement ROI. Keep the finger visible. The equation now shows the arterial-minus-venous saturation difference in parentheses, without flow, haemoglobin or CMRO2 yet.\n\nClick 3: Highlight the two internal carotid arteries and basilar artery in yellow, with their phase-contrast inflow markers. Add CBF and [Hb] to the existing saturation difference. In the thesis, haemoglobin is expressed in oxygen-binding units and the small dissolved-oxygen contribution is neglected. The fixed haemoglobin value was 9.3 mmol/L.\n\nClick 4: Add only CMRO2 = to complete the Fick relation. The finger, veins and yellow arterial markers remain. The existing equation terms keep their positions.\n\nClick 5: On the next click, the same brain takes on the blue hypoxia wash and particles. Keep the finger, acquisition vessels, labels and completed Fick relation visible. The camera and layout stay the same. This is an illustrative experimental-state effect, not a local quantitative oxygen map. The following slide shows the achieved peripheral saturation trace.\n\nPulse Oximeter Basics. US FDA. https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/pulse-oximeter-basics. SpO2 is an indirect estimate of arterial saturation. Fick relation and acquisition details: thesis and Study III article, as cited on the slide.","order":45},{"subchapter":"Controlled hypoxia","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"wipe","layout":["head","fig","foot"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"hypoxiaTrace","at":"fig","args":{"aspect":1.82,"target":80,"maxHeight":0.72,"seconds":4.8}},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"peripheral saturation · cohort median · 25–75 and 10–90 percentile envelopes","at":"foot"}],"source":"[@tireli2026thesis]","seconds":60,"notes":"The axes and labels match the locked thesis figure: approximately −40 to +40 minutes, onset at zero, 80% target, and percentile envelopes. The achieved peripheral saturation was 82±3%. The dotted field is the cohort band, not a separate reference population.","id":"a-controlled-descent-not-a-binary-condition","order":46},{"subchapter":"Physiological results","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". . ."],"cols":"5vw 1fr 5vw","rows":"3vh auto 1fr 4vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Was the deficit delivery—or oxygen use?","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/study3-figure-3-thesis-final.webp","alt":"Thesis-final Study III figure showing global CBF, global CMRO2 and precuneus lactate at normoxia and during hypoxia","at":"art"}],"source":"[@tireli2026oxygen]","seconds":75,"notes":"Use the regenerated thesis-final full figure, not detached crops or the stale appended-paper labels. It is rebuilt from the actual Study III long-format observations and asserts the final-thesis subject-level ratio OLS/HC3 estimates. Row A: resting global CBF did not differ, beta −1.8 mL/100 g/min, p=.31, N=88; CBF reactivity also did not differ, beta +0.001, p=.99. Row B: resting global CMRO2 was lower by 16.7 μmol/100 g/min, about 12%, p=.004, N=88; CMRO2 reactivity differed, beta −1.28 per percentage-point fall in SaO2, p=.029. Row C: baseline precuneus lactate did not differ, beta +0.05 mmol/L, p=.18, N=55; lactate reactivity did not differ, beta −0.001, p=.89. Reactivity is each participant's hypoxia-minus-normoxia change divided by their own normoxia-minus-hypoxia SaO2 difference. Say preserved macrovascular CBF response—not intact neurovascular coupling—and do not infer mitochondrial dysfunction directly. The current article's later mixed-effects reanalysis is a different estimand; this defense figure intentionally follows the final thesis.","order":47},{"omit":true,"subchapter":"Lactate and exercise intolerance","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["head","voxel"],"cols":"1fr","rows":"auto 1fr","cellAlign":{"voxel":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Where was lactate measured?","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"lactateVoxelBrain","at":"voxel","args":{"aspect":2.08,"maxHeight":0.77,"fps":10,"dpr":1,"src":"decks/data/patient-parcel-surface.json","centre":[0.02649,-0.56973,0.31985],"halfSize":[0.21594,0.18509,0.18509],"angulation":[37.2627,5.6925,2.3114]}}],"source":"[@tireli2026oxygen]","seconds":60,"notes":"The rotating T1w-derived surface is an anatomical display underlay, not a 3-D reconstruction of the MRS example participant. The yellow box uses the measured centre, nominal 35 by 30 by 30 mm dimensions and recorded angulation of one de-identified example single-voxel 1H-MRS PRESS acquisition in the precuneus (TE 288 ms, TR 2000 ms), normalized into the common display frame. It is not a cohort-average mask. The complete source figure on the previous slide shows that baseline lactate did not differ, beta +0.05 mmol/L, p=.178, N=55, and its hypoxic response interaction also did not differ, p=.801. Lactate is regional, while CBF and CMRO2 are global.","order":48},{"id":"did-lower-oxygen-use-track-pulse-intolerance","subchapter":"Paper 3","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","layout":["head","art","foot"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Did lower oxygen use track exercise intolerance?","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/study3-figure-4-exercise-tolerance.png","framed":false,"alt":"Submitted Figure 4: global CMRO2 at normoxia and hypoxic CMRO2 ratio reactivity in exercise-tolerant and exercise-intolerant patients with PPCS. Normoxia P=.019, N=41; reactivity P=.689, N=39.","at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"exercise intolerance · symptoms limit the graded bike test","at":"foot"}],"source":"[@tireli2026oxygen]","seconds":50,"notes":"Replace the pulse-intolerance result with Figure 4 from the supplied 23 August 2026 JCBFM manuscript. Exercise intolerance is symptom-limited termination of the graded Buffalo Concussion Bike Test (BCBT): symptoms worsen enough to stop before the participant reaches their physical-fitness limit. This differs from failure to reach 90% of age-predicted maximum heart rate. Among PPCS patients, normoxic global CMRO2 was lower in the exercise-intolerant subgroup: age- and sex-adjusted OLS with HC3 robust SE, beta -19.7 micromol/100 g/min, 95% CI -36.1 to -3.3, P=.019, N=41 (16 tolerant; 25 intolerant). Hypoxic CMRO2 ratio reactivity did not differ: beta +0.31 micromol/100 g/min per percentage-point fall in SaO2, 95% CI -1.20 to +1.82, P=.69, N=39 (16 tolerant; 23 intolerant). These are secondary within-patient analyses without multiplicity correction; association does not establish causation or diagnose autonomic dysfunction. Reactivity here is the individual deltaCMRO2/deltaSaO2 ratio, as used for this secondary analysis in the current manuscript; it is not the main patient-versus-control mixed-effects interaction. Source: 02_Main_manuscript.docx, Physiological exercise testing, Exercise intolerance Results, Figure 4 legend; exact supplied 09_Figure_4.tif. Removed the old pulse phenotype icon field because its 32/45 count belongs to a different definition. The existing narration already describes the correct exercise-intolerance result; preserve this legacy explicit slide id to retain its narration cue.","order":118,"backup":true},{"template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"04","heading":"Conclusion","eyebrow":"","seconds":10,"notes":"Chapter transition.","order":50,"id":"conclusion"},{"id":"three-passes-one-brain","subchapter":"One injury or several?","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"Conclusion","title":"One injury or several?","sourceLabel":"","branches":[],"cumulative":true,"states":[{"quote":"chapter4-findings-0","quoteData":{"id":"chapter4-findings-0","paragraphs":[],"highlights":[["BBB influx was higher","marker"],["blood volume was reduced","marker"],["Global CMRO₂ was lower","marker"],["preserved resting global CBF","marker"],["separate processes","marker"],["a shared upstream driver","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"scientificStatus":"finding","say":"One injury or several? The measurements show different patterns. Do they require different explanations?"},{"quote":"chapter4-findings-1","quoteData":{"id":"chapter4-findings-1","paragraphs":["BBB influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface."],"highlights":[["BBB influx was higher","marker"],["blood volume was reduced","marker"],["Global CMRO₂ was lower","marker"],["preserved resting global CBF","marker"],["separate processes","marker"],["a shared upstream driver","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"scientificStatus":"finding","say":"BBB influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface."},{"quote":"chapter4-findings-2","quoteData":{"id":"chapter4-findings-2","paragraphs":["BBB influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface.","Tracer-accessible blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments."],"highlights":[["BBB influx was higher","marker"],["blood volume was reduced","marker"],["Global CMRO₂ was lower","marker"],["preserved resting global CBF","marker"],["separate processes","marker"],["a shared upstream driver","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"scientificStatus":"finding","say":"Tracer-accessible blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments."},{"quote":"chapter4-findings-3","quoteData":{"id":"chapter4-findings-3","paragraphs":["BBB influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface.","Tracer-accessible blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","Global CMRO₂ was lower at rest and fell during hypoxia, with preserved resting global CBF."],"highlights":[["BBB influx was higher","marker"],["blood volume was reduced","marker"],["Global CMRO₂ was lower","marker"],["preserved resting global CBF","marker"],["separate processes","marker"],["a shared upstream driver","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"scientificStatus":"finding","say":"Global CMRO₂ was lower at rest and fell during hypoxia, with preserved resting global CBF."},{"quote":"chapter4-findings-4","quoteData":{"id":"chapter4-findings-4","paragraphs":["BBB influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface.","Tracer-accessible blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","Global CMRO₂ was lower at rest and fell during hypoxia, with preserved resting global CBF.","Different patterns could reflect separate processes or a shared upstream driver."],"highlights":[["BBB influx was higher","marker"],["blood volume was reduced","marker"],["Global CMRO₂ was lower","marker"],["preserved resting global CBF","marker"],["separate processes","marker"],["a shared upstream driver","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"scientificStatus":"interpretation","say":"Different patterns could reflect separate processes or a shared upstream driver."},{"quote":"chapter4-findings-5","quoteData":{"id":"chapter4-findings-5","paragraphs":["BBB influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface.","Tracer-accessible blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","Global CMRO₂ was lower at rest and fell during hypoxia, with preserved resting global CBF.","Different patterns could reflect separate processes or a shared upstream driver.","We cannot resolve the mechanism or establish causality for the explanations that follow."],"highlights":[["BBB influx was higher","marker"],["blood volume was reduced","marker"],["Global CMRO₂ was lower","marker"],["preserved resting global CBF","marker"],["separate processes","marker"],["a shared upstream driver","marker"],["cannot resolve the mechanism","marker"],["establish causality","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"scientificStatus":"limitation","say":"We cannot resolve the mechanism or establish causality for the explanations that follow."}],"copyStyle":"conclusion-findings","dimPrevious":true}],"source":"[@tireli2026; @tireli2026oxygen]","seconds":49,"notes":"The final click explicitly introduces the following explanations as hypotheses: our measurements cannot resolve their mechanism or establish causality. Earlier findings dim as the current point appears.\n\nChapter 4, §4.1: distinct spatial patterns motivate separate processes versus a shared upstream driver; they do not establish a single mechanism. Study II and III have separately analysed samples and different controls. Ki is regional; CMRO₂ is global, so it has no comparable regional spatial map. Preserved global CBF means no significant adjusted resting group difference, not demonstrated equivalence or normal regional delivery.\n\nTwo cumulative main findings. On next, return to the map and ask what could connect them. The studies have separately analysed samples and different control groups; no within-person mediation or causal link was established.\n\nSo, what did we find? Brain blood volume accessible to the tracer was lower throughout the brain. The increase in BBB influx was more restricted, to cortical grey matter and the grey-white matter interface.\n\nWe also found lower global oxygen consumption at rest, and poorer maintenance during hypoxia, with preserved resting global blood flow. These were separately analysed samples with different control groups.\n\nSource review: [local source path omitted]\n\nDo not claim that these are the same cohort; analyse and present the studies as separate samples with different controls.\nUse BBB influx constant Ki for the supplied DCE manuscript; do not treat Ki, Ktrans, extracellular volume ve and plasma volume vp as interchangeable measurements.\nRegional Ki elevation survived correction at cortical grey matter and the grey–white interface; no individual Ki parcel survived FDR. Widespread lower vb is the stronger spatial finding.\nPreserved resting global CBF means no statistically significant adjusted group difference here, not equivalence or proof that regional/microvascular oxygen delivery is normal.\nLower CMRO₂ and its hypoxic response support altered physiology; they do not measure ATP production or prove a specific mitochondrial lesion.\nOne-voxel ¹H-MRS lactate concentration does not identify glycolytic flux, astrocyte–neuron lactate transfer, pyruvate oxidation, mitochondrial respiration or clearance.\nFewer perfused capillaries, altered recruitment/tone and transit/exchange mechanisms are hypotheses; tracer-accessible vb does not measure anatomical capillary number.\nFunctional shunting is a physiological model concept here, not an observed anatomical shunt or a patient-specific measurement.\nNguyen shows microglial sealing of vessels after repeated injury alongside astrocyte phagocytosis; it must not be reduced to microglia simply causing BBB leakage.\nGuglielmetti tests focal cortical-impact mice and PDH activity; PDH substrate entry into oxidation is not synonymous with direct respiratory-chain function.\nGard uses athletes and uncorrected cytokine comparisons; evidence is suggestive, not a direct measurement of inflammation in the supplied human studies.\nExercise intolerance is associated with resting CMRO₂ in uncorrected secondary analysis; the hypoxic response is not significantly different between exercise groups.\nCross-sectional associations do not establish causality, recovery trajectories, clinical diagnostic performance or treatment efficacy.","stepNotes":["One injury or several? The measurements show different patterns. Do they require different explanations?","BBB influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface.","Tracer-accessible blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","Global CMRO₂ was lower at rest and fell during hypoxia, with preserved resting global CBF.","Different patterns could reflect separate processes or a shared upstream driver.","We cannot resolve the mechanism or establish causality for the explanations that follow."],"order":51,"omit":false},{"id":"vascular-interpretation","subchapter":"Study II · interpretation","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"Study II","title":"BBB · discussion","layout":"topics","cumulative":true,"branches":[],"sourceLabel":"","states":[{"quote":"reading-ready","topics":[],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"say":""},{"quote":"blood-volume","topics":[{"label":"Blood volume","text":"Lower tracer-accessible blood volume may reflect altered vascular tone, recruitment or capillary density. It does not establish anatomical rarefaction."}],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Blood volume. Lower tracer-accessible blood volume may reflect altered vascular tone, recruitment or capillary density. It does not establish anatomical rarefaction."],"highlights":[["Blood volume","marker"]]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[{"id":"Yoen2021","author":"Yoen et al. · 2021","title":"BBB disruption in mTBI with post-concussion syndrome","population":"42 clinical PCS patients; 29 symptomatic controls; median 2 months after injury.","method":"DCE-MRI; Patlak and extended Tofts models.","finding":"Patlak cortical Ktrans was higher; plasma volume was lower in cerebellar white matter and brainstem.","caveat":"Retrospective; not all symptoms persisted ≥3 months; results depended on modelling; different distribution from our findings.","url":"https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 16","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 1–5, 8, 10–12; Table 1 and Fig. 4","fullTitle":"Blood-Brain Barrier Disruption in Mild Traumatic Brain Injury Patients with Post-Concussion Syndrome: Evaluation with Region-Based Quantification of Dynamic Contrast-Enhanced MR Imaging Parameters Using Automatic Whole-Brain Segmentation.","paperId":"Yoen2021","pmid":"32783413","displayPopulation":"Clinical PCS · median 2 months · DCE","displayFinding":"Higher cortical Ktrans; lower plasma volume in selected regions.","displayCaveat":"Different regional pattern and symptom duration."}],"say":"For the vascular findings, lower blood volume could reflect altered tone, recruitment or capillary density. The scan cannot distinguish those possibilities.","scientificStatus":"interpretation"},{"quote":"capillary-exchange","topics":[{"label":"Blood volume","text":"Lower tracer-accessible blood volume may reflect altered vascular tone, recruitment or capillary density. It does not establish anatomical rarefaction."},{"label":"Capillary exchange","text":"Preserved global CBF can coexist with less effective capillary exchange. Uneven transit times are one proposed explanation."}],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Blood volume. Lower tracer-accessible blood volume may reflect altered vascular tone, recruitment or capillary density. It does not establish anatomical rarefaction.","Capillary exchange. Preserved global CBF can coexist with less effective capillary exchange. Uneven transit times are one proposed explanation."],"highlights":[["Blood volume","marker"],["Capillary exchange","marker"]]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[{"id":"Yoen2021","author":"Yoen et al. · 2021","title":"BBB disruption in mTBI with post-concussion syndrome","population":"42 clinical PCS patients; 29 symptomatic controls; median 2 months after injury.","method":"DCE-MRI; Patlak and extended Tofts models.","finding":"Patlak cortical Ktrans was higher; plasma volume was lower in cerebellar white matter and brainstem.","caveat":"Retrospective; not all symptoms persisted ≥3 months; results depended on modelling; different distribution from our findings.","url":"https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 16","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 1–5, 8, 10–12; Table 1 and Fig. 4","fullTitle":"Blood-Brain Barrier Disruption in Mild Traumatic Brain Injury Patients with Post-Concussion Syndrome: Evaluation with Region-Based Quantification of Dynamic Contrast-Enhanced MR Imaging Parameters Using Automatic Whole-Brain Segmentation.","paperId":"Yoen2021","pmid":"32783413","displayPopulation":"Clinical PCS · median 2 months · DCE","displayFinding":"Higher cortical Ktrans; lower plasma volume in selected regions.","displayCaveat":"Different regional pattern and symptom duration."},{"id":"Ostergaard2014","author":"Østergaard et al. · 2014","title":"Capillary transit-time heterogeneity and flow–metabolism coupling after TBI","population":"Biophysical model informed by experimental and mainly acute/severe TBI observations.","method":"Oxygen-transport modelling and literature synthesis.","finding":"Uneven capillary transit can reduce oxygen availability despite maintained bulk flow; functional shunting is a model prediction.","caveat":"A mechanistic framework, not a demonstration of rarefaction, shunting or transit heterogeneity in our patients.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/jcbfm.2014.131","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 19","reviewBasis":"Local supplied-reference publisher PDF extracted and read; primary XML also checked","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 1, 4–9; oxygen-transport model, Fig. 3 and CTH predictions","sourceType":"theoretical model/review, not a patient cohort","fullTitle":"Capillary transit time heterogeneity and flow-metabolism coupling after traumatic brain injury.","paperId":"stergaard2014","pmid":"25052556","displayPopulation":"Biophysical model · TBI literature","displayFinding":"Uneven transit can limit oxygen extraction despite maintained flow.","displayCaveat":"Model prediction; not a measured shunt in PPCS.","localSourcePath":"[local source path omitted]"}],"say":"Global flow also does not tell us how effectively blood exchanges oxygen with tissue. Uneven capillary transit is one proposed explanation.","scientificStatus":"interpretation"},{"quote":"inflammation-repair","topics":[{"label":"Blood volume","text":"Lower tracer-accessible blood volume may reflect altered vascular tone, recruitment or capillary density. It does not establish anatomical rarefaction."},{"label":"Capillary exchange","text":"Preserved global CBF can coexist with less effective capillary exchange. Uneven transit times are one proposed explanation."},{"label":"Inflammation and repair","text":"Vascular–glial remodelling is another candidate. In mice, microglia repair leaks while engulfing detached astrocytes; the chronic human consequences remain uncertain."}],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Blood volume. Lower tracer-accessible blood volume may reflect altered vascular tone, recruitment or capillary density. It does not establish anatomical rarefaction.","Capillary exchange. Preserved global CBF can coexist with less effective capillary exchange. Uneven transit times are one proposed explanation.","Inflammation and repair. Vascular–glial remodelling is another candidate. In mice, microglia repair leaks while engulfing detached astrocytes; the chronic human consequences remain uncertain."],"highlights":[["Blood volume","marker"],["Capillary exchange","marker"],["Inflammation and repair","marker"]]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[{"id":"Yoen2021","author":"Yoen et al. · 2021","title":"BBB disruption in mTBI with post-concussion syndrome","population":"42 clinical PCS patients; 29 symptomatic controls; median 2 months after injury.","method":"DCE-MRI; Patlak and extended Tofts models.","finding":"Patlak cortical Ktrans was higher; plasma volume was lower in cerebellar white matter and brainstem.","caveat":"Retrospective; not all symptoms persisted ≥3 months; results depended on modelling; different distribution from our findings.","url":"https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 16","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 1–5, 8, 10–12; Table 1 and Fig. 4","fullTitle":"Blood-Brain Barrier Disruption in Mild Traumatic Brain Injury Patients with Post-Concussion Syndrome: Evaluation with Region-Based Quantification of Dynamic Contrast-Enhanced MR Imaging Parameters Using Automatic Whole-Brain Segmentation.","paperId":"Yoen2021","pmid":"32783413","displayPopulation":"Clinical PCS · median 2 months · DCE","displayFinding":"Higher cortical Ktrans; lower plasma volume in selected regions.","displayCaveat":"Different regional pattern and symptom duration."},{"id":"Ostergaard2014","author":"Østergaard et al. · 2014","title":"Capillary transit-time heterogeneity and flow–metabolism coupling after TBI","population":"Biophysical model informed by experimental and mainly acute/severe TBI observations.","method":"Oxygen-transport modelling and literature synthesis.","finding":"Uneven capillary transit can reduce oxygen availability despite maintained bulk flow; functional shunting is a model prediction.","caveat":"A mechanistic framework, not a demonstration of rarefaction, shunting or transit heterogeneity in our patients.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/jcbfm.2014.131","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 19","reviewBasis":"Local supplied-reference publisher PDF extracted and read; primary XML also checked","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 1, 4–9; oxygen-transport model, Fig. 3 and CTH predictions","sourceType":"theoretical model/review, not a patient cohort","fullTitle":"Capillary transit time heterogeneity and flow-metabolism coupling after traumatic brain injury.","paperId":"stergaard2014","pmid":"25052556","displayPopulation":"Biophysical model · TBI literature","displayFinding":"Uneven transit can limit oxygen extraction despite maintained flow.","displayCaveat":"Model prediction; not a measured shunt in PPCS.","localSourcePath":"[local source path omitted]"},{"id":"Nguyen2025","author":"Nguyen et al. · 2025","title":"Concussion and the immune response at the BBB","population":"Mice after single or repeated closed-skull concussion, examined over hours to days.","method":"Live microscopy, cell tracing and pathway perturbation.","finding":"Microglia sealed leaky vessels while engulfing detached astrocytes, particularly after repeated injury; leakage could fall as cellular architecture changed.","caveat":"Shows a repair–remodelling tradeoff in mice, not the cause of chronic human PPCS or lower CMRO₂.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2414316122","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 45; CMRO₂ ref. 28","reviewBasis":"Public primary XML full text extracted and read; PDF unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"Results/Figs. 2–5 and Discussion","fullTitle":"Imaging a concussion and the ensuing immune response at the blood-brain barrier.","paperId":"Nguyen2025","pmid":"40388609","displayPopulation":"Mice · single/repeated concussion","displayFinding":"Microglia sealed vessels while engulfing detached astrocytes.","displayCaveat":"Repair and remodelling; chronic human effects untested."}],"say":"Inflammation and repair may reshape the vascular-glial interface. In mice, microglia seal leaks while engulfing detached astrocytes. The chronic human consequences remain uncertain.","scientificStatus":"interpretation"}]}],"source":"[@tireli2026; @tireli2026oxygen]","seconds":75,"notes":"Blood volume: For the vascular findings, lower blood volume could reflect altered tone, recruitment or capillary density. The scan cannot distinguish those possibilities.\n\nCapillary exchange: Global flow also does not tell us how effectively blood exchanges oxygen with tissue. Uneven capillary transit is one proposed explanation.\n\nInflammation and repair: Inflammation and repair may reshape the vascular-glial interface. In mice, microglia seal leaks while engulfing detached astrocytes. The chronic human consequences remain uncertain.\n\nThe two studies use separately analysed samples and different controls. No within-person causal or mediation link is established. Preserved global CBF means no significant group difference, not demonstrated equivalence or normal capillary delivery. DCE Ki is gadolinium influx, not oxygen permeability. Tracer-accessible vb is not a direct measure of capillary radius or number. Discussion hypotheses are not measured mechanisms. Full source review: qa/defense-refinements-2026-09-08/conclusion-sources/access-ledger.md.\n\nYoen et al. · 2021: DCE-MRI; Patlak and extended Tofts models. Patlak cortical Ktrans was higher; plasma volume was lower in cerebellar white matter and brainstem. Retrospective; not all symptoms persisted ≥3 months; results depended on modelling; different distribution from our findings. https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016\nØstergaard et al. · 2014: Oxygen-transport modelling and literature synthesis. Uneven capillary transit can reduce oxygen availability despite maintained bulk flow; functional shunting is a model prediction. A mechanistic framework, not a demonstration of rarefaction, shunting or transit heterogeneity in our patients. https://doi.org/10.1038/jcbfm.2014.131\nNguyen et al. · 2025: Live microscopy, cell tracing and pathway perturbation. Microglia sealed leaky vessels while engulfing detached astrocytes, particularly after repeated injury; leakage could fall as cellular architecture changed. Shows a repair–remodelling tradeoff in mice, not the cause of chronic human PPCS or lower CMRO₂. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2414316122","stepNotes":["","For the vascular findings, lower blood volume could reflect altered tone, recruitment or capillary density. The scan cannot distinguish those possibilities.","Global flow also does not tell us how effectively blood exchanges oxygen with tissue. Uneven capillary transit is one proposed explanation.","Inflammation and repair may reshape the vascular-glial interface. In mice, microglia seal leaks while engulfing detached astrocytes. The chronic human consequences remain uncertain."],"order":42.4,"omit":true},{"id":"oxygen-reserve-interpretation","subchapter":"Study III · interpretation","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"order":47.4,"seconds":75,"notes":"Resting metabolism: A lower resting CMRO2 in isolation is ambiguous: it could reflect either reduced oxygen-use capacity or reduced demand.\n\nHypoxic response: Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions.\n\nMitochondrial metabolism: One candidate is impaired oxidative metabolism. In mice, microglia are linked to reduced pyruvate dehydrogenase activity. We did not measure mitochondrial function directly.\n\nLactate handling: Our lactate measurement cannot locate the bottleneck either. Concentration alone does not tell us about lactate transport, oxidation or the astrocyte-neuron shuttle.\n\nThe two studies use separately analysed samples and different controls. No within-person causal or mediation link is established. Preserved global CBF means no significant group difference, not demonstrated equivalence or normal capillary delivery. DCE Ki is gadolinium influx, not oxygen permeability. Tracer-accessible vb is not a direct measure of capillary radius or number. Discussion hypotheses are not measured mechanisms. Full source review: qa/defense-refinements-2026-09-08/conclusion-sources/access-ledger.md.\n\nKabasawa et al. · 2002: Oxygen-15 gas PET; regional CBF, OEF and CMRO₂. Regional CMRO₂ was below reference limits in many patients despite CBF within normal ranges; some regional values rose with cognitive improvement. English abstract only. Exact age range, civilian eligibility and detailed methods remain unverified; not a matched PPCS replication. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12607977/\nChampagne et al. · 2021: Dual-calibrated MRI; CMRO₂ analysis in regions selected for lower CBF. Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF; no OEF difference. Acute athletes, not PPCS. The CBF difference was nonsignificant after CO₂ adjustment; the reported CMRO₂ comparison was not CO₂-adjusted. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11682-019-00240-2\nGuglielmetti et al. · 2017: Hyperpolarised ¹³C-pyruvate MRSI, enzyme assays and CSF1R inhibition. Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented the day-7 rise in lactate-to-pyruvate signal and the fall in PDH activity. Experimental contusion, not chronic human concussion; PDH substrate entry is distinct from a direct respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-17758-4\n\nApproved demand/capacity interpretation: reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity can coexist. The hypoxic response shows poorer maintenance of CMRO2 under the challenge but does not independently measure neuronal demand or maximal mitochondrial capacity. A CBF increase does not establish maintained oxygen delivery when arterial oxygen content falls; global flow also cannot resolve local capillary exchange. Click 1 reproduces the manuscript sentence verbatim. Click 2 replaces it with the approved qualification; the mitochondrial and lactate topics follow on clicks 3 and 4.","stepNotes":["","A lower resting CMRO2 in isolation is ambiguous: it could reflect either reduced oxygen-use capacity or reduced demand.","Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions.","One candidate is impaired oxidative metabolism. In mice, microglia are linked to reduced pyruvate dehydrogenase activity. We did not measure mitochondrial function directly.","Our lactate measurement cannot locate the bottleneck either. Concentration alone does not tell us about lactate transport, oxidation or the astrocyte-neuron shuttle."],"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"Study III","title":"CMRO₂ · discussion","layout":"topics","cumulative":true,"branches":[],"sourceLabel":"","states":[{"quote":"reading-ready","topics":[],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"say":""},{"quote":"resting-metabolism","topics":[{"label":"Resting metabolism","text":"A lower resting CMRO2 in isolation is ambiguous: it could reflect either reduced oxygen-use capacity or reduced demand."}],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Resting metabolism. A lower resting CMRO2 in isolation is ambiguous: it could reflect either reduced oxygen-use capacity or reduced demand."],"highlights":[["Resting metabolism","marker"]]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[{"id":"Kabasawa2002","author":"Kabasawa et al. · 2002","title":"Cerebral circulation and metabolism in chronic minor TBI with higher brain dysfunction","population":"20 chronic minor-TBI patients with cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI; 13 reassessed.","method":"Oxygen-15 gas PET; regional CBF, OEF and CMRO₂.","finding":"Regional CMRO₂ was below reference limits in many patients despite CBF within normal ranges; some regional values rose with cognitive improvement.","caveat":"English abstract only. Exact age range, civilian eligibility and detailed methods remain unverified; not a matched PPCS replication.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12607977/","manuscriptReference":"Additional primary source from targeted methods audit","reviewBasis":"Indexed primary English abstract; Japanese full text unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"English abstract; 20-person PET study and 13-person follow-up","fullTitle":"[Cerebral circulation and metabolism in the patients with higher brain dysfunction caused by chronic minor traumatic brain injury: a study by the positron emission tomography in twenty subjects with normal MRI findings].","paperId":"PMID12607977","pmid":"12607977","displayPopulation":"Chronic minor TBI · oxygen-15 PET","displayFinding":"Low regional CMRO₂ with CBF within reference ranges.","displayCaveat":"English abstract only; recruitment incompletely verified."},{"id":"Champagne2021","author":"Champagne et al. · 2021","title":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion","population":"15 athletes approximately 8 days after concussion; 27 athlete controls.","method":"Dual-calibrated MRI; CMRO₂ analysis in regions selected for lower CBF.","finding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF; no OEF difference.","caveat":"Acute athletes, not PPCS. The CBF difference was nonsignificant after CO₂ adjustment; the reported CMRO₂ comparison was not CO₂-adjusted.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11682-019-00240-2","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 11","reviewBasis":"Local supplied-reference publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 5–10; Tables 1–3; Methods, Results and limitations","fullTitle":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion: A calibrated MRI study.","paperId":"Champagne2021","pmid":"32307673","displayPopulation":"Athletes · ~8 days · calibrated MRI","displayFinding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF.","displayCaveat":"Subacute sport concussion; CBF result sensitive to CO₂ adjustment.","localSourcePath":"[local source path omitted]"}],"say":"A lower resting CMRO2 in isolation is ambiguous: it could reflect either reduced oxygen-use capacity or reduced demand.","scientificStatus":"interpretation"},{"quote":"hypoxic-response","topics":[{"label":"Hypoxic response","text":"Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions."}],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Hypoxic response. Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions."],"highlights":[["Hypoxic response","marker"]]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[{"id":"Kabasawa2002","author":"Kabasawa et al. · 2002","title":"Cerebral circulation and metabolism in chronic minor TBI with higher brain dysfunction","population":"20 chronic minor-TBI patients with cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI; 13 reassessed.","method":"Oxygen-15 gas PET; regional CBF, OEF and CMRO₂.","finding":"Regional CMRO₂ was below reference limits in many patients despite CBF within normal ranges; some regional values rose with cognitive improvement.","caveat":"English abstract only. Exact age range, civilian eligibility and detailed methods remain unverified; not a matched PPCS replication.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12607977/","manuscriptReference":"Additional primary source from targeted methods audit","reviewBasis":"Indexed primary English abstract; Japanese full text unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"English abstract; 20-person PET study and 13-person follow-up","fullTitle":"[Cerebral circulation and metabolism in the patients with higher brain dysfunction caused by chronic minor traumatic brain injury: a study by the positron emission tomography in twenty subjects with normal MRI findings].","paperId":"PMID12607977","pmid":"12607977","displayPopulation":"Chronic minor TBI · oxygen-15 PET","displayFinding":"Low regional CMRO₂ with CBF within reference ranges.","displayCaveat":"English abstract only; recruitment incompletely verified."},{"id":"Champagne2021","author":"Champagne et al. · 2021","title":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion","population":"15 athletes approximately 8 days after concussion; 27 athlete controls.","method":"Dual-calibrated MRI; CMRO₂ analysis in regions selected for lower CBF.","finding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF; no OEF difference.","caveat":"Acute athletes, not PPCS. The CBF difference was nonsignificant after CO₂ adjustment; the reported CMRO₂ comparison was not CO₂-adjusted.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11682-019-00240-2","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 11","reviewBasis":"Local supplied-reference publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 5–10; Tables 1–3; Methods, Results and limitations","fullTitle":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion: A calibrated MRI study.","paperId":"Champagne2021","pmid":"32307673","displayPopulation":"Athletes · ~8 days · calibrated MRI","displayFinding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF.","displayCaveat":"Subacute sport concussion; CBF result sensitive to CO₂ adjustment.","localSourcePath":"[local source path omitted]"}],"say":"Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions.","scientificStatus":"interpretation"},{"quote":"cellular-metabolism","topics":[{"label":"Hypoxic response","text":"Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions."},{"label":"Mitochondrial metabolism","text":"Altered pyruvate oxidation or mitochondrial function could limit oxygen use. Experimental work links microglia to suppressed PDH activity."}],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Hypoxic response. Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions.","Mitochondrial metabolism. Altered pyruvate oxidation or mitochondrial function could limit oxygen use. Experimental work links microglia to suppressed PDH activity."],"highlights":[["Hypoxic response","marker"],["Mitochondrial metabolism","marker"]]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[{"id":"Kabasawa2002","author":"Kabasawa et al. · 2002","title":"Cerebral circulation and metabolism in chronic minor TBI with higher brain dysfunction","population":"20 chronic minor-TBI patients with cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI; 13 reassessed.","method":"Oxygen-15 gas PET; regional CBF, OEF and CMRO₂.","finding":"Regional CMRO₂ was below reference limits in many patients despite CBF within normal ranges; some regional values rose with cognitive improvement.","caveat":"English abstract only. Exact age range, civilian eligibility and detailed methods remain unverified; not a matched PPCS replication.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12607977/","manuscriptReference":"Additional primary source from targeted methods audit","reviewBasis":"Indexed primary English abstract; Japanese full text unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"English abstract; 20-person PET study and 13-person follow-up","fullTitle":"[Cerebral circulation and metabolism in the patients with higher brain dysfunction caused by chronic minor traumatic brain injury: a study by the positron emission tomography in twenty subjects with normal MRI findings].","paperId":"PMID12607977","pmid":"12607977","displayPopulation":"Chronic minor TBI · oxygen-15 PET","displayFinding":"Low regional CMRO₂ with CBF within reference ranges.","displayCaveat":"English abstract only; recruitment incompletely verified."},{"id":"Champagne2021","author":"Champagne et al. · 2021","title":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion","population":"15 athletes approximately 8 days after concussion; 27 athlete controls.","method":"Dual-calibrated MRI; CMRO₂ analysis in regions selected for lower CBF.","finding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF; no OEF difference.","caveat":"Acute athletes, not PPCS. The CBF difference was nonsignificant after CO₂ adjustment; the reported CMRO₂ comparison was not CO₂-adjusted.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11682-019-00240-2","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 11","reviewBasis":"Local supplied-reference publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 5–10; Tables 1–3; Methods, Results and limitations","fullTitle":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion: A calibrated MRI study.","paperId":"Champagne2021","pmid":"32307673","displayPopulation":"Athletes · ~8 days · calibrated MRI","displayFinding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF.","displayCaveat":"Subacute sport concussion; CBF result sensitive to CO₂ adjustment.","localSourcePath":"[local source path omitted]"},{"id":"Guglielmetti2017","author":"Guglielmetti et al. · 2017","title":"In vivo metabolic imaging of traumatic brain injury","population":"Mouse focal cortical-impact injury; acute/subacute endpoints.","method":"Hyperpolarised ¹³C-pyruvate MRSI, enzyme assays and CSF1R inhibition.","finding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented the day-7 rise in lactate-to-pyruvate signal and the fall in PDH activity.","caveat":"Experimental contusion, not chronic human concussion; PDH substrate entry is distinct from a direct respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-17758-4","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 26","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2, 4–8; Fig. 4 and Discussion","fullTitle":"In vivo metabolic imaging of Traumatic Brain Injury.","paperId":"Guglielmetti2017","pmid":"29235509","displayPopulation":"Mice · cortical impact · enzyme assays","displayFinding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented reduced PDH activity.","displayCaveat":"Experimental injury; not a respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay."}],"say":"One candidate is impaired oxidative metabolism. In mice, microglia are linked to reduced pyruvate dehydrogenase activity. We did not measure mitochondrial function directly.","scientificStatus":"interpretation"},{"quote":"lactate-handling","topics":[{"label":"Hypoxic response","text":"Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions."},{"label":"Mitochondrial metabolism","text":"Altered pyruvate oxidation or mitochondrial function could limit oxygen use. Experimental work links microglia to suppressed PDH activity."},{"label":"Lactate handling","text":"Lactate concentration does not measure the rates of production, transport or oxidation. The astrocyte–neuron lactate shuttle and the location of the bottleneck remain untested."}],"quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Hypoxic response. Under hypoxia, CMRO₂ falls further despite increased global CBF. Reduced demand and limited oxygen-use capacity could both contribute; these measurements cannot separate their contributions.","Mitochondrial metabolism. Altered pyruvate oxidation or mitochondrial function could limit oxygen use. Experimental work links microglia to suppressed PDH activity.","Lactate handling. Lactate concentration does not measure the rates of production, transport or oxidation. The astrocyte–neuron lactate shuttle and the location of the bottleneck remain untested."],"highlights":[["Hypoxic response","marker"],["Mitochondrial metabolism","marker"],["Lactate handling","marker"]]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[{"id":"Kabasawa2002","author":"Kabasawa et al. · 2002","title":"Cerebral circulation and metabolism in chronic minor TBI with higher brain dysfunction","population":"20 chronic minor-TBI patients with cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI; 13 reassessed.","method":"Oxygen-15 gas PET; regional CBF, OEF and CMRO₂.","finding":"Regional CMRO₂ was below reference limits in many patients despite CBF within normal ranges; some regional values rose with cognitive improvement.","caveat":"English abstract only. Exact age range, civilian eligibility and detailed methods remain unverified; not a matched PPCS replication.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12607977/","manuscriptReference":"Additional primary source from targeted methods audit","reviewBasis":"Indexed primary English abstract; Japanese full text unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"English abstract; 20-person PET study and 13-person follow-up","fullTitle":"[Cerebral circulation and metabolism in the patients with higher brain dysfunction caused by chronic minor traumatic brain injury: a study by the positron emission tomography in twenty subjects with normal MRI findings].","paperId":"PMID12607977","pmid":"12607977","displayPopulation":"Chronic minor TBI · oxygen-15 PET","displayFinding":"Low regional CMRO₂ with CBF within reference ranges.","displayCaveat":"English abstract only; recruitment incompletely verified."},{"id":"Champagne2021","author":"Champagne et al. · 2021","title":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion","population":"15 athletes approximately 8 days after concussion; 27 athlete controls.","method":"Dual-calibrated MRI; CMRO₂ analysis in regions selected for lower CBF.","finding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF; no OEF difference.","caveat":"Acute athletes, not PPCS. The CBF difference was nonsignificant after CO₂ adjustment; the reported CMRO₂ comparison was not CO₂-adjusted.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11682-019-00240-2","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 11","reviewBasis":"Local supplied-reference publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 5–10; Tables 1–3; Methods, Results and limitations","fullTitle":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion: A calibrated MRI study.","paperId":"Champagne2021","pmid":"32307673","displayPopulation":"Athletes · ~8 days · calibrated MRI","displayFinding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF.","displayCaveat":"Subacute sport concussion; CBF result sensitive to CO₂ adjustment.","localSourcePath":"[local source path omitted]"},{"id":"Guglielmetti2017","author":"Guglielmetti et al. · 2017","title":"In vivo metabolic imaging of traumatic brain injury","population":"Mouse focal cortical-impact injury; acute/subacute endpoints.","method":"Hyperpolarised ¹³C-pyruvate MRSI, enzyme assays and CSF1R inhibition.","finding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented the day-7 rise in lactate-to-pyruvate signal and the fall in PDH activity.","caveat":"Experimental contusion, not chronic human concussion; PDH substrate entry is distinct from a direct respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-17758-4","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 26","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2, 4–8; Fig. 4 and Discussion","fullTitle":"In vivo metabolic imaging of Traumatic Brain Injury.","paperId":"Guglielmetti2017","pmid":"29235509","displayPopulation":"Mice · cortical impact · enzyme assays","displayFinding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented reduced PDH activity.","displayCaveat":"Experimental injury; not a respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay."}],"say":"Our lactate measurement cannot locate the bottleneck either. Concentration alone does not tell us about lactate transport, oxidation or the astrocyte-neuron shuttle.","scientificStatus":"interpretation"}]}],"omit":true,"omitReason":"Integrated into the cumulative Study III map; upstream mechanisms moved to the unified conclusion."},{"id":"metabolic-pathway-interpretation","subchapter":"Possible metabolic explanations","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"order":51.17,"seconds":75,"notes":"Mouse studies show repair and remodelling occurring together. Their long-term significance in human PPCS remains uncertain.\n\nAfter focal injury in mice, microglia/macrophage depletion prevented reduced PDH activity. This is not a direct demonstration of mitochondrial respiratory failure in our patients.\n\nOur single-voxel lactate measurement cannot distinguish production, transport, oxidation and clearance. The lactate shuttle and other metabolic steps remain untested.\n\nSource review: [local source path omitted]\n\nDo not claim that these are the same cohort; analyse and present the studies as separate samples with different controls.\nUse BBB influx constant Ki for the supplied DCE manuscript; do not treat Ki, Ktrans, extracellular volume ve and plasma volume vp as interchangeable measurements.\nRegional Ki elevation survived correction at cortical grey matter and the grey–white interface; no individual Ki parcel survived FDR. Widespread lower vb is the stronger spatial finding.\nPreserved resting global CBF means no statistically significant adjusted group difference here, not equivalence or proof that regional/microvascular oxygen delivery is normal.\nLower CMRO₂ and its hypoxic response support altered physiology; they do not measure ATP production or prove a specific mitochondrial lesion.\nOne-voxel ¹H-MRS lactate concentration does not identify glycolytic flux, astrocyte–neuron lactate transfer, pyruvate oxidation, mitochondrial respiration or clearance.\nFewer perfused capillaries, altered recruitment/tone and transit/exchange mechanisms are hypotheses; tracer-accessible vb does not measure anatomical capillary number.\nFunctional shunting is a physiological model concept here, not an observed anatomical shunt or a patient-specific measurement.\nNguyen shows microglial sealing of vessels after repeated injury alongside astrocyte phagocytosis; it must not be reduced to microglia simply causing BBB leakage.\nGuglielmetti tests focal cortical-impact mice and PDH activity; PDH substrate entry into oxidation is not synonymous with direct respiratory-chain function.\nGard uses athletes and uncorrected cytokine comparisons; evidence is suggestive, not a direct measurement of inflammation in the supplied human studies.\nExercise intolerance is associated with resting CMRO₂ in uncorrected secondary analysis; the hypoxic response is not significantly different between exercise groups.\nCross-sectional associations do not establish causality, recovery trajectories, clinical diagnostic performance or treatment efficacy.\n\ncmro-inflammation — supporting sources:\nNguyen et al. · 2025: Live microscopy, cell tracing and pathway perturbation. Microglia sealed leaky vessels while engulfing detached astrocytes, particularly after repeated injury; leakage could fall as cellular architecture changed. Shows a repair–remodelling tradeoff in mice, not the cause of chronic human PPCS or lower CMRO₂. Public primary XML full text extracted and read; PDF unavailable https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2414316122\n\ncmro-capacity — supporting sources:\nGuglielmetti et al. · 2017: Hyperpolarised ¹³C-pyruvate MRSI, enzyme assays and CSF1R inhibition. Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented the day-7 rise in lactate-to-pyruvate signal and the fall in PDH activity. Experimental contusion, not chronic human concussion; PDH substrate entry is distinct from a direct respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay. Publisher PDF extracted and read https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-17758-4\n\ncmro-untested — supporting sources:\nGuglielmetti et al. · 2017: Hyperpolarised ¹³C-pyruvate MRSI, enzyme assays and CSF1R inhibition. Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented the day-7 rise in lactate-to-pyruvate signal and the fall in PDH activity. Experimental contusion, not chronic human concussion; PDH substrate entry is distinct from a direct respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay. Publisher PDF extracted and read https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-17758-4","stepNotes":["","Mouse studies show repair and remodelling occurring together. Their long-term significance in human PPCS remains uncertain.","After focal injury in mice, microglia/macrophage depletion prevented reduced PDH activity. This is not a direct demonstration of mitochondrial respiratory failure in our patients.","Our single-voxel lactate measurement cannot distinguish production, transport, oxidation and clearance. The lactate shuttle and other metabolic steps remain untested."],"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"POSSIBLE EXPLANATIONS","sourceLabel":"CMRO₂ manuscript · Discussion","states":[{"quote":"reading-ready","quoteData":{"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[]},"visual":"text","sourceLabel":"","evidence":[],"say":""},{"quote":"cmro-inflammation","quoteData":{"id":"cmro-inflammation","paragraphs":["Microglia can alter the neurovascular environment after injury."],"highlights":[["Microglia","marker"],["neurovascular environment","marker"]]},"visual":"text","say":"Mouse studies show repair and remodelling occurring together. Their long-term significance in human PPCS remains uncertain.","sourceLabel":"A possible explanation","scientificStatus":"hypothesis","evidence":[{"id":"Nguyen2025","author":"Nguyen et al. · 2025","title":"Concussion and the immune response at the BBB","population":"Mice after single or repeated closed-skull concussion, examined over hours to days.","method":"Live microscopy, cell tracing and pathway perturbation.","finding":"Microglia sealed leaky vessels while engulfing detached astrocytes, particularly after repeated injury; leakage could fall as cellular architecture changed.","caveat":"Shows a repair–remodelling tradeoff in mice, not the cause of chronic human PPCS or lower CMRO₂.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2414316122","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 45; CMRO₂ ref. 28","reviewBasis":"Public primary XML full text extracted and read; PDF unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"Results/Figs. 2–5 and Discussion","fullTitle":"Imaging a concussion and the ensuing immune response at the blood-brain barrier.","paperId":"Nguyen2025","pmid":"40388609","displayPopulation":"Mice · single/repeated concussion · live microscopy","displayFinding":"Microglia sealed leaky vessels while engulfing detached astrocytes.","displayCaveat":"Repair and remodelling; chronic human consequences untested."}],"speakerWordCount":16},{"quote":"cmro-capacity","quoteData":{"id":"cmro-capacity","paragraphs":["Altered pyruvate metabolism is one experimental candidate."],"highlights":[["pyruvate metabolism","marker"],["experimental candidate","marker"]]},"visual":"text","say":"After focal injury in mice, microglia/macrophage depletion prevented reduced PDH activity. This is not a direct demonstration of mitochondrial respiratory failure in our patients.","sourceLabel":"A possible explanation","scientificStatus":"hypothesis","evidence":[{"id":"Guglielmetti2017","author":"Guglielmetti et al. · 2017","title":"In vivo metabolic imaging of traumatic brain injury","population":"Mouse focal cortical-impact injury; acute/subacute endpoints.","method":"Hyperpolarised ¹³C-pyruvate MRSI, enzyme assays and CSF1R inhibition.","finding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented the day-7 rise in lactate-to-pyruvate signal and the fall in PDH activity.","caveat":"Experimental contusion, not chronic human concussion; PDH substrate entry is distinct from a direct respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-17758-4","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 26","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2, 4–8; Fig. 4 and Discussion","fullTitle":"In vivo metabolic imaging of Traumatic Brain Injury.","paperId":"Guglielmetti2017","pmid":"29235509","displayPopulation":"Mice · focal cortical impact · metabolic MRI and enzyme assays","displayFinding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented reduced PDH activity.","displayCaveat":"Not a respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay."}],"speakerWordCount":24},{"quote":"cmro-untested","quoteData":{"id":"cmro-untested","paragraphs":["Lactate concentration does not reveal lactate transport or metabolic flux."],"highlights":[["Lactate concentration","marker"],["transport","marker"],["metabolic flux","marker"]]},"visual":"text","say":"Our single-voxel lactate measurement cannot distinguish production, transport, oxidation and clearance. The lactate shuttle and other metabolic steps remain untested.","sourceLabel":"What this may mean","scientificStatus":"interpretation","evidence":[{"id":"Guglielmetti2017","author":"Guglielmetti et al. · 2017","title":"In vivo metabolic imaging of traumatic brain injury","population":"Mouse focal cortical-impact injury; acute/subacute endpoints.","method":"Hyperpolarised ¹³C-pyruvate MRSI, enzyme assays and CSF1R inhibition.","finding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented the day-7 rise in lactate-to-pyruvate signal and the fall in PDH activity.","caveat":"Experimental contusion, not chronic human concussion; PDH substrate entry is distinct from a direct respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-17758-4","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 26","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2, 4–8; Fig. 4 and Discussion","fullTitle":"In vivo metabolic imaging of Traumatic Brain Injury.","paperId":"Guglielmetti2017","pmid":"29235509","displayPopulation":"Mice · focal cortical impact · metabolic MRI and enzyme assays","displayFinding":"Microglia/macrophage depletion prevented reduced PDH activity.","displayCaveat":"Not a respiratory-chain or lactate-shuttle assay."}],"speakerWordCount":20}],"title":"The mechanism remains open","cumulative":false,"branches":[]}],"omit":true},{"id":"cross-study-claims-and-limits","omit":true,"subchapter":"Claims and limits","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["head","figure"],"rows":"auto 1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What do the data establish—and what do they not?","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"claimBoundary","at":"figure","args":{"revealSteps":2,"aspect":2.24,"maxHeight":0.77}}],"source":"[@tireli2026thesis]","seconds":90,"notes":"Start only with observations. The pattern localises the metabolic deficit to utilisation rather than macrovascular delivery, but it does not directly identify mitochondria, inflammation, capillary loss or pericyte constriction as the cause. Click 1: show the mechanisms that are compatible with the pattern but were not measured. Chronic neurovascular inflammation is a plausible upstream candidate, not an observed result. The v_B intercept cannot distinguish rarefaction, stalling and calibre change. The pulse-intolerance association is secondary and uncorrected. No DCE or CMRO2 measure tracked RPQ or FSS after FDR, so this is not a generic symptom-severity biomarker. Click 2: reveal experiments that can discriminate the candidate mechanisms: serial DCE, regional CMRO2, parametric capillary transit-time heterogeneity, same-day haemoglobin, fluid or TSPO markers and multisite validation. Main limitations remain cross-sectional design, separate demographically imbalanced control cohorts, single scanner, fixed haemoglobin, global metabolism and limited lactate control N.","order":52},{"style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"cols":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"Acknowledgements","title":"Acknowledgements","sourceLabel":"","cumulative":true,"branches":[],"states":[{"quote":"thanks-0","quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Thank you to my supervisors Henrik Larsson and Mark Vestergaard, and to all my colleagues."],"highlights":[["Henrik Larsson","marker"],["Mark Vestergaard","marker"],["Sygeforsikring “danmark”","marker"],["patients","marker"],["patience, time and effort","marker"],["my colleagues","marker"]]},"visual":"text","evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"Thank you to my supervisors Henrik Larsson and Mark Vestergaard, and to all my colleagues."},{"quote":"thanks-1","quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Thank you to my supervisors Henrik Larsson and Mark Vestergaard, and to all my colleagues.","Thank you to Sygeforsikring “danmark” for funding this work."],"highlights":[["Henrik Larsson","marker"],["Mark Vestergaard","marker"],["Sygeforsikring “danmark”","marker"],["patients","marker"],["patience, time and effort","marker"],["my colleagues","marker"]]},"visual":"text","evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"Thank you to Sygeforsikring “danmark” for funding this work."},{"quote":"thanks-2","quoteData":{"paragraphs":["Thank you to my supervisors Henrik Larsson and Mark Vestergaard, and to all my colleagues.","Thank you to Sygeforsikring “danmark” for funding this work.","My deepest thanks to the patients for their extraordinary patience, time and effort."],"highlights":[["Henrik Larsson","marker"],["Mark Vestergaard","marker"],["Sygeforsikring “danmark”","marker"],["patients","marker"],["patience, time and effort","marker"],["my colleagues","marker"]]},"visual":"text","evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"My deepest thanks to the patients for their extraordinary patience, time and effort."}]}],"seconds":30,"notes":"Thank you to my supervisors Henrik Larsson and Mark Vestergaard, and to all my colleagues.\n\nThank you to Sygeforsikring “danmark” for funding this work.\n\nMy deepest thanks to the patients for their extraordinary patience, time and effort.\nFunding: Sundhedsdonationer, Sygeforsikring “danmark”.","order":53,"id":"take-home","rows":"1fr","stepNotes":["Thank you to my supervisors Henrik Larsson and Mark Vestergaard, and to all my colleagues.","Thank you to Sygeforsikring “danmark” for funding this work.","My deepest thanks to the patients for their extraordinary patience, time and effort."],"subchapter":"Acknowledgements"},{"id":"backup-influx-accumulation","backup":true,"style":"keynote","width":"wide","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["head","retention","meaning"],"rows":"auto auto auto","boxGap":"calc(24 * var(--u))","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Influx and accumulation","at":"head"},{"type":"body","at":"retention","text":"To connect this to the Patlak measurement, suppose tracer that enters the extravascular tissue remains there during the measurement. Its accumulated amount is then"},{"type":"math","at":"retention","tex":"A_e(t)=\\int_0^t J_{\\mathrm{in}}(\\tau)\\,d\\tau=K_i\\int_0^t C_a(\\tau)\\,d\\tau"},{"type":"body","at":"meaning","text":"This is the accumulating term in the Patlak equation. If return to the blood is negligible during the measurement, the accumulation coefficient represents the influx clearance.","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"body","at":"meaning","text":"$A_e(t)$ is the amount of retained tracer per 100 g tissue, in µmol. This expression assumes that $K_i$ is constant during the measurement.","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"}],"seconds":60,"notes":"Retained uptake links the influx law to the Patlak accumulating term. The coefficient can represent unidirectional influx only when tracer return is negligible over the measurement, and the model is fitted in its valid regime. The rate is per 100 g, so integrating gives micromol per 100 g. Source: Patlak CS, Blasberg RG, Fenstermacher JD. Graphical evaluation of blood-to-brain transfer constants from multiple-time uptake data. J Cereb Blood Flow Metab. 1983;3:1-7. See also Heye et al. 2016, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2015.10.018.","omit":true,"order":124},{"id":"backup-influx-ps","backup":true,"style":"keynote","width":"wide","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["head","insert","result","inverse","convention"],"rows":"auto auto auto auto auto","boxGap":"calc(24 * var(--u))","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Influx and the permeability–surface area product","at":"head"},{"type":"body","at":"insert","text":"Now insert Crone-Renkin. For these equations, $PS$, $F$ and $K_i$ must use a consistent blood or plasma convention."},{"type":"math","at":"insert","tex":"E=1-e^{-PS/F}"},{"type":"body","at":"result","text":"Substituting into $K_i=FE$ gives","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"result","tex":"\\boxed{K_i=F\\left(1-e^{-PS/F}\\right)}","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"body","at":"inverse","text":"Within this capillary model, knowing $K_i$ and $F$ lets us calculate","revealAt":2,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"inverse","tex":"\\boxed{PS=-F\\ln\\left(1-\\frac{K_i}{F}\\right)}\\qquad 0\\leq K_i<F","revealAt":2,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"body","at":"convention","text":"Using our whole-blood convention, $PS$ here is the blood-referenced effective product. The plasma-referenced product is $PS_{\\mathrm{plasma}}=(1-\\mathrm{Hct})PS$, where Hct is haematocrit, the fraction of blood volume occupied by red cells."}],"seconds":60,"notes":"The inverse follows algebraically from the exact extraction equation. It is defined for 0 <= Ki < F. The thesis uses whole-blood Ca and Ki. For extracellular gadolinium with negligible red-cell entry, Cb=(1-Hct)Cp, Fp=(1-Hct)Fb, Ki,p=(1-Hct)Ki,b. Physical P times S is plasma-referenced. Defining the blood-referenced effective PSb=PSp/(1-Hct) gives Ki,b=Fb[1-exp(-PSb/Fb)]. Therefore Ki,b approximates PSb, not the unrescaled plasma product. Hct is assumed constant. See thesis methods.tex, unit-convention paragraph. Sources: Crone C (1963), https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1748-1716.1963.tb02652.x; Renkin EM (1959), https://doi.org/10.1152/ajplegacy.1959.197.6.1205. Equations and original capillary-model assumptions: https://www.imagwiki.nibib.nih.gov/physiome/jsim/models/webmodel/NSR/renkin2region59.","omit":true,"order":125},{"id":"backup-low-extraction","backup":true,"style":"keynote","width":"wide","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["head","expansion","substitution","interpretation"],"rows":"auto auto auto auto","boxGap":"calc(24 * var(--u))","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"The low-extraction limit","at":"head"},{"type":"body","at":"expansion","text":"The approximation $K_i\\approx PS$ comes from the low-extraction limit. When $PS/F\\ll 1$,"},{"type":"math","at":"expansion","tex":"e^{-PS/F}=1-\\frac{PS}{F}+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{PS}{F}\\right)^2-\\cdots"},{"type":"body","at":"substitution","text":"Substitution gives","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"substitution","tex":"K_i=PS-\\frac{(PS)^2}{2F}+\\cdots\\quad\\boxed{K_i\\approx PS}","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"body","at":"interpretation","text":"Physically, extraction is so small that tracer concentration falls very little along the capillary. Increasing flow supplies more tracer, while reducing the fraction extracted from each passing volume.","revealAt":2,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"body","at":"interpretation","text":"In this limit those effects cancel to first order, leaving transfer governed by $PS$. This is the **permeability-limited regime**.","revealAt":2,"enter":"fade"}],"seconds":60,"notes":"The Taylor series is an expansion in the dimensionless ratio PS/F. (PS)^2 means the square of the entire permeability-surface area product. It does not mean P times S squared. The approximation retains the convention established on the previous slide. The thesis uses whole-blood Ca and Ki. For extracellular gadolinium with negligible red-cell entry, Cb=(1-Hct)Cp, Fp=(1-Hct)Fb, Ki,p=(1-Hct)Ki,b. Physical P times S is plasma-referenced. Defining the blood-referenced effective PSb=PSp/(1-Hct) gives Ki,b=Fb[1-exp(-PSb/Fb)]. Therefore Ki,b approximates PSb, not the unrescaled plasma product. Hct is assumed constant. See thesis methods.tex, unit-convention paragraph. Source: Heye et al. Tracer kinetic modelling for DCE-MRI quantification of subtle blood-brain barrier permeability. NeuroImage. 2016;125:446-455, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2015.10.018. Exact relation: Sources: Crone C (1963), https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1748-1716.1963.tb02652.x; Renkin EM (1959), https://doi.org/10.1152/ajplegacy.1959.197.6.1205. Equations and original capillary-model assumptions: https://www.imagwiki.nibib.nih.gov/physiome/jsim/models/webmodel/NSR/renkin2region59.","omit":true,"order":126},{"id":"backup","omit":true,"template":"chapter","style":"keynote","number":"05","heading":"Backup","eyebrow":"models · assumptions · acquisition · literature","seconds":5,"notes":"Technical backup begins here. The main talk ends on the previous slide.","order":127,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-study-ii-effective-input","omit":true,"subchapter":"Study II · input and modelling","width":"wide","align":"left","layout":["head","choices","equation"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Which input did Study II actually use?","at":"head"},{"type":"columns","ratio":"1 1 1","at":"choices","of":[[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"1 · ARRIVAL"},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"t_{\\mathrm{rICA}}"},{"type":"body","text":"right ICA supplies the arterial bolus-arrival reference"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"2 · SHAPE"},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"C_{\\mathrm{SSS}}(t)"},{"type":"body","text":"the larger SSS supplies a higher-SNR concentration curve"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"3 · EFFECTIVE INPUT"},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"\\Delta t=t_{\\mathrm{SSS,arr}}-t_{\\mathrm{rICA,arr}}\\quad C_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)=C_{\\mathrm{SSS}}(t+\\Delta t)"},{"type":"body","text":"rICA timing + SSS amplitude and dispersion\nthis effective input entered the Study II Patlak fit"}]]},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"less arterial partial volume · venous dispersion / haematocrit / relaxivity differences remain","at":"equation"}],"source":"[@tireli2026; @tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":55,"notes":"Study II did not use a directly sampled rICA concentration curve as its final input. p-Brain extracted the high-SNR SSS curve and shifted it by the SSS-minus-rICA arrival-time difference. This borrows arterial timing while retaining the venous curve's amplitude and dispersion, so it is best called a time-aligned venous input function used as an effective input—not a literal AIF. This shifted SSS curve entered the Study II Patlak fit. The direct-rICA trace in the main animation is a pedagogical sampling example, not the final Study II input. p-Brain can also use direct ICA and external providers.","order":128,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-signal-to-concentration","omit":true,"width":"wide","align":"center","layout":["head","equation","acq"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Signal is not concentration. How was S(t) inverted?","at":"head"},{"type":"math","display":true,"at":"equation","tex":"\\begin{gathered}\\eta(t)=\\frac{S(t)}{M_0\\sin\\alpha},\\qquad S(t)=M_0\\sin\\alpha\\left[1-e^{-T_D(R_{1,0}+r_1C(t))}\\right]\\\\[5pt]\\boxed{C(t)=-\\frac{1}{r_1}\\left[\\frac{1}{T_D}\\ln\\!\\left(1-\\frac{S(t)}{M_0\\sin\\alpha}\\right)+\\frac{1}{T_{1,0}}\\right]}\\end{gathered}"},{"type":"body","text":"separate precontrast T₁/M₀ map · T₁ grid 100–6000 ms · α=30° · Tᴰ=120 ms · r₁=4 s⁻¹mM⁻¹\nfirst 5 C frames: subtract their mean, then zero them · η≥1 is invalid → 0","at":"acq"}],"source":"[@tofts1999; @larsson2009; @tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":55,"notes":"Study II did not fit a new dynamic T1 at every DCE frame; that belongs to an unused VFA/SPGR branch. p-Brain estimated one baseline T1,0 and M0 map from the separate precontrast acquisition, then inverted every dynamic saturation-recovery signal sample with the displayed equation. The precontrast acquisition used delays 120, 300, 600, 1000, 2000, 4000 and 10,000 milliseconds; do not confuse those acquisition delays with the 100–6000 millisecond fitted T1 grid. If S divided by M0 sin alpha is at least one, the logarithm is undefined; that sample becomes NaN and is set to zero. After conversion, the mean of the first five concentration frames is subtracted and the baseline window is forced to zero. The inversion assumes the saturation-recovery signal model, fixed relaxivity and effectively fast water exchange. B1 or flip-angle error, M0 error, drift, blood–tissue relaxivity mismatch, exchange and saturation propagate nonlinearly into concentration.","order":129,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-patlak-compartments","omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","zoom":0.84,"layout":["head","art","equation","."],"rows":"auto 1fr auto 7vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center","equation":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What compartments make a Patlak line possible?","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/backup/compartmental-models.png","framed":true,"alt":"One- and two-compartment DCE models","at":"art"},{"type":"math","display":true,"at":"equation","tex":"\\boxed{C_t(t)=v_R^{\\mathrm{app}}C_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)+K_i^{(b)}\\int_0^t C_{\\mathrm{in}}(\\tau)\\,d\\tau,\\qquad t\\ge t^*}"},{"type":"body","text":"one measured source · linear first-order time-invariant kinetics · zero initial tracer · tracer does not perturb physiology\narbitrary rapidly reversible compartments · at least one effectively irreversible sink during the fitted interval\nplasma convention: vₚ=(1−Hct)vᴿᵃᵖᵖ and Kᵗʳᵃⁿˢ=(1−Hct)Kᵢ⁽ᵇ⁾","at":"equation"}],"source":"[@patlak1983; @larsson2009]","seconds":60,"notes":"Patlak assumes a single measured input, linear time-invariant kinetics, one or more rapidly reversible compartments and a compartment whose uptake is effectively irreversible over the fitted interval. After t-star, the reversible compartments track the input, so their contribution is proportional to its current concentration. The accumulated compartment remains effectively unidirectional during that fitted window. C_in is deliberately neutral notation: Study II used a whole-blood-referenced effective input, whereas the conventional Tofts derivation uses plasma. The intercept b_R is operational; p-Brain reports it as v_B, but it is not guaranteed to equal literal blood volume. Converting a whole-blood convention to plasma multiplies both the intercept and influx constant by one minus haematocrit.","order":130,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-patlak-linearity","omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","zoom":0.82,"layout":["head","equation","art","."],"rows":"auto auto 1fr 7vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center","equation":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Why does the late relation become linear?","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/backup/patlak-plot.png","framed":true,"alt":"Patlak time curves and transformed linear relation","at":"art"},{"type":"math","display":true,"at":"equation","tex":"\\begin{gathered}x(t)=\\frac{\\int_0^tC_{\\mathrm{in}}(\\tau)d\\tau}{C_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)},\\qquad y(t)=\\frac{C_t(t)}{C_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)}\\\\[3pt]\\boxed{y=v_R^{\\mathrm{app}}+K_i^{(b)}x},\\qquad t\\ge t^*,\\qquad K_i^{(b)}=F_bE\\end{gathered}"},{"type":"body","text":"linearity begins after reversible pools track the input—not merely when the first pass ends · Kᵢ≈PS only in the low-extraction, permeability-limited regime\nimplementation: Huber δ=1.345 · x≥xₘₐₓ/3 · left-Riemann sum · slope×6000 · intercept×100","at":"equation"}],"source":"[@patlak1983; @larsson2009; @sourbron2011]","seconds":65,"notes":"The fitted interval starts only once the transformed points are approximately linear and the reversible pools follow the input. It is not valid to say that low late input concentration suppresses backflux; a falling blood concentration can increase the outward driving force. K_i is flow times extraction and approaches PS only in the low-extraction, permeability-limited regime. p-Brain uses Huber regression with delta 1.345 over x values at least one third of x-max, a left-Riemann integral, slope times 6000 and intercept times 100. Multiple boluses do not invalidate a linear time-invariant Patlak equation, but x need not be monotone: an x-threshold can select a disjoint, nonchronological set, and near-zero input can define x-max. Points should therefore be coloured by time and a contiguous late t-star window plus curvature and fit-window stability should be inspected.","order":131,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-patlak-failure","omit":true,"width":"wide","align":"left","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"When can Patlak fail?"},{"type":"columns","ratio":"1 1 1","of":[[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"KINETICS"},{"type":"body","text":"appreciable backflux\nnonstationarity\nreversible pools not equilibrated\nwrong or unstable fit window"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"MEASUREMENT"},{"type":"body","text":"input delay and dispersion\nCᵢₙ → 0 denominator instability\narterial partial volume\nwater exchange · B₁ error · drift"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"INTERPRETATION"},{"type":"body","text":"Kᵢ=FE · not automatically PS\nlow flow / high extraction can delay t*\nbut do not alone disprove a Patlak line\ntest curvature + window stability"}]]},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"a straight line is a model diagnostic—not proof of a microscopic route"}],"source":"[@heye2016; @manning2021; @patlak1983]","seconds":65,"notes":"In Larsson's simple two-compartment simulation, resolved backflux bent late points downward, underestimating K_i and overestimating the intercept; the direction is model- and window-dependent rather than universal. Input amplitude error scales K_i and volume, while input delay and dispersion reshape the plot. Other failures include near-zero input denominators, water exchange, drift, B1 or M0 error, partial volume, nonstationarity, unmodelled sources or metabolites and low CNR. Low flow or high extraction does not by itself invalidate a Patlak line; it can delay reversible-pool equilibration and invalidates the shortcut K_i approximately equals PS. A straight line is necessary, not sufficient: test curvature and fit-window stability.","order":132,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-extended-tofts","omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","zoom":0.8,"layout":["head","model","limits","."],"rows":"auto 0.40fr 0.48fr 7vh","cellAlign":{"model":"center","limits":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What if tracer can return from tissue to blood?","at":"head"},{"type":"math","display":true,"at":"model","tex":"\\begin{gathered}\\boxed{C_t(t)=v_pC_p(t)+K^{\\mathrm{trans}}\\!\\int_0^t C_p(\\tau)e^{-k_{\\mathrm{ep}}(t-\\tau)}d\\tau},\\qquad k_{\\mathrm{ep}}=\\frac{K^{\\mathrm{trans}}}{v_e}\\\\[3pt]C_p=\\frac{C_b}{1-\\mathrm{Hct}},\\qquad v_p=(1-\\mathrm{Hct})v_b,\\qquad K^{\\mathrm{trans}}=(1-\\mathrm{Hct})K_i^{(b)}\\end{gathered}"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"a 2CXM limit when vₚ ≈ 0 or plasma flow Fₚ → ∞ · not a general flow-and-exchange model","at":"model"},{"type":"columns","ratio":"1 1 1","at":"limits","of":[[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"PLASMA PASSAGE"},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"v_pC_p(t)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["instantaneous vascular term","no separate flow estimate","no transit-time estimate"]}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"FORWARD EXCHANGE"},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"K^{\\mathrm{trans}}"},{"type":"bullets","items":["flow-limited or permeability-limited","depends on the extraction regime"]}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"BACKFLUX"},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"k_{\\mathrm{ep}}=K^{\\mathrm{trans}}/v_e"},{"type":"bullets","items":["well-mixed EES returns tracer","late washout carries the information"]}]]}],"source":"[@tofts1999; @sourbron2011]","seconds":75,"notes":"Extended Tofts uses the conventional plasma-input convention and was not a Study II endpoint. It replaces Patlak's effectively irreversible sink with a well-mixed extravascular-extracellular compartment that can return tracer to plasma. The first term is the instantaneous plasma volume; the convolution term is forward delivery followed by exponential washout. Limiting cases matter: v_p to zero gives standard Tofts; k_ep times acquisition duration much less than one makes the exponential approximately one, producing Patlak-like accumulation while v_e becomes unidentifiable; Ktrans to zero leaves only the vascular term; and k_ep times sample interval much greater than one makes exchange near-instantaneous, so only the sum v_p plus v_e is seen. The model does not identify plasma flow or a distributed vascular residue because vascular transit is collapsed to a delta function.","order":133,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-extended-tofts-identifiability","omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"left","zoom":0.88,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". regimes .",". guard .",". . ."],"cols":"5vw 1fr 5vw","rows":"5vh auto 1fr auto 8vh","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"When is extended Tofts identifiable?","at":"head"},{"type":"columns","ratio":"1 1 1","at":"regimes","of":[[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"THE DATA MUST CONTAIN"},{"type":"bullets","items":["resolved first pass for vₚ","long, stable washout for kₑₚ","adequate temporal sampling","contrast-to-noise above drift"]},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"T_{\\mathrm{acq}}\\gtrsim O(k_{\\mathrm{ep}}^{-1})"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"THE MODEL ASSUMES"},{"type":"bullets","items":["one measured plasma input","linear time-invariant kinetics","well-mixed EES","vₚ = 0 or Fₚ = ∞ for exact 2CXM equivalence","no intracellular trapping"]},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"\\mathrm{ETM}\\equiv\\mathrm{2CXM}\\iff(v_p=0)\\;\\lor\\;(F_p=\\infty)"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"COMMON DEGENERACIES"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Ktrans ↔ vₑ correlation","vₚ ↔ arrival and dispersion","slow backflux ↔ drift","fast exchange ↔ vₚ+vₑ only","excellent fit ≠ valid parameter labels"]},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"\\operatorname{rank}(J)=3,\\qquad\\kappa(J^{\\mathsf T}WJ)\\;\\text{acceptable}"}]]},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"prefer ETM over Patlak only when backflux is resolved and an ETM-valid architectural regime is defensible · a good fit alone proves neither","at":"guard"}],"source":"[@tofts1999; @sourbron2011; @heye2016]","seconds":75,"notes":"Three parameters do not become identifiable merely because an optimiser returns three numbers. Plasma volume needs a resolved first pass; backflux needs a sufficiently long and stable washout; Ktrans and v_e are strongly coupled when only their ratio is visible. Even an excellent curve fit does not make extended-Tofts parameters faithful to a general finite-flow two-compartment exchange system. Inspect the sensitivity or Fisher-information matrix, profile likelihoods, residual structure and boundary hits. Extended Tofts is useful when measurable washout violates Patlak irreversibility, the acquisition resolves it and the limiting physiology is defensible. Patlak is often more stable for weak chronic leakage when backflux is below the information content of the scan.","order":134,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-study-ii-acquisition","omit":true,"width":"wide","align":"left","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What did the Study II scan trade away?"},{"type":"columns","ratio":"1 1 1","of":[[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"DYNAMIC"},{"type":"body","text":"250 frames · 2.44 s\n≈10 minutes\nTR/TE 3.95/1.88 ms\nflip 30°"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"SPATIAL"},{"type":"body","text":"10 slices\n8 mm thick · 1.5 mm gap\n≈2.4 mm acquired in-plane\n0.9 mm reconstructed"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CONTRAST"},{"type":"body","text":"gadobutrol 0.09 mmol/kg\ntwo administrations\n3 mL/s\n20 mL saline flush"}]]},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"moderate kinetic sampling prioritised over spatial coverage · 0.90 mm is reconstructed grid spacing, not acquired resolution"}],"source":"[@tireli2026; @tireli2026thesis]","subchapter":"Study II · acquisition and anatomy","seconds":60,"notes":"Do not mix Study II with the p-Brain validation protocol. Study II used 250 frames at 2.44 seconds, totaling 610 seconds or about 10.2 minutes. There were ten slices, 8 millimetres thick with a 1.5-millimetre gap; the in-plane acquisition was about 2.4 millimetres and was reconstructed onto a 0.90-millimetre grid, which does not restore acquired resolution. Total gadobutrol was 0.09 millimoles per kilogram split over two injections at 3 millilitres per second, each followed by a 20-millilitre saline flush at the same rate. The separate precontrast map used seven delays from 120 to 10,000 milliseconds. A 2.44-second sample is moderate rather than ideal for the fastest intravascular transit. The spatial trade-off affects atlas coverage and motivates explicit parcel-coverage QC.","order":135,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-study-ii-anatomy","omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["head","art","detail"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"How did anatomy become an analysis unit?","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/backup/segmentation-pipeline.png","framed":true,"alt":"T1-weighted segmentation and DCE registration pipeline","at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"85 analysed parcels · bilateral bankssts, frontal pole and temporal pole excluded (<30% usable DCE coverage) · OLS + HC3 + rank-INT · BH-FDR within metric and anatomical level","at":"detail"}],"source":"[@tireli2026; @tireli2026thesis]","seconds":65,"notes":"Study II used FreeSurfer 8 with the DKT parcellation, not the current p-Brain SynthSeg default. Tissue and parcel masks were registered from the high-resolution T1-weighted anatomy to the thick, gapped DCE slab. Bilateral banks of the superior temporal sulcus, frontal pole and temporal pole were excluded because fewer than 30 percent of subjects had usable DCE coverage. Choroid-plexus and white-matter-hypointensity labels had no usable voxels in the registered field of view; that is a coverage or segmentation outcome, not biological absence. Eighty-five parcels remained. Group inference used OLS with group, age and sex, HC3 standard errors, rank inverse-normalisation sensitivity analyses and Benjamini–Hochberg FDR separately within each metric and anatomical level. With 15 controls, HC3 is not a substitute for a larger matched design.","order":136,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-study-i-validation","omit":true,"width":"wide","align":"left","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What did Study I validate—and what did it not?"},{"type":"columns","ratio":"1 1 1","of":[[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"SOLVER PARITY"},{"type":"subtitle","text":"4 participants · ≈6.9×10⁵ voxels"},{"type":"math","display":true,"tex":"r=\\rho_c=1.000"},{"type":"body","text":"identical inputs · all five maps"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"AUTONOMOUS DOWNSTREAM · n=12"},{"type":"subtitle","text":"same supplied reference input"},{"type":"body","text":"r / ICC\nCBF .972 / .969\nCBV .997 / .997 · MTT .961 / .961\nKᵢ .963 / .962 · vᴮ .986 / .985"}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"VESSELS · SEPARATE TEST"},{"type":"subtitle","text":"ICA Dice .75 ± .06"},{"type":"subtitle","text":"SSS Dice .57 ± .07"},{"type":"body","text":"max-voxel curves r ≈ .98\nnot provider equivalence, pathology, multisite or CTH"}]]},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"agreement conditional on one supplied input · plus separate vessel-ROI validation"}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":60,"notes":"Validation had three distinct tiers. First, mathematical solver parity used four participants and roughly 690,000 voxels with identical inputs, producing r and concordance one to numerical precision for all five maps. Second, the autonomous downstream comparison used 12 controls but supplied both pipelines the same reference input. Pooled r, concordance and ICC were all above .96; the slide shows r and ICC. Third, vessel localisation was tested separately: ICA Dice .75 plus or minus .06, SSS Dice .57 plus or minus .07 and maximum-voxel curve correlations around .98. This does not validate provider equivalence, pathology or multisite generalisation, unconditional end-to-end equality or CTH accuracy.","order":137,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-study-iii-flow-oximetry","omit":true,"subchapter":"Study III · measurement details","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["head","flow","equation"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","cellAlign":{"flow":"center","equation":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"How were flow and venous oxygenation measured?","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/backup/oxygen-methods-wide.png","framed":true,"alt":"Phase-contrast velocity encoding beside the superior sagittal sinus susceptibility-cylinder model","at":"flow"},{"type":"math","display":true,"at":"equation","tex":"\\begin{gathered}\\boxed{\\mathrm{CBF}=\\frac{100\\cdot60}{\\rho_{\\mathrm{brain}}V_{\\mathrm{brain}}}\\left(Q_{\\mathrm{LICA}}+Q_{\\mathrm{RICA}}+Q_{\\mathrm{BA}}\\right)},\\qquad \\rho_{\\mathrm{brain}}=1.05\\;\\mathrm{g/mL}\\\\[3pt]\\boxed{\\mathrm{CMRO}_2=\\mathrm{CBF}[\\mathrm{Hb}](S_aO_2-S_vO_2)}\\end{gathered}"}],"source":"[@tireli2026oxygen; @tireli2026thesis]","seconds":65,"notes":"Phase-contrast MRI encodes through-plane velocity and integrates it over bilateral ICA and basilar cross-sections. Ten dynamics were acquired over 35 seconds without cardiac gating at VENC 100 centimetres per second. The three flows are in millilitres per second, so multiply by 60 and normalise by segmented brain mass, rho-brain times brain volume, to obtain millilitres per 100 grams per minute. Only then apply Fick's oxygen balance. Susceptibility-based oximetry uses phase difference delta-phi equals gamma delta-B delta-TE and a straight-cylinder field model in a posterior SSS segment; assumptions include steady state, negligible dissolved oxygen, representative global venous drainage, uniform cylindrical geometry, correct angle and phase unwrapping. Haemoglobin was fixed at 9.3 millimoles per litre rather than measured same day. If sex-typical haemoglobin ordering held, fixing haemoglobin would tend to attenuate the group contrast; without measured haemoglobin, the bias direction is not guaranteed.","order":138,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-study-iii-lactate","omit":true,"width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","zoom":0.84,"layout":["head","art","equation"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","cellAlign":{"art":"center","equation":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Why was lactate measured at TE = 288 ms?","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/backup/mrs-lactate.png","framed":true,"alt":"Lactate J-modulation, molecular structure and representative spectra","at":"art"},{"type":"math","display":true,"at":"equation","tex":"S(\\mathrm{TE})\\propto\\cos(\\pi J\\,\\mathrm{TE})\\qquad J\\approx7\\,\\mathrm{Hz}"},{"type":"body","text":"ideal weak-coupling mnemonic: ≈143 ms first inverted echo · ≈286–288 ms second upright refocus\nPRESS 30 × 35 × 30 mm³ precuneus · TR 2000 ms · TE 288 ms · 144 averages\nLCModel 6.3-1F · unsuppressed-water reference · tissue/CSF-fraction correction","at":"equation"}],"source":"[@tireli2026oxygen; @tireli2026thesis]","seconds":60,"notes":"The cosine is an ideal weak-coupling mnemonic, not a complete PRESS density-matrix evolution. For J near 7 hertz, about 143 milliseconds gives the first inverted echo and about 286–288 milliseconds the second upright refocus. The long echo suppresses the short-T2 macromolecular baseline under the 1.3-ppm lactate doublet, but loses lactate SNR and preferentially retains longer-T2 components. LCModel 6.3-1F referenced the spectrum to unsuppressed water and corrected for tissue and CSF fractions. The readout is regional—one large precuneus voxel—while CBF and CMRO2 are global. The low control N makes a null lactate result imprecise, not proof of absence.","order":139,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-literature-anatomy","omit":true,"subchapter":"Literature and design limits","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["head","figure","foot"],"rows":"auto 1fr auto","cellAlign":{"figure":"center","foot":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Do the closest prior findings occupy the same anatomy?","at":"head"},{"type":"scene","scene":"literatureComparator","at":"figure","args":{"aspect":2.22,"maxHeight":0.76,"fps":10,"dpr":1}},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Yoen: cortical Kᵗʳᵃⁿˢ higher; brainstem + cerebellar-WM vₚ lower · present study: local Kᵢ increase + diffuse vᴮ decrease","at":"foot"}],"source":"[@yoen2021; @tireli2026]","seconds":65,"notes":"This is the approved anatomy-first comparison rather than a text-only table. The two left brains map Yoen's full-text findings onto the same reference segmentation used on the right: higher cortical Ktrans, and lower plasma volume in cerebellar white matter and brainstem. The two right brains map the present Study II pattern: higher K_i in cortical grey matter and the GM/WM interface, and lower v_B in every pre-specified tissue class. The anatomy is a direction-and-location comparison, not a reanalysis of Yoen's images. Yoen's comparator group was not a conventional healthy cohort: most presented with headache or other symptoms but had normal conventional MRI. Its beyond-three-month cortical result was a trend, and continuous imaging–neuropsychological correlations were null; only a small atypical auditory-CPT subgroup differed. Compare direction and anatomy only: Ktrans and v_p are plasma-space quantities and are not numerically interchangeable with the present blood-referenced K_i and v_B. Yoo 2019 remains outside the detailed comparison because a verified full text was unavailable during the audit; the local archived metadata and PDF were corrupted.","order":140,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-denominators-limits","omit":true,"width":"wide","align":"left","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"What were the denominator and design limits?"},{"type":"columns","ratio":"1 1","of":[[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"STUDY II"},{"type":"subtitle","text":"PPCS · 70 enrolled → 46 analysed"},{"type":"bullets","items":["60 attended → 57 complete DCE","11 signal-QC exclusions → 46","controls: 21 DCE → 15 after 6 signal-QC exclusions","older, less-female controls · thick gapped slab"]}],[{"type":"eyebrow","text":"STUDY III"},{"type":"subtitle","text":"PPCS · 45 valid hypoxia sessions"},{"type":"bullets","items":["rest: CBF/CMRO₂ n=41 · lactate n=38","reactivity: CBF/CMRO₂ n=39 · lactate n=35","controls: CBF/CMRO₂ n=47","control lactate n=17 rest / 16 reactivity"]}]]},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"age / sex adjustment reduces confounding · it does not recreate matching or randomisation"}],"source":"[@tireli2026; @tireli2026oxygen; @tireli2026thesis]","seconds":60,"notes":"Study II and III use overlapping patients but distinct control cohorts and endpoint-specific denominators. Study II controls were older and less female. Study III controls came from an earlier same-site cohort and lacked the patient clinical and exercise screen. The designs were cross-sectional. Group models adjusted for age and sex, but covariate adjustment cannot recreate matching or randomisation. The symptom and exercise analyses were secondary and not multiplicity-confirmed. Fixed haemoglobin, global metabolic measures and a single regional lactate voxel further limit mechanistic inference.","order":141,"backup":true},{"id":"backup-extended-background","template":"chapter","style":"keynote","heading":"Backup","number":"05","eyebrow":"Literature · Paper 1 · Cohort · Paper 2 · Paper 3","backup":true,"order":90,"seconds":5,"notes":"Supporting material. The main presentation ends at Acknowledgements. Subsections: Literature, Paper 1, Cohort, Paper 2, Paper 3."},{"id":"backup-extended-background-map","order":100,"subchapter":"Literature","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I"],"alt":"Interactive literature network of 19,542 papers with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links","states":[{"focus":"all","overview":true,"title":false,"heading":"What do we actually know after concussion?","metric":"392 papers","progressive":true,"buildSeconds":3.2,"subtitle":false}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"plainHeadings":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz"}],"seconds":25,"notes":"Click 0\n- What does the literature show, from the **immediate response to concussion** to **longer-lasting changes**?\nPoint to: The whole evidence landscape\nThese selected experiments and clinical studies build the rationale for the thesis. They do not establish one continuous causal pathway from an impact to every chronic symptom.","backup":true},{"id":"pbrain-paper-to-module","order":26.8,"subchapter":"p-Brain · share the method","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":520,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"pbrain-puzzle","mode":"paper","at":"art","args":{"revealSteps":3},"minimalFooter":true}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":45,"notes":"Footer captions removed for the live presentation, except the two highlighted module prompts. The manuscript and puzzle labels remain part of the diagram.\n\nA researcher may develop a method beyond the built-in choices. Start with an empty research manuscript: first the method has to be described, then its implementation can be shared. The three clicks write the paper, turn the highlighted method into a module, and place that module in p-Brain.\n\nThe manuscript types rapidly and pauses on its Methods sentence: “We analysed the data with p-Brain using our custom kinetic model.” The phrase “our custom kinetic model” is highlighted in orange. This is an illustrative manuscript, not a real publication. The typing represents the researcher describing their work, not an AI writing or validating a paper.\n\nThe orange Methods phrase lifts from the page and unfolds into a solid puzzle piece labeled Your model. This is a visual metaphor for implementing and sharing the method through the p-Brain model interface. It does not imply that prose is automatically converted into working software. The researcher supplies the implementation and declares its required inputs and produced outputs.\n\nThe p-Brain framework appears beside the manuscript with a matching model slot. Your model moves across and seats in that slot; the surrounding modules briefly light up. Common loading, processing, aggregation and diagnostics can be reused around a compatible model implementation. The paper describes the method, and the shared module makes it usable in the same framework. The board illustrates interfaces, not an exact execution dependency graph.\n\nSources: article_pBrain/pbrain_body.tex, modular framework; https://doi.org/10.1002/mrm.70554; https://github.com/edtireli/p-brain (commit c3201686b67ec159fc739b4cf8cd2a7b6a8881d3), README Add your own and Models. The manuscript is illustrative, not a real publication or a claim of new validation results.","speakerCues":["A researcher may develop a method beyond the built-in choices. Start with an empty research manuscript: first the method has to be described, then its implementation can be shared. The three clicks write the paper, turn the highlighted method into a module, and place that module in p-Brain.","The manuscript types rapidly and pauses on its Methods sentence: “We analysed the data with p-Brain using our custom kinetic model.” The phrase “our custom kinetic model” is highlighted in orange. This is an illustrative manuscript, not a real publication. The typing represents the researcher describing their work, not an AI writing or validating a paper.","The orange Methods phrase lifts from the page and unfolds into a solid puzzle piece labeled Your model. This is a visual metaphor for implementing and sharing the method through the p-Brain model interface. It does not imply that prose is automatically converted into working software. The researcher supplies the implementation and declares its required inputs and produced outputs.","The p-Brain framework appears beside the manuscript with a matching model slot. Your model moves across and seats in that slot; the surrounding modules briefly light up. Common loading, processing, aggregation and diagnostics can be reused around a compatible model implementation. The paper describes the method, and the shared module makes it usable in the same framework. The board illustrates interfaces, not an exact execution dependency graph."]},{"id":"pbrain-modular-framework","order":26.5,"subchapter":"p-Brain · a modular framework","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":520,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"pbrain-puzzle","mode":"modules","at":"art","args":{"revealSteps":7},"captions":{"0":{"text":"Think of the software as a set of pieces.","highlights":["set of pieces"]},"2":{"text":"If your study needs a different method replace the relevant piece.","highlights":["different method replace"]}},"minimalFooter":true}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":85,"notes":"Footer captions removed for the live presentation, except the two highlighted module prompts. The manuscript and puzzle labels remain part of the diagram.\n\np-Brain is open source under the MIT license. The empty puzzle establishes the idea of interchangeable interfaces. On the first click the core modules arrive in a shuffled order. This board illustrates available module choices, not the exact dependency graph.\n\nThe core framework brings together loading, T1/M0 mapping, signal-to-concentration conversion, input functions, tissue regions, normalisation, kinetic models, summaries and diagnostics. Quality control and provenance support the workflow. Diffusion is deliberately held back for the expansion example.\n\nThree built-in pieces lift out: the kinetic model, signal-to-concentration conversion and tissue segmentation. Three orange custom implementations take their places. Built-in kinetic choices include Patlak, Tikhonov deconvolution and extended Tofts. A custom provider must implement the relevant interface so the surrounding workflow can reuse its outputs.\n\nNow add capabilities alongside the existing pieces. Diffusion, tractography and structural connectivity form an additional column; none of the core or custom pieces is replaced. This is the expansion example. The camera remains fixed so the distinction between swapping a method and extending the framework is visible.\n\nLift out the input-function piece and seat the orange robot piece in its place. This robot represents the CNN vessel detector used for input extraction, distinct from the optional text assistant in the next section. The next click opens the actual input-function localisation figure over the still-visible framework.\n\nThe supplied supplementary figure shows venous input localisation in the superior sagittal sinus. The CNN localises the vessel on the bolus-peak concentration frame. A maximum-signal voxel within it supplies the plotted double-bolus concentration–time curve. The venous curve is then time-shifted to arterial bolus arrival to form the effective input used for modelling. The diagram stays paused behind this aside. The next click removes the figure.\n\nThe input-function figure disappears and the framework returns with the CNN robot module seated. Pause here. The next click illustrates another input-function method using the same module slot.\n\nThe CNN input-function piece lifts out and a Cluster analysis piece seats in the same slot. Three groups of raised dots depict grouping similar observations. This is an illustrative alternative input-function implementation, not a claim that the article validates a built-in clustering provider. The surrounding modules and camera remain in place.\n\nSources: article_pBrain/pbrain_body.tex, framework and workflow sections; https://doi.org/10.1002/mrm.70554; https://github.com/edtireli/p-brain (commit c3201686b67ec159fc739b4cf8cd2a7b6a8881d3), pbrain/_assist.py, pbrain/_ollama.py and pbrain/io/layout.py. Optional text assistance is documented in the current repository; it is not part of the article’s estimator-validation claim.","speakerCues":["p-Brain is open source under the MIT license. The empty puzzle establishes the idea of interchangeable interfaces. On the first click the core modules arrive in a shuffled order. This board illustrates available module choices, not the exact dependency graph.","The core framework brings together loading, T1/M0 mapping, signal-to-concentration conversion, input functions, tissue regions, normalisation, kinetic models, summaries and diagnostics. Quality control and provenance support the workflow. Diffusion is deliberately held back for the expansion example.","Three built-in pieces lift out: the kinetic model, signal-to-concentration conversion and tissue segmentation. Three orange custom implementations take their places. Built-in kinetic choices include Patlak, Tikhonov deconvolution and extended Tofts. A custom provider must implement the relevant interface so the surrounding workflow can reuse its outputs.","Now add capabilities alongside the existing pieces. Diffusion, tractography and structural connectivity form an additional column; none of the core or custom pieces is replaced. This is the expansion example. The camera remains fixed so the distinction between swapping a method and extending the framework is visible.","Lift out the input-function piece and seat the orange robot piece in its place. This robot represents the CNN vessel detector used for input extraction, distinct from the optional text assistant in the next section. The next click opens the actual input-function localisation figure over the still-visible framework.","The supplied supplementary figure shows venous input localisation in the superior sagittal sinus. The CNN localises the vessel on the bolus-peak concentration frame. A maximum-signal voxel within it supplies the plotted double-bolus concentration–time curve. The venous curve is then time-shifted to arterial bolus arrival to form the effective input used for modelling. The diagram stays paused behind this aside. The next click removes the figure.","The input-function figure disappears and the framework returns with the CNN robot module seated. Pause here. The next click illustrates another input-function method using the same module slot.","The CNN input-function piece lifts out and a Cluster analysis piece seats in the same slot. Three groups of raised dots depict grouping similar observations. This is an illustrative alternative input-function implementation, not a claim that the article validates a built-in clustering provider. The surrounding modules and camera remain in place."]},{"id":"pbrain-input-workflow","order":107,"subchapter":"Paper 1","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":520,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"pbrain-puzzle","mode":"inputs","at":"art","args":{"revealSteps":6},"minimalFooter":true}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":95,"notes":"Footer captions removed for the live presentation, except the two highlighted module prompts. The manuscript and puzzle labels remain part of the diagram.\n\nData may arrive in flat NIfTI folders, BIDS layouts or scanner exports containing PAR/REC and DICOM. JSON sidecars hold metadata. BIDS is a folder convention, not a fourth imaging format. The three folders are alternative illustrative datasets, not a proposal to merge different subjects in one run.\n\nThe folders move into place and the Load, method-choice and Analysis pieces appear. No connections are drawn and no methods are selected. Pause here to explain the structure. The next presenter click starts the manual file-mapping demonstration.\n\nFor an unfamiliar or custom layout, the user maps files to DCE, anatomical T1, inversion recovery and metadata. The pointer wires these examples one by one into Load. The scene then pauses: no downstream method connections are drawn and no methods are selected. Standard supported file layouts already have deterministic discovery. NIfTI passes through; PAR/REC and DICOM are converted with dcm2niix. The next click starts the method choices.\n\nWith the inputs loaded, choose the three downstream methods: T1/M0 fitting, signal-to-concentration conversion and kinetic models. The illustrated choices are inversion recovery, saturation recovery and Patlak plus Tikhonov. These are examples, not universally suitable defaults: the acquisition, research question and required outputs guide the selection. Only after the method choices are complete does the arrow continue to analysis.\n\nReplace Load with the optional local AI-assisted setup interface and pause with the robot piece seated. The three ivory method pieces retain the researcher’s study settings. The next presenter click starts the input-mapping proposal. The public assistant proposes DCE, anatomical T1 and inversion-recovery series from scan headers, or subject/cohort folder layouts and input paths from the file tree. It does not select the T1/M0 fitter, signal-to-concentration converter or kinetic models; these come from configuration, profiles, CLI options or defaults. It does not perform quantitative analysis.\n\nThe next click rapidly proposes file-role mappings. Review the input mappings at Confirm before loading and analysis. The three method pieces remain ivory and labeled Study setting: the assistant has not changed them. JSON sidecars supply acquisition metadata through the normal loader; JSON is not an extra role returned by the LLM schema. The timing is illustrative, not a runtime benchmark. The current assistant also explains errors, summarises QC and drafts Methods text from provenance; these are separate text features.\n\nThe next click, or the Confirm tile, approves the illustrated input mappings, then the normal quantitative pipeline runs with the retained study configuration. Confirmed folder mappings can be saved to pbrain.layout.toml; method choices are supplied through study configuration or command-line options and recorded in run provenance. File layout and method configuration are distinct. This is a workflow demonstration, not a live run. The local text assistant is separate from the vision-based AIF localiser.\n\nSources: article_pBrain/pbrain_body.tex, framework and workflow sections; https://doi.org/10.1002/mrm.70554; https://github.com/edtireli/p-brain (commit c3201686b67ec159fc739b4cf8cd2a7b6a8881d3), pbrain/_assist.py, pbrain/_ollama.py and pbrain/io/layout.py. Optional text assistance is documented in the current repository; it is not part of the article’s estimator-validation claim.","speakerCues":["Data may arrive in flat NIfTI folders, BIDS layouts or scanner exports containing PAR/REC and DICOM. JSON sidecars hold metadata. BIDS is a folder convention, not a fourth imaging format. The three folders are alternative illustrative datasets, not a proposal to merge different subjects in one run.","The folders move into place and the Load, method-choice and Analysis pieces appear. No connections are drawn and no methods are selected. Pause here to explain the structure. The next presenter click starts the manual file-mapping demonstration.","For an unfamiliar or custom layout, the user maps files to DCE, anatomical T1, inversion recovery and metadata. The pointer wires these examples one by one into Load. The scene then pauses: no downstream method connections are drawn and no methods are selected. Standard supported file layouts already have deterministic discovery. NIfTI passes through; PAR/REC and DICOM are converted with dcm2niix. The next click starts the method choices.","With the inputs loaded, choose the three downstream methods: T1/M0 fitting, signal-to-concentration conversion and kinetic models. The illustrated choices are inversion recovery, saturation recovery and Patlak plus Tikhonov. These are examples, not universally suitable defaults: the acquisition, research question and required outputs guide the selection. Only after the method choices are complete does the arrow continue to analysis.","Replace Load with the optional local AI-assisted setup interface and pause with the robot piece seated. The three ivory method pieces retain the researcher’s study settings. The next presenter click starts the input-mapping proposal. The public assistant proposes DCE, anatomical T1 and inversion-recovery series from scan headers, or subject/cohort folder layouts and input paths from the file tree. It does not select the T1/M0 fitter, signal-to-concentration converter or kinetic models; these come from configuration, profiles, CLI options or defaults. It does not perform quantitative analysis.","The next click rapidly proposes file-role mappings. Review the input mappings at Confirm before loading and analysis. The three method pieces remain ivory and labeled Study setting: the assistant has not changed them. JSON sidecars supply acquisition metadata through the normal loader; JSON is not an extra role returned by the LLM schema. The timing is illustrative, not a runtime benchmark. The current assistant also explains errors, summarises QC and drafts Methods text from provenance; these are separate text features.","The next click, or the Confirm tile, approves the illustrated input mappings, then the normal quantitative pipeline runs with the retained study configuration. Confirmed folder mappings can be saved to pbrain.layout.toml; method choices are supplied through study configuration or command-line options and recorded in run provenance. File layout and method configuration are distinct. This is a workflow demonstration, not a live run. The local text assistant is separate from the vision-based AIF localiser."],"backup":true},{"id":"pbrain-paper-patient-study","order":26.7,"subchapter":"p-Brain · a patient-study paper","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":520,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"pbrain-puzzle","mode":"paper","scenario":"patients","at":"art","args":{"revealSteps":3},"minimalFooter":true}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":45,"notes":"Footer captions removed for the live presentation, except the two highlighted module prompts. The manuscript and puzzle labels remain part of the diagram.\n\nA second researcher is applying p-Brain to a patient cohort. Their contribution is a study-specific analysis setup: an input function and tissue regions appropriate to their protocol. The manuscript starts blank. The three clicks type the paper, reveal both reusable contributions, and fit them into the framework.\n\nThe patient-study manuscript types rapidly. Its Methods section says that the patients were analysed with p-Brain using a custom input function and tissue regions. Both phrases are highlighted in orange. This is an illustrative study, with no sample size, clinical claims or fabricated findings.\n\nBoth highlighted phrases lift off the page and unfold into separate orange modules: Your input function and Your tissue regions. The researcher supplies compatible implementations or supported external inputs. The visual transformation is a metaphor for sharing these study choices as reusable components; p-Brain does not generate the code from the paper.\n\nThe p-Brain puzzle appears with two matching slots. The custom input function and tissue-region pieces seat in those slots. The remaining loading, T1/M0 mapping, signal conversion, kinetic-model fitting, summaries and diagnostics are shared. Custom providers must meet the relevant interface requirements. The board illustrates modular choices, not the exact execution dependency graph.\n\nSources: https://github.com/edtireli/p-brain/tree/c3201686b67ec159fc739b4cf8cd2a7b6a8881d3, README Add your own, pbrain/aif/ and pbrain/tissue_roi/. The patient-study manuscript is illustrative and contains no real patient results. It demonstrates custom input-function and tissue-region providers, not automatic code generation or a claim that every custom implementation is validated.","speakerCues":["A second researcher is applying p-Brain to a patient cohort. Their contribution is a study-specific analysis setup: an input function and tissue regions appropriate to their protocol. The manuscript starts blank. The three clicks type the paper, reveal both reusable contributions, and fit them into the framework.","The patient-study manuscript types rapidly. Its Methods section says that the patients were analysed with p-Brain using a custom input function and tissue regions. Both phrases are highlighted in orange. This is an illustrative study, with no sample size, clinical claims or fabricated findings.","Both highlighted phrases lift off the page and unfold into separate orange modules: Your input function and Your tissue regions. The researcher supplies compatible implementations or supported external inputs. The visual transformation is a metaphor for sharing these study choices as reusable components; p-Brain does not generate the code from the paper.","The p-Brain puzzle appears with two matching slots. The custom input function and tissue-region pieces seat in those slots. The remaining loading, T1/M0 mapping, signal conversion, kinetic-model fitting, summaries and diagnostics are shared. Custom providers must meet the relevant interface requirements. The board illustrates modular choices, not the exact execution dependency graph."]},{"id":"pbrain-publication","order":26.9,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":760,"layout":[". . .",". paper .",". . ."],"cols":"6vw 1fr 6vw","rows":"6vh 1fr 10vh","cellAlign":{"paper":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-wiley-header.png","alt":"Published p-Brain article in Magnetic Resonance in Medicine","at":"paper"}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":20,"notes":"Close the modularity story with the actual p-Brain publication. The preceding manuscript animations were illustrative researcher-use examples. This image is the published methods/framework article in Magnetic Resonance in Medicine, DOI 10.1002/mrm.70554. Next, show its voxelwise and anatomical output figures together."},{"id":"pbrain-tractography","order":108,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":760,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". . ."],"cols":"5vw 1fr 5vw","rows":"2vh auto 1fr 10vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Tractography and structural connectivity","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/defense/pbrain-tractography-figure.png","alt":"Article Figure 4: whole-brain tractography, structural connectome and selected brain-stem connections","at":"art"}],"source":"[@tireli2026pbrain]","seconds":30,"notes":"The optional diffusion-MRI track supplies whole-brain deterministic tractography and a structural connectome for the example subject. The top panel shows anterior and three-quarter views, with streamlines coloured by local orientation: red left–right, green anterior–posterior and blue inferior–superior. The lower panels show the parcel connectome and the connections of the selected brain-stem region. Chord colour represents log streamline count; streamline counts are not a direct count of axons or a measure of functional connectivity. The tracking mask and diffusion-space parcellation use SynthSeg. These are outputs from diffusion MRI, alongside the preceding DCE-MRI outputs. Source: article_pBrain/submission_MRM_R1/figures_for_production/Figure 4.pdf, full unmodified figure; article Figure 4 caption; DOI 10.1002/mrm.70554.","backup":true,"subchapter":"Paper 1"},{"id":"backup-cmro2-vb-relation","order":120,"backup":true,"subchapter":"Paper 3","style":"keynote","width":"wide","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":300,"layout":["head","definitions","geometry","scaling","extraction","surface","relation"],"rows":"auto auto auto auto auto auto auto","boxGap":"calc(10 * var(--u))","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"CMRO2 - vb relation","at":"head"},{"type":"math","at":"definitions","tex":"\\text{Radius only; fixed }N,L,P,f\\quad(P:\\mathrm{O_2})\\qquad 1:\\text{ controls}\\quad 2:\\text{ PPCS}"},{"type":"math","at":"geometry","tex":"V_b=N\\pi r^2L,\\qquad S=N\\cdot2\\pi rL"},{"type":"math","at":"scaling","tex":"V_b\\propto r^2,\\qquad S\\propto r","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"extraction","tex":"E=1-e^{-PS/f}","revealAt":2,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"surface","tex":"S=-\\frac{f}{P}\\ln(1-E)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\frac{S_2}{S_1}=\\frac{\\ln(1-E_2)}{\\ln(1-E_1)}","revealAt":3,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"relation","tex":"\\frac{V_{b,2}}{V_{b,1}}=\\left(\\frac{r_2}{r_1}\\right)^2=\\left(\\frac{S_2}{S_1}\\right)^2=\\left[\\frac{\\ln(1-E_2)}{\\ln(1-E_1)}\\right]^2","revealAt":4,"enter":"fade"}],"seconds":90,"notes":"The geometry is present on arrival. Each Next fades in one new equation, retaining every earlier line in its original position. Click 1: fixed capillary number and length give different radius scaling for volume and surface. Click 2: use the idealised oxygen Crone–Renkin relation. Click 3: rearrange the relation and compare groups under equal P and f. Click 4: radius alone links the surface ratio to the squared blood-volume ratio. The intermediate S = -(f/P) ln(1-E) is an explicit algebraic step between Appendix A equations (3) and (4).\n\nSource: [local source path omitted]"},{"id":"backup-cmro2-vb-relation-values","order":121,"backup":true,"subchapter":"Paper 3","style":"keynote","width":"wide","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":300,"layout":["head","definitions","relation","inputs","radius","volume","comparison"],"rows":"auto auto auto auto auto auto auto","boxGap":"calc(10 * var(--u))","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"CMRO2 - vb relation","at":"head"},{"type":"math","at":"definitions","tex":"\\text{Radius only; fixed }N,L,P,f\\quad(P:\\mathrm{O_2})\\qquad 1:\\text{ controls}\\quad 2:\\text{ PPCS}"},{"type":"math","at":"relation","tex":"\\frac{V_{b,2}}{V_{b,1}}=\\left(\\frac{r_2}{r_1}\\right)^2=\\left(\\frac{S_2}{S_1}\\right)^2=\\left[\\frac{\\ln(1-E_2)}{\\ln(1-E_1)}\\right]^2"},{"type":"math","at":"inputs","tex":"E_1\\approx0.32,\\qquad E_2\\approx0.28","revealAt":1,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"radius","tex":"\\frac{S_2}{S_1}=\\frac{r_2}{r_1}=\\frac{\\ln(1-0.28)}{\\ln(1-0.32)}=\\frac{\\ln0.72}{\\ln0.68}\\approx0.852","revealAt":2,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"volume","tex":"\\frac{V_{b,2}}{V_{b,1}}\\approx0.852^2\\approx0.726","revealAt":3,"enter":"fade"},{"type":"math","at":"comparison","tex":"\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\Delta r}{r_1}\\approx-15\\%,\\qquad\\frac{\\Delta V_b}{V_{b,1}}\\approx-27\\%\\quad\\text{(model)}\\\\[0.25em]\\frac{\\Delta v_b}{v_{b,1}}\\approx-20\\%\\quad\\text{(DCE)}\\end{gathered}","revealAt":4,"enter":"fade"}],"seconds":90,"notes":"Keep the derived relation on arrival, then substitute the Appendix A extraction values on Next. Click 1: controls approximately 0.32 and PPCS patients approximately 0.28. Click 2: the surface/radius ratio is approximately 0.852. Click 3: squaring gives approximately 0.726, or a 27% lower model capillary volume. Click 4: a roughly 15% lower radius and 27% lower exchanging capillary volume are compared with the roughly 20% relative DCE-derived v_b reduction reported in the supplement. Here Δ means the group-2 minus group-1 change; the DCE percentage is relative to its control estimate. The magnitudes are of the same order, not an exact numerical match. These are rounded group values used for plausibility, not a fitted or validated mechanistic model.\n\nSource: [local source path omitted]"},{"id":"study-ii-literature","order":42.2,"subchapter":"Study II · results and interpretation","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I","Study II","Study III"],"alt":"Full interactive literature network with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links","states":[{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["adultCivilianPpcs"],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"groupLabel":"Adult clinical PCS/PPCS","audienceStory":{"title":"Adult clinical PCS/PPCS","points":["Persistent symptoms or a **clinical PCS diagnosis**.","Predominantly **civilian clinical and community cohorts**."],"position":"below"},"speakingPrompt":{"title":"Adult clinical PCS/PPCS","lines":["Persistent symptoms or a **clinical PCS diagnosis**.","Predominantly **civilian clinical and community cohorts**."]},"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":["humans"],"countScope":"Publications in this map","say":"We return to the sixty-one adult clinical PCS and PPCS papers in this map.","studyMarkers":[]},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"studiesOnly":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052)."],"highlights":["cortical grey matter","grey–white matter interface","all seven tissue compartments","symptom scores (RPQ)","fatigue"],"citations":[]},"say":"Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052)."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"studiesOnly":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052).","Blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments."],"highlights":["cortical grey matter","grey–white matter interface","all seven tissue compartments","symptom scores (RPQ)","fatigue"],"citations":[]},"say":"Blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"studiesOnly":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052).","Blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","No tissue-level association with symptom scores (RPQ) or fatigue survived correction for multiple comparisons."],"highlights":["cortical grey matter","grey–white matter interface","all seven tissue compartments","symptom scores (RPQ)","fatigue"],"citations":[]},"say":"No tissue-level association with symptom scores (RPQ) or fatigue survived correction for multiple comparisons."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"paperSelection":["Yoen2021"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Yoen2021"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052).","Blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","No tissue-level association with symptom scores (RPQ) or fatigue survived correction for multiple comparisons."],"highlights":["cortical grey matter","grey–white matter interface","all seven tissue compartments","symptom scores (RPQ)","fatigue"],"citations":[]},"say":"How does this compare with the literature? Yoen and colleagues published a close clinical comparison in 2021."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"paperSelection":["Yoen2021"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Yoen2021"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052).","Blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","No tissue-level association with symptom scores (RPQ) or fatigue survived correction for multiple comparisons."],"highlights":["cortical grey matter","grey–white matter interface","all seven tissue compartments","symptom scores (RPQ)","fatigue"],"citations":[]},"say":"Yoen compared 42 PCS patients after mild TBI with 29 non-trauma clinic patients, mostly with headaches. With automatic tissue segmentation and Patlak they found higher cortical transfer and lower plasma volume in cerebellar white matter and brainstem. RPQ correlations were not significant. Their comparison group was clinically symptomatic. Extended Tofts–Kermode gave different results, with lower brainstem transfer and no significant increase; an exploratory attention subgroup had higher cortical Patlak transfer. These details and uncorrected comparisons qualify the comparison.","paperCallout":{"node":"Yoen2021","author":"Yoen et al. · 2021","url":"https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016","labels":["Cohort","Methods","Results","Symptoms"],"paragraphs":["42 PCS patients after mTBI; 29 non-trauma clinic patients, mostly with headaches. Median scan: 2 months after injury.","DCE-MRI with automatic segmentation, Patlak and extended Tofts–Kermode models.","Patlak: higher cortical transfer and lower plasma volume in cerebellar white matter and brainstem.","No significant symptom correlations (RPQ; n = 18)."]}},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Interpretation","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Lower blood volume could reflect fewer perfused capillaries, reduced capillary recruitment, or altered vascular tone."],"highlights":["fewer perfused capillaries","reduced capillary recruitment","altered vascular tone"],"citations":[]},"say":"Lower blood volume could reflect fewer perfused capillaries, reduced capillary recruitment, or altered vascular tone.","paperSelection":["Yoen2021"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Yoen2021"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":false},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Interpretation","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Lower blood volume could reflect fewer perfused capillaries, reduced capillary recruitment, or altered vascular tone.","Present data cannot separate these possibilities, but the compartment-wide distribution makes a single focal lesion pattern unlikely."],"highlights":["fewer perfused capillaries","reduced capillary recruitment","altered vascular tone","cannot separate these possibilities","single focal lesion pattern unlikely"],"citations":[]},"say":"Present data cannot separate these possibilities, but the compartment-wide distribution makes a single focal lesion pattern unlikely.","paperSelection":["Yoen2021"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Yoen2021"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":false},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Interpretation","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Lower blood volume could reflect fewer perfused capillaries, reduced capillary recruitment, or altered vascular tone.","Present data cannot separate these possibilities, but the compartment-wide distribution makes a single focal lesion pattern unlikely.","Higher cortical tracer influx is consistent with increased BBB permeability long after injury. It may reflect persistent barrier dysfunction, but without pre-injury measurements we cannot establish whether the injury caused it."],"highlights":["fewer perfused capillaries","reduced capillary recruitment","altered vascular tone","cannot separate these possibilities","single focal lesion pattern unlikely","increased BBB permeability","cannot establish whether the injury caused it"],"citations":[]},"say":"Higher cortical tracer influx is consistent with increased BBB permeability long after injury. It may reflect persistent barrier dysfunction, but without pre-injury measurements we cannot establish whether the injury caused it.","paperSelection":["Yoen2021"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Yoen2021"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":false}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"overviewTour":true,"paperSequence":false,"populationTour":false,"speakingPrompts":true,"plainHeadings":true,"showMapLabels":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","populationFlow":"nested-human-pcs-v1","discussionMap":true}],"seconds":100,"notes":"Cumulative results, followed by Yoen’s node, then its card. Retain both studies during cumulative interpretation. All inflammatory and upstream mechanistic discussion is deferred to the unified conclusion after Study III.\n\nYoen full PDF reread, including all model comparisons and symptom results: qa/bbb-discussion-revision-2026-09-09/yoen-reread.json. Controls: 25 headaches, 2 memory complaints, 1 visual disturbance, 1 dizziness, without trauma. Patlak cortical Ktrans p=.042; cerebellar-WM vp p=.009; brainstem vp p=.011. Extended Tofts has lower brainstem Ktrans p=.035, with no Ktrans increase. RPQ n=18 continuous correlations null; categorical attention p=.041 exploratory. No multiplicity correction reported.\n\nOwn manuscript: Ki significant in cortical GM and GM/WM interface (q=.020 each); cerebral WM q=.052. Blood volume lower in all seven tissues after FDR. No tissue-level DCE/FSS/RPQ association survived correction. Do not claim every parcel has increased Ki or that no symptom relationship can exist.\n\n0. We return to the sixty-one adult clinical PCS and PPCS papers in this map.\n\n1. Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052).\n\n2. Blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.\n\n3. No tissue-level association with symptom scores (RPQ) or fatigue survived correction for multiple comparisons.\n\n4. How does this compare with the literature? Yoen and colleagues published a close clinical comparison in 2021.\n\n5. Yoen compared 42 PCS patients after mild TBI with 29 non-trauma clinic patients, mostly with headaches. With automatic tissue segmentation and Patlak they found higher cortical transfer and lower plasma volume in cerebellar white matter and brainstem. RPQ correlations were not significant. Their comparison group was clinically symptomatic. Extended Tofts–Kermode gave different results, with lower brainstem transfer and no significant increase; an exploratory attention subgroup had higher cortical Patlak transfer. These details and uncorrected comparisons qualify the comparison.\n\n6. Lower blood volume could reflect fewer perfused capillaries, reduced capillary recruitment, or altered vascular tone.\n\n7. Present data cannot separate these possibilities, but the compartment-wide distribution makes a single focal lesion pattern unlikely.\n\n8. Higher cortical tracer influx is consistent with increased BBB permeability long after injury. It may reflect persistent barrier dysfunction, but without pre-injury measurements we cannot establish whether the injury caused it.","stepNotes":["We return to the sixty-one adult clinical PCS and PPCS papers in this map.","Tracer influx was higher in cortical grey matter and at the grey–white matter interface. The cerebral white-matter result was borderline after correction (q = 0.052).","Blood volume was reduced across all seven tissue compartments.","No tissue-level association with symptom scores (RPQ) or fatigue survived correction for multiple comparisons.","How does this compare with the literature? Yoen and colleagues published a close clinical comparison in 2021.","Yoen compared 42 PCS patients after mild TBI with 29 non-trauma clinic patients, mostly with headaches. With automatic tissue segmentation and Patlak they found higher cortical transfer and lower plasma volume in cerebellar white matter and brainstem. RPQ correlations were not significant. Their comparison group was clinically symptomatic. Extended Tofts–Kermode gave different results, with lower brainstem transfer and no significant increase; an exploratory attention subgroup had higher cortical Patlak transfer. These details and uncorrected comparisons qualify the comparison.","Lower blood volume could reflect fewer perfused capillaries, reduced capillary recruitment, or altered vascular tone.","Present data cannot separate these possibilities, but the compartment-wide distribution makes a single focal lesion pattern unlikely.","Higher cortical tracer influx is consistent with increased BBB permeability long after injury. It may reflect persistent barrier dysfunction, but without pre-injury measurements we cannot establish whether the injury caused it."]},{"id":"study-iii-literature","order":47.2,"subchapter":"Study III · results and interpretation","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I","Study II","Study III"],"alt":"Full interactive literature network with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links","states":[{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":["civilianPpcsOxygenFlow"],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"labelCohortPapers":false,"paperLabelNotes":{},"cohortColors":{"civilianPpcsOxygenFlow":"#f2bd76"},"countScope":"Publications in this map","withinCohorts":["civilianPpcsMetabolismFlow"],"studyMarkers":[],"groupLabel":"Oxygen metabolism and flow","speakingPrompt":{"title":"Oxygen metabolism and flow","lines":["Clinical PCS/PPCS: oxygen use, perfusion and vascular responses."]},"say":"We return to oxygen metabolism and blood flow within the adult clinical PCS/PPCS literature. These publications use different measures; they are not all direct measurements of oxygen consumption."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"studiesOnly":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Resting global CMRO₂ was about 12% lower, with no significant difference in resting global CBF."],"highlights":["12% lower","no significant difference","14% in PPCS","1% in controls","CBF rose","lactate rose"],"citations":[]},"say":"Resting global CMRO₂ was about 12% lower, with no significant difference in resting global CBF."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"studiesOnly":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Resting global CMRO₂ was about 12% lower, with no significant difference in resting global CBF.","During hypoxia, CMRO₂ fell by 14% in PPCS and 1% in controls; CBF rose in both groups."],"highlights":["12% lower","no significant difference","14% in PPCS","1% in controls","CBF rose","lactate rose"],"citations":[]},"say":"During hypoxia, CMRO₂ fell by 14% in PPCS and 1% in controls; CBF rose in both groups."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"studiesOnly":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Resting global CMRO₂ was about 12% lower, with no significant difference in resting global CBF.","During hypoxia, CMRO₂ fell by 14% in PPCS and 1% in controls; CBF rose in both groups.","Precuneus lactate rose in both groups, with no significant difference in its hypoxic response."],"highlights":["12% lower","no significant difference","14% in PPCS","1% in controls","CBF rose","lactate rose"],"citations":[]},"say":"Precuneus lactate rose in both groups, with no significant difference in its hypoxic response."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"paperSelection":["PMID12607977"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["PMID12607977"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":true,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Resting global CMRO₂ was about 12% lower, with no significant difference in resting global CBF.","During hypoxia, CMRO₂ fell by 14% in PPCS and 1% in controls; CBF rose in both groups.","Precuneus lactate rose in both groups, with no significant difference in its hypoxic response."],"highlights":["12% lower","no significant difference","14% in PPCS","1% in controls","CBF rose","lactate rose"],"citations":[]},"say":"Kabasawa offers a comparison with our resting finding: oxygen PET in chronic mild TBI found low regional oxygen consumption in many patients while blood flow remained within reference ranges."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"paperSelection":["PMID12607977"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["PMID12607977"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":false,"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Results","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Resting global CMRO₂ was about 12% lower, with no significant difference in resting global CBF.","During hypoxia, CMRO₂ fell by 14% in PPCS and 1% in controls; CBF rose in both groups.","Precuneus lactate rose in both groups, with no significant difference in its hypoxic response."],"highlights":["12% lower","no significant difference","14% in PPCS","1% in controls","CBF rose","lactate rose"],"citations":[]},"paperCallout":{"node":"PMID12607977","author":"Kabasawa et al. · 2002","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12607977/","paragraphs":["Regional oxygen consumption was below normal limits in many patients, while CBF remained within reference ranges.","¹⁵O PET in 20 patients with cognitive difficulties and normal MRI.","Chronic phase after mTBI."],"labels":["Low CMRO₂, normal-range CBF","Patients","Timing"]},"say":"Kabasawa studied twenty patients in the chronic phase after mild TBI, with cognitive difficulties and normal MRI. Oxygen PET found low regional oxygen consumption in many patients, while blood flow remained within reference ranges. This is a comparison with our resting result."},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Interpretation","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Lower resting CMRO₂ alone could reflect lower oxygen-use capacity, lower demand, or both."],"highlights":["lower oxygen-use capacity","lower demand","production, transport, oxidation and clearance"],"citations":[]},"say":"Kabasawa concerns resting oxygen consumption. Our study also tests what happens when oxygen availability is reduced. Lower resting CMRO₂ alone could reflect lower oxygen-use capacity, lower demand, or both.","paperSelection":["PMID12607977"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["PMID12607977"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":false},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"Interpretation","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Lower resting CMRO₂ alone could reflect lower oxygen-use capacity, lower demand, or both.","Lactate concentration cannot separate production, transport, oxidation and clearance, or identify the step limiting oxygen use."],"highlights":["lower oxygen-use capacity","lower demand","production, transport, oxidation and clearance"],"citations":[]},"say":"Lactate concentration cannot separate production, transport, oxidation and clearance, or identify the step limiting oxygen use.","paperSelection":["PMID12607977"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["PMID12607977"],"paperColor":"#f2bd76","referenceReveal":false}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"overviewTour":true,"paperSequence":false,"populationTour":false,"speakingPrompts":true,"plainHeadings":true,"showMapLabels":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","populationFlow":"nested-human-pcs-v1","discussionMap":true}],"seconds":140,"notes":"Main comparison: Kabasawa only. Eight states: opening map, three cumulative own results, Kabasawa node emerges, Kabasawa card, two cumulative interpretation steps. Our results remain visible during the comparison. Shiga, Abiko and Champagne remain in the full literature network and the source notes for questions, without main-talk cards or highlighted labels. Cohort membership and counts remain unchanged. Kabasawa is a related regional resting result, not a direct replication of global chronic PPCS or hypoxic reactivity. Exact numeric time since injury remains unverified; show chronic phase only.\n\nSOURCE REVIEW\n\nKabasawa used oxygen-15 gas PET in twenty patients with chronic mTBI, cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI. Regional oxygen consumption was below the normal limit in more than half the patients in every sampled region except the right occipital lobe, while CBF and OEF remained within normal ranges. This is a useful comparison with the resting result. The full Japanese paper has not been obtained; ages, recruitment and original injury criteria are not established by the primary English abstract.\n\nShiga compared ten symptomatic TBI patients at least one year after injury with ten volunteers. All patients had regions of low oxygen consumption despite normal MRI. Six also had reduced carbon-11 flumazenil binding. Flumazenil binds the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors; reduced binding measures lower binding-site availability and does not by itself prove neuronal death. Fourteen regions with low CMRO2 had no binding abnormality. The primary abstract does not establish mild-only injury, and the full paper remains unavailable.\n\nAbiko measured regional oxygen consumption with oxygen-15 gas PET in twelve patients with disability after traffic accidents. Lower IQ correlated with higher CMRO2 in right Brodmann areas 44 and 45. This is an association across patients, not evidence that global oxygen consumption was elevated or reduced compared with controls. The corresponding full author-thesis chapter includes eleven adults and one eleven-year-old; the initial injury severity is not established as mild-only.\n\nChampagne used dual-calibrated ASL and BOLD MRI in fifteen recently concussed athletes and twenty-seven athlete controls. Regional resting CMRO2 was lower, with no significant oxygen-extraction difference and higher vascular reactivity. Their CBF difference was no longer significant after carbon-dioxide adjustment. Regions were selected by the CBF group difference. This is a related finding in an acute sports population, rather than a replication of our global chronic result.\n\nSource access: Kabasawa and Shiga primary indexed abstracts rechecked; their full journal texts remain unavailable. Abiko full corresponding author-thesis chapter read (qa/ppcs-final-source-audit-2026-09-09/sources/Abiko2018-thesis.txt); final journal PDF not obtained. Champagne full PDF/text read (qa/defense-refinements-2026-09-08/conclusion-sources/Champagne2021.txt). Do not describe the four papers as independent replications in the same population.\n\nFlumazenil interpretation: [11C]FMZ binds the benzodiazepine site of susceptible GABA-A receptor subtypes. Binding potential reflects available receptors and affinity, not a direct neuron count or a measure of GABA concentration. Reduced binding can accompany neuronal loss, altered receptor expression or affinity. Longitudinal primary TBI study discusses these alternatives and observed increases during recovery: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9253887/ (doi:10.1093/braincomms/fcac159). No neuronal-death inference is made in the audience-facing card.\n\nOwn resting CMRO₂: adjusted difference −16.8 µmol/100 g/min (95% CI −28.2 to −5.3; p=.004). Approximately 12% is the adjusted difference relative to the control mean 134.8. Raw means 114.0 versus 134.8 should not be used to derive 12%. Resting global CBF difference p=.31: lack of significance is not equivalence and does not establish normal regional or microvascular oxygen delivery.\n\nOwn hypoxia: descriptive CMRO₂ changes−14.4% in PPCS and−0.9% in controls; adjusted Group×SaO₂ interaction p=.039. CBF rose 18.3% versus 20.8%; interaction p=.714. The outcome and SaO₂ predictor are mathematically related. These measurements cannot isolate demand, oxygen exchange and cellular oxygen-use capacity.\n\nLactate: single-voxel precuneus¹H-MRS concentration, not whole-brain glycolytic flux. Resting group difference p=.18; hypoxic rises 49.5% in PPCS and 63.5% in controls; interaction p=.801. Do not claim this excludes glycolytic compensation or diagnoses defective lactate shuttling.\n\nCohort:45 PPCS and 47 controls. Per-metric samples: normoxic CBF/CMRO₂ 41 PPCS and 47 controls; reactivity 39 and 47; lactate at rest 38 and 17; lactate reactivity 35 and 16. Median 29 months since injury (IQR 19–47). FixedHb 9.3 mmol/L; patients were older and more often female. Main models adjusted for age and sex.\n\nChampagne is outside adultCivilianPpcs:15 athletes scanned 8.0±4.6 days after concussion (range 1–17), versus 27 healthy athletes. Mean ages 21 versus 20; predominantly male. Do not count this paper within the chronic civilian subgroup.\n\nChampagne methods: dual-echo pCASL plus BOLD during hypercapnia (PETCO₂ target 10 mmHg above baseline) and hyperoxia (PETO₂ target 410 mmHg). Calibration assumes isometabolism, unlike our hypoxic stress experiment. Grey-matter ROIs were defined by group CBF differences before extracting other metrics.\n\nChampagneTable 3: CBF 76±17 versus 94±16 mL/100 g/min (p=.001), with group difference non-significant after PETCO₂adjustment(p=.134). RegionalCMRO₂ 165±38 versus 222±69 µmol/100 g/min(p=.006); no CO₂-adjusted CMRO₂ test reported. OEF.30±.11 versus.31±.11(p=.800; adjustedp=.209). CVR 8.3±1.5 versus 6.6±1.3%ΔCBF/mmHg(p=.001). These findings do not show preserved perfusion or compensatory OEF.\n\nKeep symptom/exercise analyses in existing results/backup to avoid another detour: no RPQ association at rest or reactivity; FSS reactivity p=.053, non-significant. Exercise intolerance associated with lower resting CMRO₂(p=.019, uncorrected secondary analysis), not hypoxic reactivity.\n\nCHAMPAGNE GAS-PROTOCOL RECHECK, 9 September 2026. Full publisher PDF Methods, Figure1 and Tables1–3 re-read; pages3 and9 rendered and visually checked. Dual-echo pCASL acquired perfusion and BOLD simultaneously. Two separate hypercapnia and hyperoxia paradigms, not an induced hypocapnia protocol and not hypoxia. Hypercapnia target PETCO2 baseline+10mmHg at PETO2 110mmHg; hyperoxia target PETO2 410mmHg while holding PETCO2 at each participant's own baseline. Achieved CO2 rise approximately8mmHg. Calibration solves M and resting OEF and assumes unchanged CMRO2 during both challenges. Resting CMRO2 was subsequently estimated from resting CBF×arterial oxygen content×OEF. Therefore the gas manipulation does not turn their reported endpoint into a measurement of CMRO2 reactivity.\n\nRelative baseline hypocapnia is nevertheless relevant: concussion PETCO2 38±4mmHg vs controls41±3 in the hypercapnia run (p=.002); 38±3 vs41±3 in the hyperoxia run (p=.002). Avoid diagnosing pathological hypocapnia solely from those group averages. Baseline CBF p=.001 unadjusted, p=.134 after PETCO2 adjustment; CMRO2 p=.006, with both CO2-adjusted and confounding-effect entries explicitly N/A in Table3. This leaves the independence of the CMRO2 result from the baseline CO2 difference unestablished; it does not prove CO2 caused their CMRO2 result. Grey-matter comparison ROIs were selected for group CBF differences, and CMRO2 itself contains CBF, so this is not independent spatial confirmation. No hypoxic challenge and no measured hypoxic CMRO2 response. Keep as a qualified regional resting comparison in acute athletes; not direct validation of global chronic PPCS findings or oxygen-use reserve. DOI10.1007/s11682-019-00240-2; PMID32307673.\n\nKABASAWA TIMING RECHECK. Only chronic phase is verified for injury-to-first-scan timing in the primary 2002 abstract, PMID12607977. Its9.3-month figure is follow-up after the first assessment, not injury-to-scan time. The original journal archive requires membership; no full 2002 text was located. A2001 meeting abstract by Kabasawa and Ogawa (J-STAGE38 supplementS332, abstractII-J-54) reports mean6.4months since injury in20 patients. Similar design and authors do not establish identical cohorts, so6.4months is not attributed to2002 in the slide. Primary precursor archived in qa/kabasawa-timing-2026-09-09/; exact time after injury remains unverified for2002.","stepNotes":["We return to oxygen metabolism and blood flow within the adult clinical PCS/PPCS literature. These publications use different measures; they are not all direct measurements of oxygen consumption.","Resting global CMRO₂ was about 12% lower, with no significant difference in resting global CBF.","During hypoxia, CMRO₂ fell by 14% in PPCS and 1% in controls; CBF rose in both groups.","Precuneus lactate rose in both groups, with no significant difference in its hypoxic response.","Kabasawa offers a comparison with our resting finding: oxygen PET in chronic mild TBI found low regional oxygen consumption in many patients while blood flow remained within reference ranges.","Kabasawa studied twenty patients in the chronic phase after mild TBI, with cognitive difficulties and normal MRI. Oxygen PET found low regional oxygen consumption in many patients, while blood flow remained within reference ranges. This is a comparison with our resting result.","Kabasawa concerns resting oxygen consumption. Our study also tests what happens when oxygen availability is reduced. Lower resting CMRO₂ alone could reflect lower oxygen-use capacity, lower demand, or both.","Lactate concentration cannot separate production, transport, oxidation and clearance, or identify the step limiting oxygen use."],"omit":false},{"id":"study-ii-discussion","order":42.3,"width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"seconds":44,"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"Study II · Discussion","title":"Study II in context","cumulative":false,"sourceLabel":"","branches":[],"states":[{"quote":"reading-ready","visual":"text","layout":"bullets","quoteData":{"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[]},"evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":""},{"quote":"bbb-vs-yoen","quoteData":{"id":"bbb-vs-yoen","paragraphs":["Our cortical Kᵢ increase agrees with the cortical transfer finding in clinical PCS.","Our lower tracer-accessible blood volume extends across more tissue compartments."],"highlights":[["more tissue compartments","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"Study discussion","evidence":[{"id":"Yoen2021","author":"Yoen et al. · 2021","title":"BBB disruption in mTBI with post-concussion syndrome","population":"42 clinical PCS patients; 29 symptomatic controls; median 2 months after injury.","method":"DCE-MRI; Patlak and extended Tofts models.","finding":"Patlak cortical Ktrans was higher; plasma volume was lower in cerebellar white matter and brainstem.","caveat":"Retrospective; not all symptoms persisted ≥3 months; results depended on modelling; different distribution from our findings.","url":"https://doi.org/10.3348/kjr.2020.0016","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 16","reviewBasis":"Publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 1–5, 8, 10–12; Table 1 and Fig. 4","fullTitle":"Blood-Brain Barrier Disruption in Mild Traumatic Brain Injury Patients with Post-Concussion Syndrome: Evaluation with Region-Based Quantification of Dynamic Contrast-Enhanced MR Imaging Parameters Using Automatic Whole-Brain Segmentation.","paperId":"Yoen2021","pmid":"32783413"}],"say":"Yoen studied clinical PCS, at a median of two months. They also found higher cortical transfer, but lower plasma volume only in selected regions. Our blood-volume reduction extends across the tissue compartments.","notes":"Our cohort: adult non-athlete PPCS, median 28 months since latest mTBI. Yoen: 42 PCS patients and 29 symptomatic clinical controls, often with headache; median 2 months, including patients before 3 months. Cortical Patlak Ktrans P=.042 in pooled cohort; not independently significant within timing subgroups and absent with extended Tofts. Lower vp in cerebellar white matter and brainstem. Keep Ki/Ktrans and vb/vp notation distinct; this is a direction/distribution comparison, not equality of absolute values.","speakerWordCount":32},{"quote":"bbb-vs-yoo","quoteData":{"id":"bbb-vs-yoo","paragraphs":["Yoo found higher Ktrans in normal-appearing white matter in clinical PCS.","Our cerebral white-matter Kᵢ increase did not survive FDR correction."],"highlights":[["did not survive FDR correction","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"Study discussion","evidence":[{"id":"Yoo2019","author":"Yoo et al. · 2019","title":"Quantitative DCE-MRI in mTBI with post-concussion syndrome","population":"44 PCS patients and 32 controls.","method":"Regional DCE-MRI.","finding":"Ktrans and extracellular volume were higher in normal-appearing white matter and selected regions vulnerable to axonal injury.","caveat":"Abstract only: detailed age, symptom-duration and recruitment criteria were not verified; this is not evidence of diffuse capillary loss.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00330-018-5656-z","manuscriptReference":"BBB ref. 15","reviewBasis":"Publisher and indexed abstract; full text unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"Publisher abstract, Methods and Results","fullTitle":"Quantitative dynamic contrast-enhanced MR imaging shows widespread blood-brain barrier disruption in mild traumatic brain injury patients with post-concussion syndrome.","paperId":"Yoo2019","pmid":"30066251"}],"say":"The spatial pattern is not identical across studies. Yoo reported higher transfer in normal-appearing white matter in clinical PCS. In our study, the white-matter influx increase did not survive correction.","notes":"Yoo: 44 PCS patients and 32 controls, retrospective; primary abstract only. Also higher ve in normal-appearing white matter and predilection sites for axonal injury. Do not invent age, injury mechanism or minimum symptom duration. The contrast may reflect sampling, acquisition, regional definitions and model choice. A nonsignificant result is not evidence of absent white-matter pathology.","speakerWordCount":30}]}],"stepNotes":["","Yoen studied clinical PCS, at a median of two months. They also found higher cortical transfer, but lower plasma volume only in selected regions. Our blood-volume reduction extends across the tissue compartments.","The spatial pattern is not identical across studies. Yoo reported higher transfer in normal-appearing white matter in clinical PCS. In our study, the white-matter influx increase did not survive correction."],"notes":"bbb-vs-yoen\nYoen studied clinical PCS, at a median of two months. They also found higher cortical transfer, but lower plasma volume only in selected regions. Our blood-volume reduction extends across the tissue compartments.\nOur cohort: adult non-athlete PPCS, median 28 months since latest mTBI. Yoen: 42 PCS patients and 29 symptomatic clinical controls, often with headache; median 2 months, including patients before 3 months. Cortical Patlak Ktrans P=.042 in pooled cohort; not independently significant within timing subgroups and absent with extended Tofts. Lower vp in cerebellar white matter and brainstem. Keep Ki/Ktrans and vb/vp notation distinct; this is a direction/distribution comparison, not equality of absolute values.\n\nbbb-vs-yoo\nThe spatial pattern is not identical across studies. Yoo reported higher transfer in normal-appearing white matter in clinical PCS. In our study, the white-matter influx increase did not survive correction.\nYoo: 44 PCS patients and 32 controls, retrospective; primary abstract only. Also higher ve in normal-appearing white matter and predilection sites for axonal injury. Do not invent age, injury mechanism or minimum symptom duration. The contrast may reflect sampling, acquisition, regional definitions and model choice. A nonsignificant result is not evidence of absent white-matter pathology.","omit":true},{"id":"study-iii-discussion","order":47.3,"width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"seconds":44,"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"Study III · Discussion","title":"Study III in context","cumulative":false,"sourceLabel":"","branches":[],"states":[{"quote":"reading-ready","visual":"text","layout":"bullets","quoteData":{"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[]},"evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":""},{"quote":"oxygen-vs-champagne","quoteData":{"id":"oxygen-vs-champagne","paragraphs":["Champagne found lower regional CMRO₂ after sport concussion.","We find lower global CMRO₂ in non-athlete adults with PPCS."],"highlights":[["lower global CMRO₂","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"Study discussion","evidence":[{"id":"Champagne2021","author":"Champagne et al. · 2021","title":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion","population":"15 athletes approximately 8 days after concussion; 27 athlete controls.","method":"Dual-calibrated MRI; CMRO₂ analysis in regions selected for lower CBF.","finding":"Lower CMRO₂ in regions selected for lower CBF; no OEF difference.","caveat":"Acute athletes, not PPCS. The CBF difference was nonsignificant after CO₂ adjustment; the reported CMRO₂ comparison was not CO₂-adjusted.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11682-019-00240-2","manuscriptReference":"CMRO₂ ref. 11","reviewBasis":"Local supplied-reference publisher PDF extracted and read","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":"[local source path omitted]","highlights":[],"locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 5–10; Tables 1–3; Methods, Results and limitations","fullTitle":"Compromised resting cerebral metabolism after sport-related concussion: A calibrated MRI study.","paperId":"Champagne2021","pmid":"32307673","localSourcePath":"[local source path omitted]"}],"say":"Champagne found lower regional oxygen consumption in athletes about eight days after concussion. We find a global reduction in non-athlete adults with persistent symptoms, a median of twenty-nine months after injury.","notes":"15 athletes at 8.0 ± 4.6 days, 27 athlete controls. CMRO2 tested within regions selected for lower CBF. CBF P=.001 unadjusted and P=.134 after end-tidal CO2 adjustment; CMRO2 P=.006 not CO2-adjusted. Do not describe this as a preserved-CBF PPCS replication or a longitudinal demonstration that acute changes persist. Our lower resting OEF differs from their nonsignificant OEF contrast.","speakerWordCount":31},{"quote":"oxygen-vs-kabasawa","quoteData":{"id":"oxygen-vs-kabasawa","paragraphs":["Kabasawa found low regional CMRO₂ despite CBF within reference ranges.","Our global findings also separate oxygen use from total blood flow."],"highlights":[["oxygen use from total blood flow","marker"]]},"visual":"text","layout":"bullets","sourceLabel":"Study discussion","evidence":[{"id":"Kabasawa2002","author":"Kabasawa et al. · 2002","title":"Cerebral circulation and metabolism in chronic minor TBI with higher brain dysfunction","population":"20 chronic minor-TBI patients with cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI; 13 reassessed.","method":"Oxygen-15 gas PET; regional CBF, OEF and CMRO₂.","finding":"Regional CMRO₂ was below reference limits in many patients despite CBF within normal ranges; some regional values rose with cognitive improvement.","caveat":"English abstract only. Exact age range, civilian eligibility and detailed methods remain unverified; not a matched PPCS replication.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12607977/","manuscriptReference":"Additional primary source from targeted methods audit","reviewBasis":"Indexed primary English abstract; Japanese full text unavailable","txtPath":"[local source path omitted]","pdfPath":null,"highlights":[],"locator":"English abstract; 20-person PET study and 13-person follow-up","fullTitle":"[Cerebral circulation and metabolism in the patients with higher brain dysfunction caused by chronic minor traumatic brain injury: a study by the positron emission tomography in twenty subjects with normal MRI findings].","paperId":"PMID12607977","pmid":"12607977","displayPopulation":"Chronic minor TBI · cognitive dysfunction · ¹⁵O-PET","displayFinding":"Low regional CMRO₂ despite CBF within reference ranges.","displayCaveat":"20 patients; primary English abstract only."}],"say":"Kabasawa offers an earlier comparison in chronic minor brain injury with cognitive dysfunction. Regional oxygen consumption could be low while flow remained within reference ranges. Our global measurements show a related separation between flow and oxygen use.","notes":"20 chronic minor-TBI patients with cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI. English abstract only; detailed age range and civilian eligibility unverified. Regional CMRO2 below lower reference limit in more than half of subjects in each region except right occipital cortex. Reference-range assessment, not a matched healthy-control group effect. This is not an exact PPCS replication.","speakerWordCount":37}]}],"stepNotes":["","Champagne found lower regional oxygen consumption in athletes about eight days after concussion. We find a global reduction in non-athlete adults with persistent symptoms, a median of twenty-nine months after injury.","Kabasawa offers an earlier comparison in chronic minor brain injury with cognitive dysfunction. Regional oxygen consumption could be low while flow remained within reference ranges. Our global measurements show a related separation between flow and oxygen use."],"notes":"oxygen-vs-champagne\nChampagne found lower regional oxygen consumption in athletes about eight days after concussion. We find a global reduction in non-athlete adults with persistent symptoms, a median of twenty-nine months after injury.\n15 athletes at 8.0 ± 4.6 days, 27 athlete controls. CMRO2 tested within regions selected for lower CBF. CBF P=.001 unadjusted and P=.134 after end-tidal CO2 adjustment; CMRO2 P=.006 not CO2-adjusted. Do not describe this as a preserved-CBF PPCS replication or a longitudinal demonstration that acute changes persist. Our lower resting OEF differs from their nonsignificant OEF contrast.\n\noxygen-vs-kabasawa\nKabasawa offers an earlier comparison in chronic minor brain injury with cognitive dysfunction. Regional oxygen consumption could be low while flow remained within reference ranges. Our global measurements show a related separation between flow and oxygen use.\n20 chronic minor-TBI patients with cognitive dysfunction and normal MRI. English abstract only; detailed age range and civilian eligibility unverified. Regional CMRO2 below lower reference limit in more than half of subjects in each region except right occipital cortex. Reference-range assessment, not a matched healthy-control group effect. This is not an exact PPCS replication.","omit":true,"omitReason":"Integrated into the cumulative Study III map; upstream mechanisms moved to the unified conclusion."},{"id":"inflammation-hypothesis","order":51.2,"width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","layout":["art"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"art":"stretch"},"seconds":86,"blocks":[{"type":"evidence-network","at":"art","src":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json","hiddenNodes":["Study I","Study II","Study III"],"alt":"Full interactive literature network with precomputed source-text similarity and recorded citation links","states":[{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"How could the leak persist?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[],"citations":[],"topics":[]},"say":"How could the leak persist? These are proposed explanations, not mechanisms established by our measurements.","studiesOnly":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"How could the leak persist?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Persistent inflammation. CSF shows elevated neuroinflammatory markers (IL-2, IL-15, TNF-α, TNF-β, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC; uncorrected comparisons)."],"highlights":[],"citations":[{"author":"Neumann · 2023","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fneur.2023.1127708"},{"author":"Gard · 2023","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12974-023-02864-0"},{"author":"Chaban · 2020","url":"https://doi.org/10.1089/neu.2019.6963"}],"topics":[{"label":"Persistent inflammation","text":"CSF shows elevated neuroinflammatory markers (IL-2, IL-15, TNF-α, TNF-β, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC; uncorrected comparisons).","context":["Gard · human athletes with PPCS","Concussion · months–years (median 17 mo)"]}]},"say":"Gard found eight higher CSF markers in athletes with persistent symptoms, a median of 17 months after their last concussion: IL-2, IL-15, TNF-alpha, TNF-beta, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC. These were exploratory comparisons without multiple-comparison correction. Neumann and Chaban provide related TSPO and plasma evidence in different populations and time windows.","paperSelection":["Neumann2023","Gard2023","Chaban2020"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Neumann2023","Gard2023","Chaban2020"],"paperColor":"#94bbc9","referenceReveal":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"How could the leak persist?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Persistent inflammation. CSF shows elevated neuroinflammatory markers (IL-2, IL-15, TNF-α, TNF-β, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC; uncorrected comparisons).","Tight-junction remodelling. Claudin-5, occludin and ZO-1 seal endothelial junctions; lower levels leave a looser seal."],"highlights":[],"citations":[{"author":"Obermeier · 2013","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/nm.3407"},{"author":"Gowrikumar · 2025","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/21688370.2025.2470482"}],"topics":[{"label":"Persistent inflammation","text":"CSF shows elevated neuroinflammatory markers (IL-2, IL-15, TNF-α, TNF-β, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC; uncorrected comparisons).","context":["Gard · human athletes with PPCS","Concussion · months–years (median 17 mo)"]},{"label":"Tight-junction remodelling","text":"Claudin-5, occludin and ZO-1 seal endothelial junctions; lower levels leave a looser seal.","context":["Gowrikumar · mouse TBI + endothelial cells","TBI tissue: 4 hours–30 days"]}]},"say":"Claudin-5, occludin and ZO-1 normally seal endothelial junctions; lower levels after injury leave a looser seal. The thesis proposes junction remodelling as a possible molecular explanation. Claudin-1 can destabilise the normal complex in experimental work; this is not a literal one-for-one replacement of the seal.","paperSelection":["Obermeier2013","Gowrikumar2025"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Obermeier2013","Gowrikumar2025"],"paperColor":"#94bbc9","referenceReveal":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"How could the leak persist?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Persistent inflammation. CSF shows elevated neuroinflammatory markers (IL-2, IL-15, TNF-α, TNF-β, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC; uncorrected comparisons).","Tight-junction remodelling. Claudin-5, occludin and ZO-1 seal endothelial junctions; lower levels leave a looser seal.","Aquaporin-4. A water channel on astrocytic end-feet. Loss of its normal polarisation may impair clearance."],"highlights":[],"citations":[{"author":"Mestre · 2018","url":"https://doi.org/10.7554/elife.40070"},{"author":"Iliff · 2014","url":"https://doi.org/10.1523/jneurosci.3020-14.2014"},{"author":"Braun · 2024","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/brain/awae065"},{"author":"Rasmussen · 2018","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s1474-4422(18)30318-1"}],"topics":[{"label":"Persistent inflammation","text":"CSF shows elevated neuroinflammatory markers (IL-2, IL-15, TNF-α, TNF-β, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC; uncorrected comparisons).","context":["Gard · human athletes with PPCS","Concussion · months–years (median 17 mo)"]},{"label":"Tight-junction remodelling","text":"Claudin-5, occludin and ZO-1 seal endothelial junctions; lower levels leave a looser seal.","context":["Gowrikumar · mouse TBI + endothelial cells","TBI tissue: 4 hours–30 days"]},{"label":"Aquaporin-4","text":"A water channel on astrocytic end-feet. Loss of its normal polarisation may impair clearance.","context":["Mice: knockout / contusion / blast models","Mouse TBI: 1–28 days · human support"]}]},"say":"Aquaporin-4 is a water channel on astrocytic end-feet. Loss of its normal polarisation may impair clearance. This is another possible contributor to persistence; the present studies do not measure it.","paperSelection":["Mestre2018","Iliff2014TBI","Braun2024","Rasmussen2018"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Mestre2018","Iliff2014TBI","Braun2024","Rasmussen2018"],"paperColor":"#94bbc9","referenceReveal":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"What could reduce blood volume?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[],"citations":[],"topics":[]},"say":"What could reduce the tracer-accessible blood volume throughout the brain? The thesis distinguishes loss of vessels from loss of perfusion through vessels that are still there.","studiesOnly":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"What could reduce blood volume?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Thinner / fewer capillaries. Thinner or fewer capillaries reduce exchange surface and would lower vᵦ."],"highlights":[],"citations":[{"author":"van Dinther · 2022","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0271678x221076557"}],"topics":[{"label":"Thinner / fewer capillaries","text":"Thinner or fewer capillaries reduce exchange surface and would lower vᵦ.","context":["Human / animal microvascular disease","Ageing and chronic disease · review"]}]},"say":"Thinner or fewer capillaries reduce exchange surface and would lower tracer-accessible blood volume. These are different anatomical changes; rarefaction means fewer vessels. Neither was measured directly by DCE-MRI.","paperSelection":["vanDinther2022"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["vanDinther2022"],"paperColor":"#94bbc9","referenceReveal":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"What could reduce blood volume?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Thinner / fewer capillaries. Thinner or fewer capillaries reduce exchange surface and would lower vᵦ.","Capillary stalling. Capillary segments blocked by leukocytes or stationary blood cells would also lower vᵦ."],"highlights":[],"citations":[{"author":"Bracko 2021","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0271678x20982383"},{"author":"Choe 2022","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fncel.2022.848764"}],"topics":[{"label":"Thinner / fewer capillaries","text":"Thinner or fewer capillaries reduce exchange surface and would lower vᵦ.","context":["Human / animal microvascular disease","Ageing and chronic disease · review"]},{"label":"Capillary stalling","text":"Capillary segments blocked by leukocytes or stationary blood cells would also lower vᵦ.","context":["Bracko · Alzheimer’s mouse models","Chronic disease · real-time flow studies"]}]},"say":"Capillary segments blocked by leukocytes or stationary blood cells would also lower tracer-accessible blood volume if those segments become unavailable to the passing tracer. The blood can still be physically present: this is a perfusion mechanism, not necessarily anatomical vessel loss.","paperSelection":["Bracko2021","Choe2022"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Bracko2021","Choe2022"],"paperColor":"#94bbc9","referenceReveal":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"What could reduce oxygen use?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":[],"highlights":[],"citations":[],"topics":[]},"say":"Could a smaller functioning microvascular compartment also contribute to lower oxygen consumption? Then the two results need not require two independent lesions.","studiesOnly":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"What could reduce oxygen use?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Reduced exchange surface. If lower vᵦ reflects less capillary surface for oxygen exchange, extraction and CMRO₂ could fall despite preserved global flow."],"highlights":[],"citations":[{"author":"Thesis · §4.4 and capillary-radius calculation"},{"author":"Crone · 1963","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1748-1716.1963.tb02652.x"},{"author":"Renkin · 1959","url":"https://doi.org/10.1152/ajplegacy.1959.197.6.1205"}],"topics":[{"label":"Reduced exchange surface","text":"If lower vᵦ reflects less capillary surface for oxygen exchange, extraction and CMRO₂ could fall despite preserved global flow.","context":["Transport model · thesis interpretation","Conditional link between the two studies"]}]},"say":"If lower blood volume represents less exchange-relevant capillary surface, oxygen extraction can fall at a given global flow. CMRO₂ could then fall without requiring intrinsic mitochondrial failure. That is a conditional exchange argument: tracer-accessible blood volume is not a direct measure of exchange surface, and the classical extraction relation is a simplified transport model.","paperSelection":["Crone1963","Renkin1959","vanDinther2022"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Crone1963","Renkin1959","vanDinther2022"],"paperColor":"#94bbc9","referenceReveal":true},{"focus":"cohorts","cohorts":[],"title":false,"subtitle":false,"hiddenNodes":["Study II","Study III"],"cohortLegend":false,"cohortColors":{"adultCivilianPpcs":"#f2bd76"},"withinCohorts":[],"countScope":"Publications in this map","studyMarkers":[{"node":"Study II","label":"Study II","role":"","color":"#78d8ef"},{"node":"Study III","label":"Study III","role":"","color":"#f0805a"}],"discussionOverlay":{"title":"What could reduce oxygen use?","titleHighlight":true,"paragraphs":["Reduced exchange surface. If lower vᵦ reflects less capillary surface for oxygen exchange, extraction and CMRO₂ could fall despite preserved global flow.","Mitochondrial dysfunction. Glutamate release, ionic dysregulation and mitochondrial calcium influx can impair oxidative phosphorylation."],"highlights":[],"citations":[{"author":"Giza & Hovda · 2014","url":"https://doi.org/10.1227/neu.0000000000000505"}],"topics":[{"label":"Reduced exchange surface","text":"If lower vᵦ reflects less capillary surface for oxygen exchange, extraction and CMRO₂ could fall despite preserved global flow.","context":["Transport model · thesis interpretation","Conditional link between the two studies"]},{"label":"Mitochondrial dysfunction","text":"Glutamate release, ionic dysregulation and mitochondrial calcium influx can impair oxidative phosphorylation.","context":["Giza & Hovda · animal / human review","Concussion · acute / subacute mechanisms"]}]},"say":"Mitochondrial dysfunction is another possible explanation. After injury, glutamate release, ionic dysregulation and calcium influx into mitochondria can impair oxidative phosphorylation. This follows the acute neurometabolic mechanism described in the thesis. Whether this remains the explanation for a chronic CMRO₂ deficit is not established by our measurements.","paperSelection":["Giza2014"],"labelCohortPapers":true,"labelPaperIds":["Giza2014"],"paperColor":"#94bbc9","referenceReveal":true}],"synchronizedReveal":true,"overviewTour":true,"paperSequence":false,"populationTour":false,"speakingPrompts":true,"plainHeadings":true,"showMapLabels":true,"compressedSrc":"decks/data/ppcs-field-network-interactive.json.gz","populationFlow":"nested-human-pcs-v1","discussionMap":true,"discussionCopy":"thesis"}],"stepNotes":["How could the leak persist? These are proposed explanations, not mechanisms established by our measurements.","Gard found eight higher CSF markers in athletes with persistent symptoms, a median of 17 months after their last concussion: IL-2, IL-15, TNF-alpha, TNF-beta, VEGF, eotaxin, IP-10 and TARC. These were exploratory comparisons without multiple-comparison correction. Neumann and Chaban provide related TSPO and plasma evidence in different populations and time windows.","Claudin-5, occludin and ZO-1 normally seal endothelial junctions; lower levels after injury leave a looser seal. The thesis proposes junction remodelling as a possible molecular explanation. Claudin-1 can destabilise the normal complex in experimental work; this is not a literal one-for-one replacement of the seal.","Aquaporin-4 is a water channel on astrocytic end-feet. Loss of its normal polarisation may impair clearance. This is another possible contributor to persistence; the present studies do not measure it.","What could reduce the tracer-accessible blood volume throughout the brain? The thesis distinguishes loss of vessels from loss of perfusion through vessels that are still there.","Thinner or fewer capillaries reduce exchange surface and would lower tracer-accessible blood volume. These are different anatomical changes; rarefaction means fewer vessels. Neither was measured directly by DCE-MRI.","Capillary segments blocked by leukocytes or stationary blood cells would also lower tracer-accessible blood volume if those segments become unavailable to the passing tracer. The blood can still be physically present: this is a perfusion mechanism, not necessarily anatomical vessel loss.","Could a smaller functioning microvascular compartment also contribute to lower oxygen consumption? Then the two results need not require two independent lesions.","If lower blood volume represents less exchange-relevant capillary surface, oxygen extraction can fall at a given global flow. CMRO₂ could then fall without requiring intrinsic mitochondrial failure. That is a conditional exchange argument: tracer-accessible blood volume is not a direct measure of exchange surface, and the classical extraction relation is a simplified transport model.","Mitochondrial dysfunction is another possible explanation. After injury, glutamate release, ionic dysregulation and calcium influx into mitochondria can impair oxidative phosphorylation. This follows the acute neurometabolic mechanism described in the thesis. Whether this remains the explanation for a chronic CMRO₂ deficit is not established by our measurements."],"notes":"Current flow: three proposed contributors to persistent leak (CSF inflammatory signals, junction remodelling, AQP4 organisation), then thinner/fewer capillaries and stalling, then oxygen exchange and mitochondrial dysfunction. The two directional inflammation/leak rows are removed. Previous rows dim on Next; every source for the active point remains highlighted in the map, without automatic tooltips. Gard’s eight nominally higher markers were verified against Table 2 and the Results; IL-15 is correct despite the figure-caption IL-5 typo. No multiple-comparison correction was applied. The mechanism and causality caveat now frames the map before and after it.\n\nStudy context sits below each highlighted topic label. Named context identifies the specific anchor (Gard, Gowrikumar, Ivens/Weissberg, Robinson or Bracko), not a shared population/time window for all selected references. AQP4 combines uninjured knockout evidence with post-traumatic mouse measurements through 28 days and separate human supporting data; the human timing is not asserted as 1–28 days. CSF timing belongs to Gard; related PET/plasma sources remain selected without being described as the same cohort. Giza and van Dinther are reviews, so there is no invented single injury-to-scan interval.\n\nCurrent display: highlighted topic at left, short thesis-based explanation at right. CSF observation is first. No automatic paper tooltips. The microglial-cytokine sentence has been removed. AQP4 wording distinguishes normal polarisation, which supports exchange, from loss of polarisation, which may impair clearance.\n\nNo automatic paper tooltips: each point highlights its complete thesis citation group at the real graph coordinates. Detailed methods and limits remain in speaker/source notes.\n\nWording shortened directly from Chapter 4, especially “Cortical leak and tight-junction remodelling”. Full sentences retain the causal links: endothelial seal → blood-derived proteins → glial activation → further barrier damage; AQP4 is the separate persistence/clearance argument. Protein names are explained in the junction card rather than compressed into “claudin-1 induction”.\n\nThe first point describes the actual CSF/plasma observations rather than a list of abbreviations. Neumann remains selected as the thesis imaging reference, with the FDR limitation in source notes; the text does not assert a confirmed regional TSPO increase. Claudin-1 disruption of the complex replaces the thesis’s over-literal “takes their place” wording. All mechanistic interpretations remain conditional for the current cohort.\n\nFinal discussion follows thesis Chapter 4 §§4.1–4.4. Fixed full network; one topic title first, then cumulative short points below it. The active references emerge at their existing graph coordinates. Three phases: barrier persistence; rarefaction/stalling; impaired exchange or mitochondrial metabolism. Pericyte loss is reserved for backup. Read full main texts for detailed cards. Scope and timing stay explicit in cards and notes; none is represented as a direct mechanistic test in the thesis participants.\n\nFull-source review: [local source path omitted]\n\nNeumann: eight recovered collegiate athletes, 8–30 days, versus ten students; higher directions but no individual ROI survived FDR. Gard: 24 symptomatic athletes, median 17 months since last concussion, versus 12 controls; eight of 25 CSF markers higher in uncorrected exploratory tests. Chaban: 207/82 contributed at any visit, 157/54 at 12 months; systemic and not PPCS-selected. These limitations prevent presenting the first point as demonstrated harmful chronic inflammation in civilian PPCS.\n\nJunction remodelling is not a literal one-for-one replacement of the seal. Ivens is albumin/astrocyte/excitability, not direct evidence of CMRO₂ reduction. Iliff is contusional mouse TBI, not mild injury; AQP4 is a water channel. Clearance may matter for persistence, not the ten-minute Ki measurement. van Dinther and Bracko are reviews of neighbouring diseases. Giza is a translational concussion review, strongest for acute/subacute mechanisms.\n\n0. Chapter four first asks how barrier dysfunction might persist. These are possible mechanisms from other populations and experimental work; we did not measure inflammation in our participants.\n\n1. There are signals in three compartments. Neumann found higher TSPO trends after return to play, but no individual region survived correction. Gard found exploratory CSF cytokine differences in athletes with persistent symptoms. Chaban found circulating cytokine differences persisting to one year. These are complementary signals in different populations, not proof of harmful brain inflammation in our patients.\n\n2. A possible molecular route is junction remodelling. Gowrikumar links claudin-1 induction to destabilisation of claudin-5, occludin and ZO-1 through SRC-associated signalling. This is not simply replacing the seal with a more permeable claudin, and it was not measured in chronic PPCS.\n\n3. If serum proteins gain access to tissue, the leak itself can change glial function. Ivens shows albumin affecting astrocyte potassium buffering and excitability; related work supports inflammatory signalling. This supplies one arm of a possible feedback cycle. Our gadolinium measurement does not demonstrate albumin entry.\n\n4. The other direction is also biologically plausible: inflammatory mediators affect junctions, while oxidative stress and matrix metalloproteinases affect the vascular barrier and basement membrane. Together, these routes could sustain a cycle. This remains a synthesis of mechanisms, not a demonstrated cycle in our cohort.\n\n5. A third part is persistence: disrupted perivascular AQP4 organisation may impair removal of material from the interstitium. Iliff tested this after a mouse contusion. This is a possible long-term clearance mechanism, not an explanation of the short-window Ki estimate, and proteins do not pass through the AQP4 water channel.\n\n6. What could reduce the tracer-accessible blood volume throughout the brain? The thesis distinguishes loss of vessels from loss of perfusion through vessels that are still there.\n\n7. Rarefaction means fewer microvessels, and potentially less exchange surface. The van Dinther review sets out the measurement problem: lower vascular volume is compatible with rarefaction but is not a direct vessel count. We did not measure capillary density.\n\n8. Alternatively, vessels may remain present but fail to contribute to perfusion. Bracko reviews an example in Alzheimer’s mouse models: neutrophil plugging and capillary stalling. It establishes a possible mechanism, not evidence that our patients had these stalls. Vascular tone is another possibility; our data do not distinguish them.\n\n9. Could a smaller functioning microvascular compartment also contribute to lower oxygen consumption? Then the two results need not require two independent lesions.\n\n10. If lower blood volume represents less exchange-relevant capillary surface, oxygen extraction can fall at a given global flow. CMRO₂ could then fall without requiring intrinsic mitochondrial failure. That is a conditional exchange argument: tracer-accessible blood volume is not a direct measure of exchange surface, and the classical extraction relation is a simplified transport model.\n\n11. Mitochondrial impairment is the other short explanation worth keeping here. Impaired oxidative metabolism could reduce oxygen use despite preserved global blood flow. It could coexist with impaired microvascular exchange; we have not identified which is responsible in chronic PPCS.\n\n12. These mechanisms can coexist. We did not measure inflammation, capillary density, stalling or mitochondrial respiration. Our findings identify altered vascular and metabolic physiology, but cannot locate the cause of reduced oxygen use within this chain.","subchapter":"How could the leak persist?","style":"keynote","transition":"fade","transitionMs":900,"omit":false},{"id":"fatigue-severity-scale","subchapter":"Cohort","style":"keynote","width":"bleed","align":"left","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":480,"layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"stretch"},"blocks":[{"type":"scene","scene":"fssStory","at":"figure","args":{"src":"decks/data/repcon-demographics.json","revealSteps":1,"aspect":1.7777778,"maxHeight":1,"dpr":1,"shadow":true,"showQuestionnaire":true}}],"seconds":25,"notes":"Show the full questionnaire first; reveal the existing cohort score distribution on the next click.\n\nFatigue Severity Scale distribution for the same 60 baseline MRI attendees used in the cohort and RPQ slides. All 60 have an FSS score, so no values were imputed. FSS here is the recorded sum of nine statements rated from 1 (strongly disagree) to 7 (strongly agree), total range 9–63, measuring the impact of fatigue on daily functioning. This is not an average item score on a 1–7 scale. Every person glyph is one participant; three-point bins only space the marks. Exact scores determine the summaries: mean 47.7667 (shown 47.8), median 49.5, IQR 40–56.25 (shown 40.0–56.3), range 9–63. The exported score vector is sorted and contains no identifiers or participant-level linkage.\n\n[Sources]\n- article_bbb_nocth/data/_source_repcon.xlsx, REPCON EX1 FSS field joined to the canonical 60 baseline MRI attendees; exact agreement with article_bbb_nocth/data/dataset.xlsx FSS.\n- article_hypoxia/main.tex, symptom burden methods: nine items rated 1–7, summed score used in regressions.\n- tools/build_repcon_demographics.py and decks/data/repcon-demographics.json.\n\nQuestionnaire wording and past-week instruction: NIH FITBIR Fatigue Severity Scale data dictionary, accessed 10 September 2026. https://fitbir.nih.gov/dictionary/publicData/dataStructureAction!view.action?dataStructureName=FSS&publicArea=true&style.key=fitbir-style\nEnglish display of the instrument; cohort scores retain the sum of nine items used in the supplied analysis. NIH NINDS lists FSS as freely available for non-profit research: https://cde-fe.ninds.nih.gov/ninds/noc-report/F0786/Fatigue%20Severity%20Scale%20(FSS)","backup":true,"order":115,"stepNotes":["The Fatigue Severity Scale asks about the impact of fatigue over the past week. Each of nine statements is rated from 1, strongly disagree, to 7, strongly agree.","The nine responses are summed, giving a total from 9 to 63. Among the 60 baseline participants, the mean was 47.8 and the median was 49.5."]},{"id":"what-remains-missing","subchapter":"What remains missing?","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","layout":["figure"],"rows":"1fr","cellAlign":{"figure":"center"},"order":52.1,"omit":false,"seconds":38,"blocks":[{"type":"clinical-synthesis","at":"figure","section":"Conclusion","title":"What remains missing?","sourceLabel":"","branches":[],"layout":"topics","copyStyle":"further-study","cumulative":true,"dimPrevious":true,"states":[{"visual":"text","topics":[{"label":"Mechanism and causality","text":"We cannot resolve the exact mechanism or establish causality. This requires further study."}],"evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"We cannot resolve the exact mechanism or establish causality. This requires further study.","scientificStatus":"limitation"},{"visual":"text","topics":[{"label":"Mechanism and causality","text":"We cannot resolve the exact mechanism or establish causality. This requires further study."},{"label":"Age, sex and haemoglobin","text":"Better demographic balance and individual [Hb] measurements."}],"evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"Better demographic balance and individual [Hb] measurements.","scientificStatus":"future"},{"visual":"text","topics":[{"label":"Mechanism and causality","text":"We cannot resolve the exact mechanism or establish causality. This requires further study."},{"label":"Age, sex and haemoglobin","text":"Better demographic balance and individual [Hb] measurements."},{"label":"Regional metabolism","text":"Regional CMRO₂ alongside Kᵢ and vᵦ, to test where the changes overlap."}],"evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"Regional CMRO₂ alongside Kᵢ and vᵦ, to test where the changes overlap.","scientificStatus":"future"},{"visual":"text","topics":[{"label":"Mechanism and causality","text":"We cannot resolve the exact mechanism or establish causality. This requires further study."},{"label":"Age, sex and haemoglobin","text":"Better demographic balance and individual [Hb] measurements."},{"label":"Regional metabolism","text":"Regional CMRO₂ alongside Kᵢ and vᵦ, to test where the changes overlap."},{"label":"Biomarkers","text":"Inflammatory and injury biomarkers."}],"evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"Inflammatory and injury biomarkers.","scientificStatus":"future"},{"visual":"text","topics":[{"label":"Mechanism and causality","text":"We cannot resolve the exact mechanism or establish causality. This requires further study."},{"label":"Age, sex and haemoglobin","text":"Better demographic balance and individual [Hb] measurements."},{"label":"Regional metabolism","text":"Regional CMRO₂ alongside Kᵢ and vᵦ, to test where the changes overlap."},{"label":"Biomarkers","text":"Inflammatory and injury biomarkers."},{"label":"Comorbidities and follow-up","text":"Account for clinical comorbidities, and follow imaging and symptoms over time."}],"evidence":[],"sourceLabel":"","say":"Account for clinical comorbidities, and follow imaging and symptoms over time.","scientificStatus":"future"}]}],"source":"Thesis §4.8 and Chapter 5","notes":"The present cross-sectional data cannot establish the cellular mechanism or causality. Priorities follow thesis §4.8 and Chapter 5: better age/sex matching, individual haemoglobin, regional metabolism, inflammatory and injury biomarkers, clinical comorbidities, and longitudinal measurements.","stepNotes":["We cannot resolve the exact mechanism or establish causality. This requires further study.","Better demographic balance and individual [Hb] measurements.","Regional CMRO₂ alongside Kᵢ and vᵦ, to test where the changes overlap.","Inflammatory and injury biomarkers.","Account for clinical comorbidities, and follow imaging and symptoms over time."]},{"id":"backup-paper-1-figure-1","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.01,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"The p-Brain workflow","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-figure-1.png","alt":"The p-Brain workflow","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Figure 1","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Final MRM production figure; identical to the referenced current pbrain_body.tex figure. Full original panels preserved. \n\nOriginal figure caption:\nEnd-to-end \\textit{p}-Brain workflow. Four inputs (a dynamic contrast-enhanced series, an inversion-recovery or variable-flip-angle series for $T_1/M_0$ mapping, and optional $T_1$-weighted and diffusion-weighted volumes for parcellation and tractography) feed a chain of stages: $T_1/M_0$ mapping, arterial input-function extraction, tissue parcellation, signal-to-concentration conversion, and tracer-kinetic modelling, each labelled with its default provider. The robot icon marks steps performed by a convolutional neural network (here the segmentation of the arteries and veins from which the input function is derived) whose trained weights are freely available for download. An optional diffusion track (tensor and constrained-spherical-deconvolution models with deterministic tractography and a structural connectome) reuses the same manifest and quality-control machinery. The pipeline writes voxelwise, regional, and per-tissue maps of every default parameter ($K_i$ and $v_b$ from Patlak analysis, and CBF, CBV, and MTT from Tikhonov deconvolution) as NIfTI maps with CSV and JSON summaries and fit diagnostics. The output panel illustrates one such map, the blood--brain barrier influx constant $K_i$, at three spatial scales (voxelwise, ${\\approx}90$ regional parcels, and six tissue classes) on a shared colour scale. Any stage is selected with a command-line flag or replaced by a single user-supplied file without changing the surrounding code.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 024f3030bb27085b3427ed57272bfeaeaad7940b9c74fefb78dcf23147b4cf24\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-figure-5","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.02,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Mathematical validation","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-figure-5.png","alt":"Mathematical validation","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Figure 5","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Final MRM production figure; identical to the referenced current pbrain_body.tex figure. Full original panels preserved. \n\nOriginal figure caption:\n\\textbf{Mathematical validation.} On identical inputs, \\textit{p}-Brain's deconvolution and ordinary-least-squares Patlak estimators reproduce an automated re-run of the reference software to machine precision. Rows show the five derived maps (CBF, CBV, MTT, $K_i$, $v_b$) for a representative mid-brain slice: \\textit{p}-Brain (left) and the reference software (centre) share a per-metric colour scale and are visually indistinguishable, while the right column is their voxelwise difference shown on its own native scale (note the $10^{-9}$ to $10^{-14}$ colourbar exponents). The residual is structureless rounding noise: the maximum absolute difference per panel is $\\leq 10^{-9}$ (annotated) and the maximum relative error is $4\\times10^{-16}$ to $1.4\\times10^{-11}$, the floating-point floor. Pooled over four participants ($n=6.9\\times10^{5}$ voxels) the estimators match the reference at $r=\\rho_c=1.000$ for all five maps. CTH is not computed by the reference and is omitted.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 39fdca121c2771e55aea89cf3e6cd436cbca0d0c7ca6233ebd6bb80016d3ffac\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-figure-6","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.03,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Autonomous validation: spatial agreement","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-figure-6.png","alt":"Autonomous validation: spatial agreement","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Figure 6","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Final MRM production figure; identical to the referenced current pbrain_body.tex figure. Full original panels preserved. Agreement is conditional on the same supplied reference input function; this is not full input-function validation.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\n\\textbf{Autonomous validation, spatial view.} Voxelwise maps for a representative participant and mid-brain slice, comparing \\textit{p}-Brain's fully automated pipeline (left) with the reference software (centre) for the five derived metrics (CBF, CBV, MTT, $K_i$, $v_b$); both pipelines were supplied the same reference arterial input function and the maps are restricted to the segmentation-derived brain mask. The two map columns share a per-metric colour scale and are closely matched. The right column is their voxelwise difference, shown on a robust per-metric scale (95th percentile of $|\\Delta|$): unlike the machine-precision residual of Figure~\\ref{fig:mathvalidation}, the end-to-end difference is small but non-zero (median annotated) and concentrated at vessel edges and partial-volume boundaries.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 929e50012706e964117bb89cc8d24e0e99f98f21342b5f5096632197a1f1e445\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-figure-7","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.04,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Autonomous validation: 12 controls","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-figure-7.png","alt":"Autonomous validation: 12 controls","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Figure 7","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Final MRM production figure; identical to the referenced current pbrain_body.tex figure. Full original panels preserved. Agreement is conditional on the same supplied reference input function; this is not full input-function validation.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\n\\textbf{Autonomous validation.} Voxelwise comparison of \\textit{p}-Brain's fully automated pipeline against the reference software across $12$ healthy controls, both supplied the same reference arterial input function. (a) Pooled hex-scatter for CBF, CBV, MTT, $K_i$ and $v_b$ on \\textit{p}-Brain's segmentation-derived brain mask, annotated with pooled Pearson $r$, Lin $\\rho_c$ and ICC$(A,1)$. (b) Bland--Altman plots per metric (bias and 95\\% limits of agreement, $\\pm 1.96\\sigma$). (c) Per-subject Pearson $r$ for each metric across all $12$ participants (single colour per metric), with the dotted line marking $r=0.90$. (d) Per-voxel cumulative distribution of the normalized absolute error (each voxel's $|p\\text{-}\\mathrm{Brain}-\\mathrm{Reference}|$ divided by that metric's robust scale, the 95th percentile of the reference) with the median and 90th percentile annotated; normalising by a fixed per-metric scale avoids the ill-defined per-voxel relative error where a metric approaches zero, as $K_i$ does throughout healthy parenchyma. MTT is compared within the physiological transit-time range.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 58dfd66c61355740d866d4ee6e883c2b04f75a24022b2eb1ef6df5da2774d183\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-2-figure-1","subchapter":"Paper 2","backup":true,"order":117.61,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study II: MRI acquisition","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-2-figure-1.png","alt":"Study II: MRI acquisition","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 2 · Figure 1","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current manuscript Figure 1 and final-thesis acquisition description. The native acquisition discussion is present, but this full manuscript figure is not.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nBaseline MRI protocol used for the DCE-MRI analysis, showing the order of acquisition. Structural sequences were acquired for parcellation and anatomical review, followed by pre-contrast T1 mapping and the dynamic contrast-enhanced series.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: e3967a5ce6e272888c2b98cdf3daddcf1742fe1b842b3a9176cd64ea9b114331\n\nSource: tireli2026","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-3-figure-1","subchapter":"Paper 3","backup":true,"order":123.01,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study III: normoxia and hypoxia acquisition","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-3-figure-1.png","alt":"Study III: normoxia and hypoxia acquisition","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 3 · Figure 1","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Full unchanged original protocol schematic. Figure labels inspired oxygen 12–14%; final-thesis prose gives 10–14%, both titrated to target SaO2 approximately 80%. Preserve source figure; describe titration rather than presenting the displayed range as exhaustive.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nSchematic of the MRI session. Participants were examined during normoxia, followed by repeated acquisitions during hypoxia. The two imaging blocks used the same sequence order and approximate timing.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 4fb32234059b03565677a056212ee972c1e0db650b2314daf7cfe000754faa78\n\nSource: tireli2026oxygen","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-3-figure-2","subchapter":"Paper 3","backup":true,"order":123.02,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study III: participant flow","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-3-figure-2.png","alt":"Study III: participant flow","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 3 · Figure 2","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Counts checked against final-thesis Methods figure caption: 45 paired sessions; PPCS 41 normoxia/39 reactivity for CBF and CMRO2, 38/35 for lactate. Main Figure 3 later mixed-effects labels are not imported.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nParticipant flow for the PPCS cohort, with cumulative exclusions at each step and per-metric quality-control drop-outs. Insufficient desaturation means arterial oxygen saturation did not fall below 90% during the affected sequence. Healthy controls: 47 for global CBF and CMRO2; lactate 17 at normoxia and 16 for reactivity.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 3b2cd84465cd7fdd1a095f338802036621a918865de01fed9989ce2f264ac628\n\nSource: tireli2026oxygen","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s1","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.05,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Parcel-level Ki maps","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s1.png","alt":"Parcel-level Ki maps","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S1","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result. Same example and related Ki information appear in the main output-map montage, but this exact standalone source figure is not used.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nParcel-level \\(K_i\\) for the example subject, produced directly by \\textit{p}-Brain's kinetic-stage diagnostics: each SynthSeg parcel is painted with its Patlak \\(K_i\\) and shown across slices. Units: \\si{\\milli\\litre\\per100\\gram\\per\\minute}.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: e752da0f8792a9af67ad790cbed9c44ceb1b61cb10a03ffa99b91014e01853c4\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s2","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.06,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Whole-brain tissue Ki summaries","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s2.png","alt":"Whole-brain tissue Ki summaries","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S2","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result. Same example and related Ki information appear in the main output-map montage, but this exact standalone source figure is not used.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nWhole-brain \\(K_i\\) medians per tissue for the example subject. Each SynthSeg parcel is painted with its median Patlak \\(K_i\\) and shown on two representative axial slices (anterior up); the colorbar maps colour to \\(K_i\\) in \\si{\\milli\\litre\\per100\\gram\\per\\minute}, and the GM\\textgreater WM ordering is preserved. These scan-level summaries complement the richer voxelwise and parcelwise outputs.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 0fa011e1348fc9f7cc7f4b87492978f65fdb6301264a37f1f6b33bd36f098e84\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s3","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.07,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Patlak fit diagnostics","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s3.png","alt":"Patlak fit diagnostics","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S3","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nPer-region Patlak fit diagnostics automatically emitted by \\textit{p}-Brain. For every run, \\textit{p}-Brain writes a fit-diagnostic panel for each tissue class, each parcel and representative voxels, together with spatial map montages, so that every reported \\(K_i\\)/\\(v_b\\) is auditable; two representative tissue panels are shown (cortical grey matter and cerebral white matter, SynthSeg/FreeSurfer nomenclature). Each panel shows the arterial input \\(C_a(t)\\) and tissue \\(C_t(t)\\) concentration--time curves with the Patlak tail window shaded (top), and the Patlak coordinates with the excluded and fitted (tail) points, the linear fit, and the recovered \\(K_i\\) and \\(v_b\\) (bottom). The physiological ordering \\(K_i\\)(GM)\\,\\textgreater\\,\\(K_i\\)(WM) is preserved.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 564337e95887a67264117c3639b21077228f6aa2276792795caed5c7d03287e1\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s4","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.08,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Baseline T1 and M0 maps","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s4.png","alt":"Baseline T1 and M0 maps","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S4","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Original supplementary Figure S4 has two stacked montages. Rendered the complete published page 8 to preserve their layout, labels and caption; not rebuilt from separate images.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nVoxelwise $T_1$ (ms, top) and $M_0$ magnetisation (bottom) maps from a representative ten-slice inversion-recovery dataset.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 1aca4f145bada38bc0b69c3af717f86819f8329f5fdb2607f18a7feb07b33297\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s6","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.09,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Tissue segmentation","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s6.png","alt":"Tissue segmentation","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S6","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result. Distinct source figure from the thesis segmentation-pipeline figure already in backup-study-ii-anatomy.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\n\\textit{p}-Brain's SynthSeg tissue segmentation for the example subject, grouped into tissue classes and overlaid on the structural \\(T_1\\) across representative axial slices (anterior up): cortical grey matter, subcortical grey matter, cerebral white matter, cerebellum, and brainstem (legend). These masks are propagated into DCE space and drive the regional and tissue-level summaries.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 062dbcf110218386c6fbd0a09ec7f3f09d6848c5063e8e5da8b231a7beb8bdcb\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s7","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.1,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Signal-to-concentration conversion","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s7.png","alt":"Signal-to-concentration conversion","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S7","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nSignal-to-concentration conversion for a representative cortical grey-matter time-course (example subject). Left: the raw DCE MR signal \\(S(t)\\) (arbitrary units). Right: the tissue gadolinium concentration \\(C(t)\\) (mM) obtained from the saturation-recovery inversion using the fitted per-voxel \\(T_1\\) and \\(M_0\\) (Supplementary Methods). The conversion subtracts the pre-bolus baseline so \\(C(t)\\) begins at zero; in the default pipeline this is the only preprocessing applied to tissue curves before Patlak analysis (the normalisation stage is the \\texttt{identity} pass-through and the input function is used at its native mM scale).\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 16959d75facf0b3251c9c4d5e80f4009d5223d2328b02e9c96d186b6d35deca4\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s8","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.11,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"The effective input concentration curve","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s8.png","alt":"The effective input concentration curve","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S8","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nThe venous (superior sagittal sinus) concentration--time curve used as the effective arterial input for kinetic modelling in the example subject, a double-bolus acquisition. Raw per-frame samples (orange markers) are shown against the processed input function carried downstream (blue line); the venous ROI and the voxel this curve is drawn from are shown in Figure~\\ref{fig:vascular-roi}.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: b4814e468c449f65159f8cd578ee275fd9294bb40cb3c40fe888811ece0da5fc\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s9","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.12,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Tikhonov fit diagnostics","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s9.png","alt":"Tikhonov fit diagnostics","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S9","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result. Explain CBF from the flow-scaled impulse response before normalisation, not the height of normalised R(t); source caption wording is over-compressed.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nTikhonov-regularised deconvolution for two representative tissues (cortical grey matter and cerebral white matter, example subject). Top: the measured tissue concentration--time curve \\(C_t(t)\\) (points) and the reconvolution of the recovered impulse response with the arterial input (line), with the input \\(C_a(t)\\) shown faint for context. Bottom: the recovered residue function \\(R(t)\\) (normalised to \\(R(0)=1\\)), from which cerebral blood flow (CBF, the early residue peak), mean transit time (MTT, the residue integral) and cerebral blood volume (CBV, the distribution volume) are derived; the annotated values are \\textit{p}-Brain's own deconvolution outputs. The physiological ordering CBF(GM)\\,\\textgreater\\,CBF(WM) is preserved. This is the deconvolution counterpart of the per-region Patlak fit diagnostics (Figure~\\ref{fig:res-slice}).\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: b546e8210fc0660af36351b20a388aee434aa71152b25cd7234908bf76243b76\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s10","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.13,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Contrast-to-noise quality control","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s10.png","alt":"Contrast-to-noise quality control","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S10","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nVoxelwise contrast-to-noise ratio (CNR) for the example subject, computed by \\textit{p}-Brain's quality-control stage as the peak post-bolus concentration above baseline divided by the pre-bolus baseline standard deviation. Top: the CNR map, brain-masked. Bottom: the low-CNR flag, voxels with CNR $<2$ (default threshold), which can be masked from the downstream regional and whole-brain summaries. Here the median CNR is 73 and only 0.9\\% of brain voxels fall below the threshold (QC status: pass), concentrated at tissue edges and partial-volume boundaries.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 0e2b63ad0e7bdc48921f3da929d8f36268211ab52d04f475c3471f6307404f41\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s11","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.14,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Provenance and quality-control records","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s11.png","alt":"Provenance and quality-control records","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S11","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nEnd-to-end auditability. Every stage writes a \\texttt{manifest.json} recording its provenance (upstream input manifests, configuration and plug-in selections, output paths, and timestamps) and a quality-control status (\\texttt{qc.status} of pass, warn or fail); a stage that raises halts the run rather than propagating silently. The kinetic-stage manifest excerpt (left) shows the provenance chain; the CNR quality-control gate (right) reports the median CNR, the fraction of brain voxels below the threshold, and the pass/warn flag for the example subject.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 3d5a746d46ec645dc3a0c6def181006f759da5af9045e6edc54c865b2280963a\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s12","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.15,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Validation across contrast-to-noise levels","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s12.png","alt":"Validation across contrast-to-noise levels","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S12","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nVoxelwise p-Brain versus reference agreement stratified by DCE contrast-to-noise ratio, for the five default maps across 12 healthy controls. Solid curves: pooled Pearson's $r$; dashed curves: Lin's $\\rho_c$; per-bin voxel counts are annotated. The dotted line marks the mathematical tier, where identical inputs reproduce the reference to $r=1.000$ at all CNR. Agreement is maintained across the CNR-adequate majority of brain voxels and falls off only in the sparsest low-CNR bin, consistent with the voxelwise CNR quality-control gate.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 5aca7fc634514f8cd8dc8f026ec79d3a55539168560187636157886d284c8ee5\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-1-supplement-figure-s13","subchapter":"Paper 1","backup":true,"order":110.16,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Diffusion tensor outputs","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-1-supplement-figure-s13.png","alt":"Diffusion tensor outputs","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 1 · Supplementary Figure S13","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplementary figure cited by pbrain_suppinfo_body.tex. Optional detailed backup, not a new main-talk result.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nBrain-masked diffusion-tensor scalar maps for the example subject, fitted by \\textit{p}-Brain's DTI model from the multi-shell diffusion-weighted acquisition and rendered by the pipeline's montage diagnostic (anterior up): fractional anisotropy (FA), mean diffusivity (MD), and directionally colour-coded FA. White-matter tracts (corpus callosum, internal capsule) show the expected high FA; cerebrospinal fluid shows high MD. In the colour-coded map, red, green and blue encode left--right, anterior--posterior and inferior--superior fibre orientation. When run, the optional tractography model additionally writes deterministic streamlines (\\texttt{.tck}) and a streamline-density map alongside these maps.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 8e0f478fc925bb7c4b4d702f180483e007060ad3ccc2f4d16fe2829e402d1d2c\n\nSource: tireli2026pbrain","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-2-supplement-figure-s1a","subchapter":"Paper 2","backup":true,"order":117.62,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study II: healthy-control parcel means","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-2-supplement-figure-s1a.png","alt":"Study II: healthy-control parcel means","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 2 · Supplementary Figure S1A","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplement figure. Group means differ from the PPCS-minus-control difference map already shown; retain full shared colour scales. Surface interpolation is display-only and excluded parcels do not enter inference.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nGroup-mean parcel-level $K_i$ and $v_b$ in healthy controls ($n = 15$), shown on the same single-patient anatomical underlay used in the main parcel-difference figure (subject [acquisition identifier omitted]) for visual comparability. Per-metric colour scales are shared with the PPCS group map (\\figurename~\\ref{fig:supp_parcel_pcs}) so the two figures can be compared directly. Axial slices show only analysed parcel values; for visual continuity in the surface panels only, excluded cortical labels were coloured by neighbouring Desikan--Killiany surface parcels and remaining non-parcellated vertices were filled by vertex-neighbour interpolation, as in the main parcel-difference figure.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: cd9447a5ff8b3654fd4e6c49a5635e647448c7cfef2cc1e3438865672c4d1258\n\nSource: tireli2026","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-2-supplement-figure-s1b","subchapter":"Paper 2","backup":true,"order":117.63,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study II: PPCS parcel means","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-2-supplement-figure-s1b.png","alt":"Study II: PPCS parcel means","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 2 · Supplementary Figure S1B","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplement figure. Group means differ from the PPCS-minus-control difference map already shown; retain full shared colour scales. Surface interpolation is display-only and excluded parcels do not enter inference.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nGroup-mean parcel-level $K_i$ and $v_b$ in PPCS patients ($n = 46$), shown on the same single-patient anatomical underlay used in the main parcel-difference figure (subject [acquisition identifier omitted]) for visual comparability. Per-metric colour scales are shared with the healthy-control group map (\\figurename~\\ref{fig:supp_parcel_hc}). Axial slices show only analysed parcel values; for visual continuity in the surface panels only, excluded cortical labels were coloured by neighbouring Desikan--Killiany surface parcels and remaining non-parcellated vertices were filled by vertex-neighbour interpolation, as in the main parcel-difference figure.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: e4b2f24e8451c20f783e8357dd4821a6d31589887366fa74cceef2afbc0643a1\n\nSource: tireli2026","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-2-supplement-figure-s2","subchapter":"Paper 2","backup":true,"order":117.64,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study II: influence diagnostics","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-2-supplement-figure-s2.png","alt":"Study II: influence diagnostics","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 2 · Supplementary Figure S2","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Current supplement influence diagnostic: the subcortical Ki difference remains nonsignificant after exclusion; the vb difference remains significant. Includes coded observation labels from the manuscript diagnostic, not patient names.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nSensitivity analysis for the two tissue-level OLS models flagged by the Bonferroni-corrected externally studentized residual screen: subcortical grey-matter $K_i$ (top) and subcortical grey-matter $v_b$ (bottom). Left: Cook's distance with the $4/n$ threshold; red markers and labels indicate the Bonferroni-significant observation. Right: individual values by group, with the flagged observation circled, and full vs.\\ outlier-excluded HC3 coefficients shown in the panel title. The panel titles report raw HC3 $p$-values; the corresponding Benjamini--Hochberg FDR-corrected $q$-values (within each metric across the seven tissue compartments) are $q_\\text{FDR}$ from $0.625$ to $0.990$ for subcortical $K_i$ (non-significant before and after exclusion) and from $9.10 \\times 10^{-4}$ to $3.8 \\times 10^{-6}$ for subcortical $v_b$ (FDR-significant throughout), consistent with the main-text residual diagnostics.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 434e87ffc9677ab77620b92afb281ff52fe83ae8843965a6ddaf8cd1fddde849\n\nSource: tireli2026","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-3-supplement-figure-s1","subchapter":"Paper 3","backup":true,"order":123.03,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study III: symptoms and oxygen metabolism","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-3-supplement-figure-s1.png","alt":"Study III: symptoms and oxygen metabolism","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 3 · Supplementary Figure S1","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Matches thesis-final subject-level OLS reactivity estimand, including FSS reactivity P=.053; not later mixed-effects reanalysis. Full eight-panel figure retained.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nGlobal CMRO2 versus RPQ and FSS within the PPCS cohort, at normoxia and reactivity. Lines show age- and sex-adjusted OLS fits with 95% confidence intervals and HC3 standard errors. No association reached P < 0.05.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: ee58a5073206685ce3e8dcf067f00ab4307fda22d46ec19cf41ee4f7ca1d7783\n\nSource: tireli2026oxygen","seconds":15},{"id":"backup-paper-3-supplement-figure-s2","subchapter":"Paper 3","backup":true,"order":123.04,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Study III: influence diagnostics","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/paper-backup-figures/paper-3-supplement-figure-s2.png","alt":"Study III: influence diagnostics","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"Paper 3 · Supplementary Figure S2","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"Matches final thesis: beta -16.7, P=.004, N=88; after excluding one outlier beta -16.0, P=.005, N=87. Includes published coded outlier L4, not a patient name.\n\nOriginal figure caption:\nInfluence diagnostics for the resting CMRO2 model (OLS with HC3 standard errors). Cook's distance and individual values, with the full and outlier-excluded coefficients.\n\nOriginal source: [local source path omitted]\nSource SHA-256: 30e9323a4eb7a0a01f46ea640a441bddef63a611ae1ac198a237556455b3bcb1\n\nSource: tireli2026oxygen","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-dataset","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":150,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Human cortical vascular geometry","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/dataset_combined.png","alt":"Human cortical vascular geometry","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 1","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 1, PDF page 4. Human H01 reconstruction supplies the geometry, cell types and layers. Oxygen demand is modelled from neuronal density; this image contains no measured oxygen physiology.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): The H01 human cortical reconstruction. (a) Top-down (x to y) projection of the reconstructed vessel centerlines over the analysed extent of 3351 × 2026 × 174 µm (69,274 capillaries). The capillary bed is coloured by diameter (viridis, 2 to 8 µm) and the thicker penetrating feeder vessels are drawn on a separate warm scale (6 to 70 µm) so they stand out against the mesh. This is the same network the whole-slab solve uses. (b) A 700 µm sub-region (±350 µm, marked in grey in panel a) so individual vessels remain visible. The slab is only about 174 µm deep, so most vessels run laterally and only a few span the full depth. (c) The 49,379 somata coloured by cell type, neurons in warm tones and glia in cool tones, with the vasculature (grey) threading through and depth running down the page. (d) The same cells coloured by cortical layer, the depth axis recovered from the layer labels and rotated to vertical so the laminae read as bands from Layer 1 at the pia to the white matter. (e) The oxygen-demand field M_0(x) built from the neuron density, concentrating in the neuron-rich middle layers. Geometry, cell type and layer are from the imaged human electron-microscopy release of Shapson-Coe and Lichtman (citation keys: ShapsonCoe2024), and no oxygen physics enters this figure.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 20b754549fa0182c076abe779aaf7a16ec359666c657f92f238798ec47913cc2\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im1.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-solver","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":151,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checking the numerical solver","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/fig3_validation.png","alt":"Checking the numerical solver","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 2","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 2, PDF page 9. Analytic Krogh-Erlang and axis-swap numerical checks test the discretisation. They do not establish physical isotropy or validate human clinical oxygen physiology. The stated in-vivo oxygen anchor is awake-mouse evidence.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): Solver check against the analytic Krogh-Erlang profile and an axis swap. Left: the simulated radial oxygen profile around a single capillary tracks the analytic Krogh-Erlang solution \\eqrefeq:krogh for the source along x, y, z, and the body diagonal (the axis-aligned markers coincide). Right: the maximum error against the analytic profile is identical for x, y, and z (4.70 percent, axis spread 0.000 pp) and smaller along the diagonal, so the operator is axis-blind. This certifies that no spatial axis is privileged in the discretisation \\eqrefeq:sevenpoint. It is a property of the solver, not proof that tissue oxygen transport is physically isotropic. The tissue-oxygen band anchor is the awake-mouse two-photon measurement (citation keys: Lyons2016), so that in-vivo anchor is rodent.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 03d02e737752a13279348338d9e7c33ba1f0f139e6a696f9bf52d0eec457e8be\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im2.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-dynamics","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":152,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Blood and tissue oxygen over time","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/flow_o2_filmstrip.png","alt":"Blood and tissue oxygen over time","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 3","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 3, PDF page 12. Illustrative transient oxygen visualisation, separate from the quantitative steady-state solver. Reconstruction truncations and interior gaps define ports; these are not identified arterioles and venules.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): Blood flow and the tissue oxygen it drives, side by side, at five timepoints. Left column: the Fry-Secomb bolus advected through the H01 capillary bed (inferno), from the empty bed at t=0 through wash-in and peak fill to drainage and near-complete clearance. Right column: an illustrative transient tissue-oxygen field driven by that bolus, delivered where blood is present, diffusing laterally into the tissue between the vessels, and consumed as the blood drains (magma). The blood stays confined to the vessels and clears by about 2.8 s, while the oxygen lags, spreads off the vessels to fill the tissue between them, and lingers after the blood has gone before it decays. Only the sparsest watershed, farthest from any vessel, stays dark. This visualises the wash-in, diffusion and wash-out dynamics. The quantitative tissue-oxygen result is the steady-state solve of Sections (source label: sec:rescue) and (source label: sec:computed_results). The inlet and outlet ports are reconstruction truncation points and interior gaps, not identified arterioles and venules (Section (source label: sec:computed_assump)), and the emergent transit-time dispersion sits at the resting value (Section (source label: sec:computed_cth)). Animations of the flow alone and of the flow with the oxygen dynamics, over the whole slab and a zoomed sub-volume and slowed down, are provided in the supplementary files.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: ec05d6b382d151501878ed1b4cc8e3660347d6e3b060fb060c9e16be81363467\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im3.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-budget","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":153,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Modelled physiology and reference ranges","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/h01_budget.png","alt":"Modelled physiology and reference ranges","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 4","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 4, PDF page 13. Draft whole-slab estimates compared with reference bands. The budget contains derived quantities and model assumptions; agreement with broad bands is not an independent human physiological validation.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): The emergent whole-slab budget against the human reference ranges. Each panel shows one whole-slab value (marker) over its shaded human range. The mean served tissue tension (24.4 mmHg, human range 20 to 40 (citation keys: Dings1998,Lyons2016)), the cerebral blood flow implied by the prescribed transit-time distribution (≈45 mL/100g/min, a dependent quantity, human range 40 to 60 (citation keys: Ito2004,Mouridsen2014)), and the fragmentation-robust length density (620 mm/mm^3, human range 400 to 900 (citation keys: Cassot2006,Linninger2013,Smith2019)) each fall inside their human ranges. No parameter is tuned to any of these targets: the wall resistance is the measured Hellums table, the metabolism is fixed from the literature, and the geometry is the imaged human bed. The capillary blood tension and the oxygen extraction fraction are not shown here, the first because it is coupled to the prescribed inlet and the second because it is an imposed conservation anchor rather than an emergent output (Section (source label: sec:oef_result)) (citation keys: ShapsonCoe2024,Secomb2016,Ito2004).\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 07501d7c18cb60cd804407596e315959631c042f636cd925162ac903547fc11d\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im4.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-extraction","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":154,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Oxygen extraction and transit-time spread","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/h01_cth_sweep.png","alt":"Oxygen extraction and transit-time spread","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 5","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 5, PDF page 14. The plotted extraction curve is an imposed conservation quantity: a resting Fick anchor of 0.44 and Jespersen-Ostergaard transit-time dependence, separate from the spatial capillary-bed extraction. Do not present the curve as a measured clinical extraction response.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): What the transit-time spread changes: extraction, not tissue oxygen. The horizontal axis is the capillary transit-time spread CTH in seconds, with the relative dispersion RD=CTH/MTT marked above, swept at fixed mean transit time and fixed cerebral blood flow. Left: the oxygen extraction fraction falls steeply from 0.44 at rest to 0.30 at malignant heterogeneity, the convective conservation penalty. Right: the mean served tissue tension under full diffusion barely moves, from 24.4 to 22.4 mmHg, staying well inside the human range (shaded). The same widening of transit times is a strong lever on blood-side extraction and only a weak one on tissue oxygen.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: cc10623aca4a6eb6770ffcd0b5e964672d40d7644055afa476e04eff31f03450\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im5.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-diffusion","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":155,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Lateral diffusion and tissue oxygen","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/grid_2x2_v2.png","alt":"Lateral diffusion and tissue oxygen","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 6","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 6, PDF page 16. Draft comparison of no lateral diffusion and full diffusion on the same reconstructed slab, at prescribed resting and malignant transit-time dispersions. Percentages describe modelled served tissue under these assumptions.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): Diffusion, not transit-time spread, sets tissue oxygen. Each panel is the whole H01 capillary bed as the depth-rotated cortical wedge (as in Figure (source label: fig:dataset)c to e), coloured by served-tissue oxygen tension with the hypoxic volume fraction labelled and the reconstruction voids left white. Rows are the two limits of lateral oxygen diffusion, columns the resting and malignant transit-time spread (RD=0.9 and 1.8 at fixed cerebral blood flow). Reading down a column, switching on diffusion transforms the tissue from mottled and starved to warm and oxygenated, collapsing the hypoxic fraction from about 36 to about 4 percent, because the oxygen diffusion length exceeds the intercapillary distance on this dense bed. Reading across a row, widening the transit-time spread all the way to malignant barely changes it, 36 to 40 percent without diffusion and 4 to 5 percent with it. The erasure and its weak dependence on transit-time spread are robust to the operating point, the vessel reconstruction, the slab boundary, and heterogeneous or spatially correlated metabolic and flow patterns (Section (source label: sec:robustness)). The extraction-fraction decline and the stall lever are shown in Figures (source label: fig:cth) and (source label: fig:levers). Operating point as in \\S(source label: sec:validation) (citation keys: ShapsonCoe2024,Jespersen2012,Ito2004,Secomb2016).\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 7262f5ec4ab4134e4a015b397673959fcf744508e489619874774a48c21a3e67\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im6.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-boxes","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":156,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Transit-time spread in a cortical subvolume","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/tissue_boxes.png","alt":"Transit-time spread in a cortical subvolume","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 7","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 7, PDF page 17. The top panels show the prescribed transit-time distributions. The native image calls them resting and malignant bolus; the manuscript caption identifies them as transit-time distributions. The image is preserved unchanged. The lower panels show one representative modelled subvolume.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): The imposed transit-time change and the tissue pockets it does and does not create. Top: the resting (RD=0.9) and malignant (RD=1.8) transit-time distributions on a logarithmic transit-time axis, where a lognormal is a symmetric bell, filled with tracer dots. The mean transit time (MTT, dashed line) is the same for both, and what differs is the capillary transit-time heterogeneity (CTH, the spread bar below each), which doubles from 1.35 s at rest to 2.70 s at the malignant endpoint. Bottom: a two-by-two of the same sub-volume of the H01 bed, blue capillary centrelines with purple dots marking tissue below the 8 mmHg hypoxic threshold. Rows are the no-lateral-diffusion Krogh limit and full diffusion, and columns are resting and malignant CTH. Without lateral diffusion the geometric pockets fill the spaces between the vessels at both spreads (26 and 22 percent hypoxic in this representative patch). With diffusion they are gone (about 0 percent here), and widening the distribution from resting to malignant does not bring them back. This is the discrete-pocket, three-dimensional companion to the tissue-oxygen maps of Figure (source label: fig:headline).\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 1ec40f05cce610ef2aeb03782803089efb443f0a975706dcc750d3e2407ec315\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im7.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-cth-sweep","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":157,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Tissue oxygen across a transit-time sweep","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/cth_filmstrip.png","alt":"Tissue oxygen across a transit-time sweep","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 8","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 8, PDF page 18. The sweep extends to 12 seconds; values above approximately 3 seconds are stress tests beyond the stated physiological span, not demonstrated clinical states. Native simulation panels and labels are preserved.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): Tissue oxygen across the full transit-time sweep, with lateral diffusion on. Each panel is the depth-rotated H01 wedge coloured by served-tissue tension at a fixed transit-time spread, from uniform flow (CTH=0 s) to CTH=12 s, with the served hypoxic fraction above and the transit-time distribution below. The physiological anchors are resting CTH=1.35 s and malignant 2.7 s, so the panels beyond 3 s are a stress test past the physiological range. The tissue stays oxygenated across the physiological span and darkens only as the spread turns extreme, the hypoxic fraction climbing from 4 percent to 22 percent only once the spread is several times malignant.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 640a36074ff9f0083c996ed9532f53127c6bc1ab9c2b7601c4d05c95c7fb2740\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im8.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-stalls","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":158,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Transit-time spread and capillary stalls","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/levers.png","alt":"Transit-time spread and capillary stalls","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 9","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 9, PDF page 19. Draft full-diffusion model compares prescribed transit-time widening with capillary stalls. Bar values are conditional simulation outputs, not patient measurements or a direct test of PPCS.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): What raises tissue hypoxia: stalls, not transit-time spread. Each bar is the served hypoxic fraction under full diffusion. Widening the transit-time spread all the way to malignant heterogeneity (RD=1.8, CTH=2.7 s) barely clears the resting baseline (dashed line), from 4.2 to 5.2 percent. Capillary stalls tower over it, reaching 7.6 percent at a 10 percent stalled fraction and 14.3 percent at 20 percent, each stalled segment dropping to essentially zero flow so its blood fully de-saturates and it delivers no oxygen. A stall removes a whole supply line that neighbour diffusion cannot back-fill, whereas transit-time spread only re-times delivery that diffusion re-homogenises.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 69efaeedc2fe5b5956bcf5d7fd7cff411c1403d224dd1972626fa720c8de7da0\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im9.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-stall-sweep","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":159,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Tissue oxygen as the stalled fraction rises","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/stall_filmstrip.png","alt":"Tissue oxygen as the stalled fraction rises","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 10","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 10, PDF page 19. Fixed fractions of capillaries are stalled in the model, from zero to 20 percent. The displayed hypoxic burden depends on the supply, diffusion, geometry and stall assumptions; it is not a measured human prevalence.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): Tissue oxygen as the stalled capillary fraction rises, with lateral diffusion on. Each panel is the depth-rotated H01 wedge coloured by served-tissue tension at a fixed stalled fraction, from 0 to 20 percent of capillaries stalled, with the served hypoxic fraction above and the stalled fraction below. Unlike transit-time spread, which diffusion re-homogenises (Figure (source label: fig:cthstrip)), a small stalled fraction opens real focal hypoxia, because each stall removes a whole supply line that neighbour diffusion cannot back-fill.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 02ea748201459d87deeae261cbde99fc44588479b6d9b0ea65b357200f705e29\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im10.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-regional","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":160,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"A local stalled cluster","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/regional_zoom.png","alt":"A local stalled cluster","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 11","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 11, PDF page 20. One imposed cluster of 729 capillaries in a 140-micrometre sphere is compared with transit-time widening in the same cortical region. The local percentage and recovery distance are outputs of this particular draft scenario.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): One cortical region under three conditions. Left: the whole slab at rest with the region marked. Centre and right: the same region under malignant CTH and under a stalled cluster of 729 capillaries in a 140 µm sphere, with the fraction of the ringed region below the hypoxic threshold labelled. Bottom: the radial tissue-oxygen profile outward from the cluster centre. Malignant CTH leaves the region oxygenated (0 percent hypoxic in the ring), while the stalled cluster punches a deep local pocket (78 percent hypoxic in the ring) that neighbour diffusion rings but cannot fill, recovering to the resting baseline only about 100 µm beyond the cluster. This is the spatial signature that separates a stall from a transit-time spread.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: 81757d39e26f56a02586cec716f596edb58a547a65b0bb0bb422ef5f1cab735b\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im11.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15},{"id":"cth-extra-root-computed","subchapter":"Extra","backup":true,"order":161,"style":"editorial","width":"bleed","align":"center","plate":"none","transition":"fade","transitionMs":450,"layout":[". . .",". head .",". art .",". foot .",". . ."],"cols":"4vw 1fr 4vw","rows":"3vh auto minmax(0,1fr) auto 5vh","cellAlign":{"art":"center"},"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Computed and imposed transit-time distributions","at":"head"},{"type":"image","src":"decks/assets/cth-extra-july9/computed_vs_imposed.png","alt":"Computed and imposed transit-time distributions","fit":"contain","framed":false,"at":"art"},{"type":"eyebrow","text":"CTH · Figure 12","at":"foot"}],"source":"","notes":"CTH draft modelling. User-confirmed source: the 9 July 2026 root article_cth/main.pdf, Figure 12, PDF page 22. The computed flow is a wall-shear estimate on reconstructed geometry with calibrated speed and truncated or gap-related ports; it is not measured human flow. It is compared with an imposed resting lognormal distribution at a 5-micrometre grid.\n\nSource caption (current LaTeX, preserved as provenance; the confirmed PDF and its embedded image are authoritative): The real computed flow reproduces the imposed erasure at rest. Depth-rotated cortical wedges coloured by served-tissue pO_2. Columns are the two routes to the transit-time distribution, the flow computed directly on the H01 geometry by the wall-shear estimate (RD≈0.98, Section (source label: sec:computed)) and the prescribed lognormal at the measured resting value (RD=0.9). Rows are the two limits of lateral diffusion, the no-lateral-diffusion Krogh limit on top and full diffusion below. Both routes give the same tissue oxygen, about 36 percent of served tissue hypoxic without diffusion, collapsing to a few percent with it (3.4 percent computed against 4.2 percent imposed). The flow that emerges from the geometry sits at the resting operating point and produces the same erasure as the prescribed distribution. The geometry-induced malignant case is treated in the text of Section (source label: sec:computed_results). Grid 5 µm.\n\nFigure source: [local source path omitted]\nCaption source: [local source path omitted]\nSource image SHA-256: b06becdde1dc7a04a63fd34fd2262a821af27735b7836f6ae2da06d367a4cd4e\nConfirmed PDF SHA-256: 4ec878ff2ed03b3ade89aace08e3b250bb531baf8a0643fdcd39cfdb0feb955e\nVerification: native image is pixel-for-pixel identical to PDF embedded image Im12.png; staged PNG copied byte-for-byte. No cropping or redrawing. Original native figure visually inspected in full.","seconds":15}],"presentationEdits":{"schema":"spiral-presentation-edits/v1","deck":"decks/phd-defense.spiral","baseDeckHash":"ac3a9db7e097327d6099b304f5bf9a3db908c237836d63270ad9cc33213870b0","updatedAt":"2026-09-09T16:12:48.052Z","edits":{"study-i-bolus-to-voxel/20":{"[data-block=\"\\30 \"]":{"dx":-4.5368757259001195e-06,"dy":-0.000303044996121024,"hidden":true},"[data-block=\"\\31 \"]":{"dx":-0.00014518002322880372,"dy":-0.0001939487975174554},"[data-block=\"\\31 \"] > [data-editor-part=\"arterial-plot\"]":{"dx":-0.03179669352497096,"dy":0.012549093289371606},"[data-block=\"\\31 \"] > [data-editor-part=\"arterial-plot\"] > [data-edit